window.learnContent = {
  body: {
    source: 'Introduction to the body AAP April 2025 v2.ppsx',
    intro: 'The body is organised from atoms to a complete human organism. This topic introduces anatomy, physiology and pathophysiology, then builds through cells, tissues, organs and systems.',
    image: 'assets/diagrams/body-organisation.png',
    imageAlt: 'Diagram showing organisation from atom and molecule to cell, tissue, organ, organ system and organism',
    sections: [
      ['01', 'Anatomy, physiology and pathophysiology', 'Anatomy is the study of structure: how an organ or body part is constructed. Physiology asks how the body works, including how we breathe, digest food and maintain life. Pathophysiology examines abnormal changes and the effects illness or injury have on the body.'],
      ['02', 'Atoms and elements', 'Atoms are the smallest identifiable part of an element and contain neutrons, protons and electrons. Neutrons have a neutral charge, protons a positive charge and electrons a negative charge. Elements are arranged by the weight of their contents in the periodic table.'],
      ['03', 'Molecules and states of matter', 'Atoms combine to form compounds or molecules. Two hydrogen atoms and one oxygen atom form water (H2O). The space between atoms changes with state: gas particles are further apart, while solid particles are more closely packed.'],
      ['04', 'The body’s major elements', 'The introduction highlights hydrogen (H), carbon (C), nitrogen (N) and oxygen (O). These elements make up the majority of the body and combine in many different ways to build the molecules needed for life.'],
      ['05', 'Cells: the smallest living unit', 'Cells provide structure and are the smallest unit of life that can replicate. Cells take in nutrients from food, convert them into energy and specialise for different jobs. Muscle cells contract for movement, while nerve cells generate and conduct electrical impulses.'],
      ['06', 'Cell membrane, cytoplasm and cytosol', 'The cell membrane is the barrier around a cell. Small particles such as oxygen and water can pass through, while larger particles cannot. Cytoplasm includes the cell contents; cytosol is the watery matrix in which cell structures are suspended and is around 75–90% water.'],
      ['07', 'Nucleus and DNA', 'The nucleus controls cellular structure and activity. It contains DNA, the genetic and chemical instructions necessary for the development and functioning of living things. The course notes that a cell contains roughly two metres of DNA.'],
      ['08', 'Lysosomes and mitochondria', 'Lysosomes are the cell’s recyclers: they break down used components so materials can be reused. Mitochondria create ATP by converting food and oxygen into energy, through processes associated with the Krebs cycle.'],
      ['09', 'ATP and metabolism', 'ATP is the body’s usable energy store, often described as its batteries. Metabolism is the sum of chemical reactions in cells that breaks down food and inhaled oxygen into components small enough to enter cells and be used for energy, growth and repair.'],
      ['10', 'Movement across membranes', 'Diffusion is movement of a liquid or gas from a high concentration to a low concentration. It is especially important for respiratory gas exchange. Osmosis is the movement of water through a semi-permeable membrane when larger solutes cannot pass through.'],
      ['11', 'The four tissue types', 'The four tissue types are epithelial, connective, muscle and nervous tissue. Epithelial tissue forms a thin protective layer over the outside and inside of the body, including skin, organs, glands, blood vessels, intestines, the respiratory tract and abdominal cavity.'],
      ['12', 'Connective, muscle and nervous tissue', 'Connective tissue includes adipose, cartilage, bone, blood and lymphatic tissue. Muscle tissue creates movement. Nervous tissue senses touch, pain, temperature, vibration, hearing, balance, taste, smell and sight, then sends information to the brain for processing.'],
      ['13', 'Organs and systems', 'Various tissues join together to form organs, each with a specialised function. Organs join and work together as systems. The course introduces cardiovascular, nervous, gastrointestinal, integumentary, reproductive, respiratory, lymphatic and musculoskeletal systems.'],
      ['14', 'Respiratory system overview', 'The respiratory system performs gas exchange, regulates blood acidity, removes small amounts of heat and water, filters inspired air, produces vocal sounds and contains receptors for smell. Oxygen enters through the lungs, is carried by blood and is used by cells; carbon dioxide returns to the lungs for exhalation.'],
      ['15', 'Cardiovascular system overview', 'The cardiovascular system consists of the heart, blood and vessels. It transports oxygen and nutrients, removes waste, supports hormonal control, regulates temperature, contributes to host defence and supports reproduction. Deoxygenated blood returns to the right heart, travels to the lungs, and oxygenated blood returns to the left heart for delivery to tissues.'],
      ['16', 'Gastrointestinal system overview', 'The gastrointestinal system runs from mouth to anus. It uses nutrients from food for cell energy, energy storage, heat production, growth and repair. The stomach contains hydrochloric acid; the liver and pancreas produce digestive enzymes; bile is stored in the gallbladder; and the pancreas releases hormones that control blood glucose.'],
      ['17', 'Integumentary system overview', 'The integumentary system is the body’s outermost layer and includes skin, hair and nails. It provides a physical barrier, regulates temperature, allows sensation, supports vitamin D production, excretes some waste and provides insulation.'],
      ['18', 'Reproductive system overview', 'The female reproductive system supports formation of ova, reception of spermatozoa, foetal development, childbirth and lactation. Structures named in the course include fallopian tubes, ovaries, cervix, uterus and vagina. The male system produces, matures and stores spermatozoa and delivers them in semen into the female reproductive tract.'],
      ['19', 'Lymphatic system overview', 'The lymphatic system works alongside the cardiovascular system. It drains fluid from tissues, filters it and returns it to circulation, absorbs fluid in the small intestine and helps fight infection. Lymph vessels are found throughout the body.'],
      ['20', 'Musculoskeletal system overview', 'The musculoskeletal system includes muscles, bones, joints and connective tissues such as ligaments and tendons. Its functions include support, protection, movement, mineral storage, blood cell production and energy storage. The material notes 206 bones and more than 600 muscles.'],
      ['21', 'Types of muscle', 'Skeletal muscles work in pairs to flex and extend joints and are under voluntary control. Smooth muscle moves food through the digestive tract and is involuntary. Cardiac muscle is found only in the heart and is responsible for its continuous beating.'],
      ['22', 'How the topics connect', 'Atoms, molecules, cells, tissues, organs and systems are levels of one organisation. Energy production links food, breathing and circulation; nervous tissue coordinates responses; and cardiovascular, respiratory, lymphatic, urinary, reproductive and musculoskeletal functions support one another to maintain life.']
    ]
  },
  cardiovascular: {
    source: 'L3 CV System v1 September 2020.pdf',
    intro: 'The cardiovascular system consists of the heart, blood and blood vessels. It provides transport, maintains the internal environment and links the lungs with every tissue in the body.',
    image: 'assets/diagrams/heart-anatomy.png',
    imageAlt: 'Labelled heart anatomy diagram',
    sections: [
      ['01', 'What the system does', 'The cardiovascular system transports oxygen, nutrients, hormones and heat. It removes carbon dioxide and other waste products, contributes to temperature regulation, supports host defence and helps maintain the conditions cells need to function.'],
      ['02', 'The heart as a pump', 'The heart is a muscular pump with a right and left side. Each side has an atrium that receives blood and a ventricle that ejects blood. The muscular walls create pressure that moves blood through the pulmonary and systemic circuits.'],
      ['03', 'Right atrium and right ventricle', 'The right atrium receives deoxygenated blood from the superior and inferior venae cavae. It passes through the tricuspid valve into the right ventricle. The right ventricle ejects blood through the pulmonary valve into the pulmonary trunk.'],
      ['04', 'Left atrium and left ventricle', 'The left atrium receives oxygenated blood from the pulmonary veins. It passes through the mitral valve into the left ventricle. The left ventricle has a thick muscular wall and ejects blood through the aortic valve into the aorta.'],
      ['05', 'The valves', 'The tricuspid valve lies between right atrium and right ventricle. The pulmonary valve controls exit to the pulmonary trunk. The mitral valve lies between left atrium and left ventricle. The aortic valve controls exit to the aorta. Valves help prevent backflow.'],
      ['06', 'Pulmonary circulation', 'Pulmonary circulation carries deoxygenated blood from the right ventricle to the lungs. Gas exchange occurs in the lungs, where carbon dioxide leaves the blood and oxygen enters it. Oxygenated blood then returns to the left atrium through the pulmonary veins.'],
      ['07', 'Systemic circulation', 'Systemic circulation carries oxygenated blood from the left ventricle through the aorta to the body’s tissues. After oxygen and nutrient exchange, deoxygenated blood returns through veins to the right atrium.'],
      ['08', 'The great vessels', 'The superior vena cava returns blood from the upper body and the inferior vena cava returns blood from the lower body. The pulmonary trunk leaves the right ventricle. The pulmonary veins return oxygenated blood. The aorta leaves the left ventricle and distributes blood to the body.'],
      ['09', 'Arteries and arterioles', 'Arteries carry blood away from the heart under pressure. Their branching vessels become smaller arterioles, which help distribute blood to individual tissues and regulate how much blood reaches a region.'],
      ['10', 'Capillaries', 'Capillaries are small vessels with thin walls. They bring blood close to cells so oxygen, nutrients and hormones can move into tissues and carbon dioxide and waste can move back into the blood.'],
      ['11', 'Veins and venules', 'Venules collect blood from capillaries and join to form veins. Veins return blood to the heart at lower pressure and act as an important blood reservoir. The venae cavae are the major systemic veins returning blood to the right atrium.'],
      ['12', 'Coronary circulation', 'The heart muscle needs its own blood supply. The coronary arteries branch from the aorta and deliver oxygen and nutrients to the myocardium. Coronary veins return deoxygenated blood from the heart muscle.'],
      ['13', 'Blood as a transport medium', 'Blood carries oxygen from the lungs to tissues and carbon dioxide back to the lungs. It also transports nutrients from the gastrointestinal system, hormones from endocrine tissues and waste products to organs that remove them.'],
      ['14', 'Regulation and defence', 'The cardiovascular system helps distribute heat and maintain body temperature. Blood and its components contribute to host defence, while circulation allows hormones and chemical signals to reach their target tissues.'],
      ['15', 'The cardiac cycle', 'The heart alternates between filling and contraction. The atria receive blood and the ventricles fill; ventricular contraction then ejects blood into the pulmonary trunk and aorta. The valves open and close in response to pressure changes.'],
      ['16', 'Useful figures from the material', 'The course material notes that the heart beats around 70 times per minute, is about the size of a clenched fist and contains a circulation connected to around 60,000 miles of blood vessels. The entire blood volume passes through the heart approximately once each minute.'],
      ['17', 'Clinical connection', 'Chest pain, breathlessness, collapse, an abnormal pulse and signs of poor perfusion are important findings. Consider how a problem with the pump, vessels, blood volume or oxygen delivery could affect every tissue supplied by the circulation.']
    ]
  },
  respiratory: {
    source: 'AAP Resp system update JUL26.ppsx',
    intro: 'The respiratory system ventilates the lungs, extracts oxygen from the atmosphere, transfers it to the bloodstream, removes carbon dioxide and water vapour, and helps maintain the acid-base balance of the blood.',
    image: 'assets/diagrams/respiratory-system.png',
    imageAlt: 'Labelled respiratory system diagram',
    sections: [
      ['01', 'Functions of the respiratory system', 'The system ventilates the lungs, extracts oxygen from the atmosphere and transfers it to the bloodstream. It excretes water vapour and carbon dioxide, maintains the acid-base balance of blood, filters inspired air, supports voice production and contributes to smell.'],
      ['02', 'Inspired and expired air', 'Inspired atmospheric air is approximately 78% nitrogen, 21% oxygen and 0.04% carbon dioxide, with variable water vapour. Expired air remains around 78% nitrogen but contains approximately 16% oxygen, 4% carbon dioxide and saturated water vapour.'],
      ['03', 'The nose and pharynx', 'The nose begins warming, moistening and filtering inspired air. The pharynx lies behind the oral and nasal cavities, extends from the base of the skull to around C6 and is 12–14 cm long. It is divided into the nasopharynx, oropharynx and hypopharynx.'],
      ['04', 'The pharynx has two pathways', 'The pharynx is part of both the respiratory and digestive systems. It allows food to pass from the mouth to the oesophagus and conducts air from the nose and mouth towards the trachea.'],
      ['05', 'Epiglottis and airway protection', 'The epiglottis is a leaf-shaped flap of cartilage. It closes the larynx during swallowing and helps prevent aspiration of food or fluid into the lower respiratory tract.'],
      ['06', 'The larynx', 'The larynx, or voice box, lies anterior to the laryngopharynx around C4–C7. It produces sound and supports speech, protects the lower respiratory tract during swallowing, provides an air passage to the trachea, and continues warming, filtering and humidifying inspired air.'],
      ['07', 'The trachea', 'The trachea lies anterior to the oesophagus and extends from the larynx to around T5. It is a tube approximately 10–12 cm long, supported by 16–20 incomplete C-shaped rings of cartilage that help keep the airway open.'],
      ['08', 'Bronchi and bronchioles', 'At the carina the trachea divides into the left and right main bronchi. The right main bronchus is shorter, wider and more vertical. The bronchi subdivide into smaller bronchioles, which terminate at the alveoli.'],
      ['09', 'Alveoli', 'Alveoli are tiny air sacs with a large combined surface area. Their walls are one cell thick, creating a short distance for oxygen and carbon dioxide to diffuse between air and the surrounding capillary blood.'],
      ['10', 'The lungs', 'The lungs are two hollow, cone-shaped organs positioned on either side of the mediastinum, which contains the heart. Each lung has an apex above and a base below. The right lung has three lobes and the left lung has two.'],
      ['11', 'Pleura and pleural space', 'The lungs and inside of the thoracic cavity are lined by two layers of serous membrane called pleura. The parietal pleura attaches to the thoracic cage; the visceral pleura attaches to the lung. The potential pleural space contains surfactant and allows smooth expansion.'],
      ['12', 'Ventilation and pressure', 'Ventilation moves air between the atmosphere and the lungs. When thoracic volume increases, intrathoracic pressure drops below atmospheric pressure and air moves in. When thoracic volume decreases, pressure rises and air moves out.'],
      ['13', 'Inspiration', 'Inspiration is active. The external intercostal muscles contract and the ribs move up and out. The diaphragm contracts and flattens, moving down. About 75% of the pull of inspiration comes from the diaphragm.'],
      ['14', 'Expiration', 'Quiet expiration is passive. The external intercostals relax and the ribs move down and in. The diaphragm relaxes and rises back towards its dome shape, reducing thoracic volume and allowing air to leave.'],
      ['15', 'Pulmonary circulation', 'Pulmonary circulation carries deoxygenated blood from the right side of the heart to the lungs. Carbon dioxide is exchanged for oxygen, then oxygenated blood returns to the left side of the heart and is pumped to the body’s cells.'],
      ['16', 'Bronchial circulation', 'Bronchial circulation supplies oxygenated blood and nutrients to the lung tissue. Bronchial arteries branch from the thoracic aorta and supply the bronchi, bronchioles, connective tissue and pleura. They do not supply the alveoli themselves.'],
      ['17', 'External and internal respiration', 'External respiration occurs between the atmosphere and the lungs: oxygen is inhaled and carbon dioxide exhaled. Internal respiration occurs inside the body: red blood cells carry oxygen to cells, where it is used, and collect carbon dioxide.'],
      ['18', 'The diffusion gradient', 'Deoxygenated blood arriving from tissues is high in carbon dioxide and low in oxygen. Blood in alveolar capillaries is high in oxygen and low in carbon dioxide. Gases diffuse from high to low concentration across the single-cell wall.'],
      ['19', 'Oxygen delivery', 'Oxygen diffuses from alveoli into capillary blood, binds to red blood cells and is transported through the cardiovascular system. It is delivered to body cells for cellular respiration; carbon dioxide returns to the lungs for removal.'],
      ['20', 'Tidal volume and dead space', 'A normal breath is around 500 mL, known as tidal volume. Around 150 mL is anatomical dead space, meaning that not all inspired air reaches the alveoli for gas exchange.'],
      ['21', 'Nervous control of breathing', 'The medullary centre controls the rhythmicity of breathing. The pneumotaxic area in the pons coordinates inspiration and expiration and limits inspiration. The apneustic centre in the pons stimulates inspiration.'],
      ['22', 'Chemoreceptors', 'Chemoreceptors respond to changes in the partial pressures of oxygen and carbon dioxide in blood. Central chemoreceptors are in the medulla; peripheral chemoreceptors are in the carotid and aortic bodies.'],
      ['23', 'Carbon dioxide and blood acidity', 'More carbon dioxide makes blood more acidic; less carbon dioxide makes it more alkaline. Breathing faster or deeper removes more carbon dioxide and makes blood less acidic. Slower or shallower breathing retains carbon dioxide and makes blood more acidic.'],
      ['24', 'Respiratory assessment', 'Evaluate rate, rhythm and depth of breathing. Compare the findings with the patient’s appearance and work of breathing, and look for abnormal patterns that may indicate the respiratory system is failing.']
    ]
  },
  musculoskeletal: {
    source: 'L3 Musculoskeletal System v1 October 2020.pdf',
    intro: 'The musculoskeletal system is made up of bones, muscle tissue, joints and connective tissues. Together they support and protect the body, allow movement, store minerals, produce blood cells and store energy.',
    secondaryImages: [
      { src: 'assets/diagrams/bone.png', alt: 'Labelled long bone showing cancellous bone, marrow, periosteum, compact bone, nutrient artery, epiphyses and diaphysis' },
      { src: 'assets/diagrams/Axial-and-Appendicular-Skeleton.png', alt: 'Anterior and lateral views of the complete human skeleton' },
      { src: 'assets/diagrams/skull-front.png', alt: 'Anterior view of the bones of the face' },
      { src: 'assets/diagrams/skull-side.png', alt: 'Lateral view of the skull bones and sutures' },
      { src: 'assets/diagrams/spine.png', alt: 'Lateral view of the vertebral column' },
      { src: 'assets/diagrams/musculoskeletal-figure-08.jpg', alt: 'Lumbar vertebra viewed from above' },
      { src: 'assets/diagrams/musculoskeletal-figure-09.jpg', alt: 'Upper cervical vertebrae viewed from above' },
      { src: 'assets/diagrams/Lumbar-Vertebra.png', alt: 'Labelled lumbar vertebra diagram' },
      { src: 'assets/diagrams/Odontoid-process.png', alt: 'Labelled odontoid process and upper cervical vertebra diagram' },
      { src: 'assets/diagrams/Sacrum-and-Coccyx.png', alt: 'Labelled sacrum and coccyx diagram' },
      { src: 'assets/diagrams/arm.png', alt: 'Labelled shoulder girdle, arm and hand diagram' },
      { src: 'assets/diagrams/pelvis.png', alt: 'Labelled pelvis and upper femur diagram' },
      { src: 'assets/diagrams/pelvis-leg-foot.png', alt: 'Labelled pelvis, leg and foot diagram' },
      { src: 'assets/diagrams/bone-fibrous.png', alt: 'Labelled fibrous joint diagram' },
      { src: 'assets/diagrams/Cartilaginous.png', alt: 'Labelled cartilaginous joint diagram' },
      { src: 'assets/diagrams/Synovial-joint.png', alt: 'Labelled synovial joint diagram' },
      { src: 'assets/diagrams/musculoskeletal-figure-24.jpg', alt: 'Photograph of a human skeleton' }
    ],
    sections: [
      ['01', 'Learning objectives', 'The source material explains the structure and function of the musculoskeletal system and how joints, bones, muscles, ligaments and tendons work together.'],
      ['02', 'Overview', 'The system has three linked components: bones, muscle tissue, and joints with connective tissue. Bones provide the framework, muscle tissue generates force, and joints and connective tissues connect and stabilise the framework while allowing controlled movement.'],
      ['03', 'Functions of the skeletal system', 'The skeleton provides support and protection, allows movement, stores minerals, produces blood cells and stores energy. It supports soft tissues, protects the brain, spinal cord and thoracic organs, and provides attachment points for muscles.'],
      ['04', 'Bone types', 'Long bones have a shaft and two extremities and are longer than they are wide, for example the humerus. Short bones have no long shaft and are diverse in shape, such as the carpals. Flat bones include the sternum, ribs and most skull bones. Sesamoid bones develop within tendons, with the patella as the example. Irregular bones have varied shapes, including vertebrae and some skull bones.'],
      ['05', 'Anatomy of a long bone', 'The epiphyses are the expanded ends of a long bone and the diaphysis is its shaft. Compact bone forms a strong outer layer. Cancellous bone contains red bone marrow, while the medullary canal contains yellow bone marrow. The periosteum covers the outer surface, articular hyaline cartilage covers joint surfaces, and a nutrient artery enters through a foramen to supply the bone.'],
      ['06', 'Axial and appendicular skeleton', 'The axial skeleton includes the skull, vertebral column and thorax: the sternum and ribs. The appendicular skeleton includes the pelvic and shoulder girdles and the bones of the arms, hands, legs and feet.'],
      ['07', 'The skull and facial bones', 'The skull protects the brain and provides the framework of the face. The material identifies the frontal, parietal, temporal, occipital, sphenoid, ethmoid, lacrimal, zygomatic, nasal, vomer, inferior nasal concha, maxilla and mandible bones.'],
      ['08', 'The vertebral column', 'The column is divided into cervical, thoracic, lumbar, sacral and coccygeal regions. It supports the head and trunk, protects the spinal cord and provides attachment for ribs and muscles. The diagrams show seven cervical, twelve thoracic and five lumbar vertebrae, followed by the fused sacrum and coccyx.'],
      ['09', 'Typical vertebra and spinal features', 'A vertebra has a body, vertebral arch and vertebral foramen. The pedicles and laminae form the arch; transverse and spinous processes provide attachment and leverage; and superior articular processes form joints with adjacent vertebrae. Intervertebral discs sit between vertebral bodies, while intervertebral foramina allow spinal nerves to pass.'],
      ['10', 'The atlas and axis', 'The atlas is the first cervical vertebra and supports the skull. The axis is the second cervical vertebra and has an odontoid process, or dens, around which the atlas and head rotate. The transverse ligament helps hold the dens in position.'],
      ['11', 'Sacrum and coccyx', 'The sacrum is a fused triangular group of five sacral vertebrae. It articulates with the ilia at the sacroiliac joints and has foramina for nerves. The coccyx is formed from small coccygeal vertebrae below the sacrum and provides attachment for ligaments and muscles.'],
      ['12', 'Thorax', 'The thorax is formed by the sternum, ribs and thoracic vertebrae. It protects the heart and lungs, provides attachment for respiratory muscles and contributes to breathing mechanics.'],
      ['13', 'Shoulder girdle, arm and hand', 'The shoulder girdle consists of the clavicle and scapula. The arm contains the humerus; the forearm contains the radius and ulna; and the hand contains carpals, metacarpals and phalanges.'],
      ['14', 'Pelvis, leg and foot', 'The pelvis supports the trunk and transfers weight to the lower limbs. Important structures include the ilium, acetabulum, ischium, pubis, pubic arch, obturator foramen, sacroiliac joint and symphysis pubis. The lower limb contains the femur, patella, tibia and fibula; the foot contains tarsals, metatarsals and phalanges.'],
      ['15', 'Muscle tissue', 'Skeletal muscle moves the skeleton and is usually under voluntary control. Smooth muscle is not under conscious control and moves substances through organs such as the digestive tract. Cardiac muscle is found only in the wall of the heart and contracts continuously.'],
      ['16', 'Tendons and ligaments', 'A tendon is strong fibrous tissue that connects muscle to bone, transmitting muscle force to move a joint. A ligament is connective tissue that connects bone to bone, helping stabilise and guide a joint while limiting excessive movement.'],
      ['17', 'Joints', 'Joints are sites where bones meet. They allow movement of the skeleton and provide attachment between bones. The document groups joints as fibrous, cartilaginous and synovial according to the tissue between the bones and the movement permitted.'],
      ['18', 'Fibrous and cartilaginous joints', 'Fibrous joints are held together by fibrous connective tissue and permit little or no movement; skull sutures are an example. Cartilaginous joints are joined by cartilage and allow limited movement. The joints between adjacent vertebral bodies use fibrocartilage intervertebral discs supported by ligaments.'],
      ['19', 'Synovial joints', 'Synovial joints have a joint cavity, capsule, synovial membrane and articular cartilage covering the bone ends. Synovial fluid reduces friction. The capsule and surrounding ligaments hold the joint together while allowing smooth movement.'],
      ['20', 'Types of synovial joint', 'Ball-and-socket joints allow a wide range of movement, as at the shoulder and hip. Hinge joints move like a door hinge, as at the elbow and knee. Gliding joints use flat or slightly curved surfaces, as between carpal or tarsal bones. Pivot joints allow rotation, as in head rotation. Condyloid joints allow flexion, extension, abduction, adduction and circumduction. Saddle joints fit like a rider on a saddle and provide flexible movement, as at the base of the thumb.'],
      ['21', 'Summary', 'Bones form a supportive and protective framework, muscles generate movement, joints provide connection and range of motion, tendons transmit muscle force to bone, and ligaments stabilise bone-to-bone connections. The musculoskeletal system works as one integrated unit for posture, protection and movement.']
    ]
  },
  nervous: {
    source: 'L3 Nervous System v3 May 2021.ppsx',
    intro: 'The nervous system receives information, processes it and produces a response. It controls movement, regulates heart rate, creates memories and contributes to speech, behaviour and sensation.',
    image: 'assets/diagrams/nervous-system.png',
    imageAlt: 'Central and peripheral nervous system diagram',
    secondaryImages: [{ src: 'assets/diagrams/brain-lobes.jpeg', alt: 'Diagram showing the four lobes of the brain' }, { src: 'assets/diagrams/spinal-cord.jpeg', alt: 'Labelled spinal cord diagram' }],
    sections: [
      ['01', 'The three main functions', 'Sensory function detects internal stimuli such as changes in blood pressure and external stimuli such as odour, then sends information through afferent nerves. Information transfer and processing uses electrical impulses and integration. Motor function sends a response through efferent nerves to control the body, ventilation and circulation.'],
      ['02', 'Central nervous system', 'The central nervous system consists of the brain and spinal cord. It receives sensory information, analyses and processes it, then responds by sending impulses through nerves to produce an effect in the body.'],
      ['03', 'Peripheral nervous system', 'The peripheral nervous system is all the nerves outside the brain and spinal cord, including nerves and ganglia associated with them. It carries information into the CNS and instructions out to the body.'],
      ['04', 'Sensory and motor divisions', 'The sensory, or afferent, division carries impulses towards the CNS from receptors in the skin and from internal areas of the body. The motor, or efferent, division carries impulses from the CNS to the body and is divided into somatic and autonomic systems.'],
      ['05', 'Neurons', 'Neurons are the cells that generate and conduct electrochemical impulses. Most have one cell body, one axon and many dendrites. Neurons vary in size, shape and conduction speed and communicate with one another through chemicals released at synaptic bulb ends.'],
      ['06', 'Neuron structures', 'The cell body contains the nucleus and other organelles. Dendrites are the receiving portions that carry impulses towards the cell body. The axon is the long, thin portion that transmits electrical signals away from the cell body towards axon terminals.'],
      ['07', 'Myelin and Schwann cells', 'Some axons are covered by myelin sheaths, which allow electrical impulses to travel much faster. In the peripheral nervous system, Schwann cells form myelin. Nodes of Ranvier assist rapid transmission, while the myelin sheath protects nerve cells.'],
      ['08', 'Neuroglia', 'Neuroglia are supporting cells that do not generate or conduct impulses in the same way as neurons. They provide structural support, help create the blood-brain barrier and restrict movement of substances between blood and tissue fluid within the CNS. Some act as phagocytes that remove debris and damaged tissue.'],
      ['09', 'The brain', 'The brain is the part of the CNS contained within the skull. It occupies around 80% of the cranial vault; the remaining space is described in the material as cerebral blood and cerebrospinal fluid. The brain supports intellect, emotions, behaviour and memory as well as movement and regulation.'],
      ['10', 'The four major brain sections', 'The brain is divided into the brain stem, cerebellum, diencephalon and cerebrum. These areas have different roles but work together to process information and coordinate responses.'],
      ['11', 'The brain stem', 'The brain stem consists of the midbrain, pons Varolii and medulla oblongata. Together these regions relay information and help control consciousness, breathing and other essential functions.'],
      ['12', 'Medulla oblongata', 'The medulla relays sensory and motor input between other brain regions and the spinal cord. With the pons and midbrain it contributes to consciousness. It contains centres managing heart rate, blood pressure and breathing and the origins of several cranial nerves.'],
      ['13', 'Pons and midbrain', 'The pons relays impulses from one side of the cerebellum to the other and between the medulla and midbrain. It contains cranial nerve origins and centres that regulate breathing. The midbrain relays motor output from the cerebral cortex and sensory input from the spinal cord to the thalamus, helps coordinate movement and contains two cranial nerve origins.'],
      ['14', 'Cerebellum', 'The cerebellum has two hemispheres and coordinates complex, skilled movements. It regulates posture and balance and helps make movement accurate and controlled.'],
      ['15', 'Diencephalon', 'The diencephalon lies deep within the brain beneath the cerebrum and connects the cerebrum with the brain stem. It links the nervous and endocrine systems and includes the thalamus and hypothalamus.'],
      ['16', 'Thalamus and hypothalamus', 'The thalamus relays sensory input to the cerebral cortex and contributes to perception of touch, pressure, pain and temperature. The hypothalamus integrates autonomic nervous activity, regulates behaviour and circadian rhythms, controls body temperature and regulates eating and drinking.'],
      ['17', 'Cerebrum and cortex', 'The cerebrum allows reading, writing and speech and contains sensory, motor and association areas. It is the largest part of the brain, split into left and right hemispheres, with an outer layer of grey matter and inner white matter. The cerebral cortex is a thin outer rim containing billions of neurons.'],
      ['18', 'The four cerebral lobes', 'Each cerebral hemisphere is divided into frontal, parietal, temporal and occipital lobes. The supplied diagram shows the central sulcus, lateral sulcus and the parieto-occipital sulcus as landmarks separating regions.'],
      ['19', 'Meninges', 'In addition to the skull, the brain is protected by three meninges. The dura mater is the tough outer connective tissue layer. The arachnoid mater is the middle layer and surrounds the sub-arachnoid space. The pia mater is the delicate inner layer containing small vessels that supply oxygen and nutrients.'],
      ['20', 'Cerebrospinal fluid', 'Cerebrospinal fluid is a clear, colourless liquid. It protects the brain and spinal cord from physical and chemical injury, carries oxygen, glucose and other nutrients needed for brain function, and removes waste products.'],
      ['21', 'Spinal cord and vertebral column', 'The vertebral column has 33 vertebrae: 7 cervical, 12 thoracic, 5 lumbar, 5 sacral and 4 coccygeal. The vertebrae provide physical protection for the spinal cord, which they enclose.'],
      ['22', 'Spinal cord anatomy', 'The spinal cord is cylindrical, averages around 12 mm in diameter and extends from the medulla to the superior border of the second lumbar vertebra. It tapers into the conus medullaris; nerves below it form the cauda equina, or horse’s tail.'],
      ['23', 'Spinal nerves', 'There are 31 pairs of spinal nerves: 8 cervical, 12 thoracic, 5 lumbar, 5 sacral and 1 coccygeal. They contain afferent sensory fibres and efferent motor fibres linking the spinal cord with the body.'],
      ['24', 'Somatic nervous system and reflexes', 'The somatic nervous system is also called the voluntary nervous system. It allows conscious control of skeletal muscles through motor fibres from the CNS. It also includes reflexes, which are involuntary responses that primarily act as protective mechanisms.'],
      ['25', 'Autonomic nervous system', 'The autonomic nervous system regulates involuntary functions. It has sympathetic, parasympathetic and enteric divisions, with activity regulated by areas including the hypothalamus and brain stem.'],
      ['26', 'Enteric division', 'The enteric division is an extensive network of neurons in the walls of the gastrointestinal tract, pancreas and gallbladder. It monitors mechanical and chemical conditions, modifies gut movement and secretions, and controls the diameter of local blood vessels.'],
      ['27', 'Sympathetic division', 'The sympathetic division prepares the body for stress, sudden strenuous exercise and increased vigilance: the fight-or-flight response. It stimulates cardiac activity, opens the airways, dilates skeletal blood vessels and reduces gastric activity.'],
      ['28', 'Parasympathetic division', 'The parasympathetic division is the rest-and-digest division. It has a general peacemaker effect, stimulates digestion and absorption of food, and slows cardiac and respiratory activity.']
    ]
  },
  'lymphatic-urinary-reproductive': {
    source: 'L3 Lymphatic, Urinary  Reproductive Systems v1 September 2020.pdf',
    intro: 'These systems help maintain the body’s internal environment. The lymphatic system manages tissue fluid and immunity, the urinary system removes waste and regulates fluid balance, and the reproductive system supports the continuation of life.',
    sections: [
      ['01', 'Lymphatic drainage', 'The lymphatic system works alongside the cardiovascular system. It drains excess fluid from tissues, filters it and returns it to the circulation. Lymphatic vessels are found throughout the body and support immune defence.'],
      ['02', 'Urinary function', 'The urinary system filters the blood and removes metabolic waste in urine. It contributes to fluid, electrolyte and acid-base balance, helping keep the internal environment stable.'],
      ['03', 'Female reproductive function', 'The female reproductive system supports formation of ova, reception of spermatozoa, foetal development, childbirth and lactation. The ovaries, fallopian tubes, cervix, uterus and vagina each have specialised roles.'],
      ['04', 'Male reproductive function', 'The male reproductive system produces, matures and stores spermatozoa and delivers sperm in semen into the female reproductive tract. Reproductive function depends on coordinated organs, ducts and hormonal control.']
    ]
  },
  digestive: {
    source: 'L3 Digestive System  v1 September 2020.pdf',
    intro: 'The digestive system is responsible for taking in food, breaking it down physically and chemically, absorbing useful nutrients and water, and eliminating waste. This page follows the supplied course material from the overall system and its five processes through the alimentary canal and accessory organs.',
    image: 'assets/diagrams/digestive-system.png',
    imageAlt: 'Labelled digestive system diagram',
    imageOverlay: { text: 'Anus', x: 80, y: 57 },
    sections: [
      ['01', 'Learning objectives and why we need food', 'The source document aims to explain the structure and functions of the digestive system. Food provides nutrients for use as energy by cells, storage of energy, production of heat, and growth and repair. Essential nutrients are presented as three basic food groups; minerals and vitamins are also absorbed.', true],
      ['02', 'The components of the digestive system', 'The digestive system is the collective name for three related parts: the digestive processes, the alimentary canal (also called the gastrointestinal or GI tract), and the accessory organs of digestion. The alimentary canal is the continuous route through which food passes, while accessory organs produce substances that support digestion.', true],
      ['03', 'The five digestive processes', 'Food is physically and chemically complex, so it passes through five stages before useful substances can enter the bloodstream and lymph. Ingestion is taking food into the alimentary canal. Propulsion mixes and moves food along the canal. Digestion is mechanical and chemical breakdown. Absorption moves digested substances into blood and lymph vessels. Elimination is the excretion of waste.', true],
      ['04', 'Nerve supply to the GI tract', 'The GI tract is supplied by the autonomic nervous system and is therefore controlled involuntarily. Parasympathetic activity, often called rest and digest, increases peristalsis and gland secretion, including gastric juice and enzymes. Sympathetic fight-or-flight activity reduces peristalsis and secretion. The two influences interact: activity such as eating increases parasympathetic activity.', true],
      ['05', 'The alimentary tract', 'The alimentary canal is also termed the gastrointestinal or GI tract. It actively transports the flow of food through the whole digestive process, from ingestion through propulsion, digestion and absorption to elimination. The labelled overview includes the mouth, pharynx, oesophagus, stomach, small intestine, large intestine, rectum and anus.', true],
      ['06', 'The mouth and oral cavity', 'The mouth, or oral cavity, contains the tongue, teeth and salivary glands. The tongue forms and propels the food bolus into the oesophagus. Teeth chew food, breaking it down mechanically. Saliva contains enzymes that begin chemical digestion before the bolus leaves the mouth.', true],
      ['07', 'The pharynx and epiglottis', 'The pharynx, or throat, is an expanded muscular tube behind the nose and mouth and is shared with the respiratory tract. The source diagram divides it into three parts. The epiglottis is a leaf-shaped plate of yellow fibro-elastic cartilage at the entrance to the larynx. During swallowing it covers the trachea, helping stop food and fluids entering the lungs.', true],
      ['08', 'The oesophagus', 'The oesophagus, or gullet, is a collapsible muscular tube descending from the pharynx to the stomach. In an adult it is approximately 25 cm long and 2 cm in diameter. It is made of smooth muscle, elastic tissue and a mucous membrane. Sphincters act as valves, while peristalsis moves the bolus down towards the stomach.', true],
      ['09', 'Peristalsis', 'Peristalsis is the movement of food throughout the digestive system. It is produced by rhythmic muscular contractions in the walls of the tract. The contractions push food in one direction and support propulsion through the alimentary canal.', true],
      ['10', 'The stomach: structure and protection', 'The stomach is a muscular, bag-like organ whose walls are lined with a mucous membrane. At its connection with the oesophagus it contains the cardiac sphincter. This sphincter helps prevent regurgitation, or the movement of stomach contents back into the oesophagus.', true],
      ['11', 'The stomach: mechanical and chemical digestion', 'For mechanical digestion, the stomach contracts in different ways to churn food. For chemical digestion, it mixes food with gastric juices, hydrochloric acid and enzymes, liquefying the contents. Once food has been liquefied in the stomach it is called chyme. Chyme passes into the duodenum through the pyloric sphincter, which also helps prevent contents moving back into the stomach from the duodenum.', true],
      ['12', 'The duodenum', 'The duodenum is approximately 25 cm long in an adult and is the first part of the small intestine. Both the bile duct and the pancreatic duct empty into it. Chemical digestion of carbohydrates, proteins and fats occurs across the small intestine, beginning with the contents entering the duodenum.', true],
      ['13', 'The small intestine', 'The small intestine is approximately 5 metres long. It is a muscular tube lined with connective tissue and a mucous membrane. It comprises the duodenum, jejunum and ileum, is continuous with the duodenum above and the large intestine below, and is the site of most water absorption.', true],
      ['14', 'Small-intestine surface area and absorption', 'The outer covering of the small intestine is formed by the peritoneum. Its large surface area supports digestion and absorption. Finger-like villi increase the available surface area. Nutrients pass through the inner lining and are absorbed by blood vessels contained in the wall of the intestine.', true],
      ['15', 'The large intestine', 'The large intestine, or colon, is approximately 1.5 metres long. It absorbs electrolytes and vitamins, then stores and expels faeces. The labelled diagram identifies the appendix, ascending colon, transverse colon, descending colon, sigmoid colon and rectum as parts of the lower digestive tract.', true],
      ['16', 'The peritoneum', 'The peritoneum is a serous membrane that lines the abdominal cavity. It has two layers that cover the abdominal organs. Serous fluid between the layers helps prevent friction as the organs move. The peritoneum attaches organs to the posterior abdominal wall and carries blood vessels that help fight infection.', true],
      ['17', 'Accessory organs of digestion', 'Accessory organs produce some of the chemicals necessary for digestion. The source document lists the salivary glands, liver and pancreas. They support the alimentary canal by adding saliva, bile and pancreatic secretions, even though food does not pass through the organs themselves.', true],
      ['18', 'The main functions of the liver', 'The liver carries out carbohydrate, lipid (fat) and protein metabolism. It processes drugs and hormones, excretes bilirubin, produces bile, and stores vitamins and minerals. It also carries out phagocytosis, breaking down cells. The source describes the liver as the highest heat-producing organ in the body: it makes, breaks down and stores substances.', true],
      ['19', 'The gallbladder and bile', 'The main function of bile is to help digest fat and aid its absorption. The gallbladder stores and concentrates bile, then releases it into the small intestine, specifically the first part, the duodenum. Bile therefore links the liver and gallbladder with digestion and absorption in the small intestine.', true],
      ['20', 'The pancreas', 'The pancreas is a dual-function organ approximately 12–15 cm long and positioned behind the stomach. Its exocrine function is to secrete digestive enzymes in pancreatic juice to aid digestion. Its endocrine function is to secrete the hormones insulin and glucagon, which control blood sugar levels.', true]
    ]
  }
  ,
  trauma: {
    source: 'L3 - Process and Systems of Trauma v4 April 2025.ppsx + AAP Trauma Overview Aug24 v3.pdf',
    intro: 'This topic combines the trauma process and systems lesson with the Trauma Overview session. It follows the complete pathway from mechanism and major-trauma recognition through scene management, DR CAcBCDE assessment, life-saving care, transport, triage, handover and rehabilitation. The summaries below expand both supplied teaching documents into revision notes.',
    sections: [
      ['01', 'What is trauma?', 'Trauma is an injury caused when an external source of energy transfers force to the body faster than the body can sustain and dissipate it. The injury pattern depends on the type of energy, the direction and duration of the force, the surface or object involved and how the patient interacted with it.'],
      ['02', 'What is major trauma?', 'Major trauma is an injury likely to result in permanent disability or death. The seriousness of the injury may not be obvious at first, so the mechanism, patient factors and findings from a structured assessment all matter when deciding how urgently specialist care is needed.'],
      ['03', 'Why the trauma system matters', 'The deck identifies major trauma as the leading cause of death in patients under 40. It describes 22 major trauma centres across England from April 2012, including four in London, and reports that survival increased by 20 percent after major trauma centres were introduced.'],
      ['04', 'Trauma in London', 'The London data in the presentation covers April 2018 to June 2019. It describes an average of 20 trauma patients per day, an average journey of 18 minutes to a major trauma centre, an average blunt-injury scene time of 37 minutes and an average penetrating-injury scene time of 15 minutes. The teaching point is to avoid delaying on scene for actions that are not absolutely necessary.'],
      ['05', 'The major trauma system', 'Pre-hospital teams, HEMS, ambulance crews, major trauma centres and rehabilitation services work together. HEMS may include advanced paramedics, doctors, paramedics and a pilot, while the major trauma centre provides specialist consultants, nurses, doctors, surgeons and access to multidisciplinary rehabilitation.'],
      ['06', 'Immediate HEMS dispatch', 'The immediate dispatch protocol includes a person under a train, a fall of more than two floors or 20 feet, ejection from a vehicle, entrapment under a vehicle other than a motorcycle, death of another occupant and traumatic amputation above the wrist or above the ankle. The deck also includes requests from another emergency service, including the RNLI.'],
      ['07', 'Interrogation dispatch', 'Interrogation dispatch is used when more information is needed before deciding on the response. Examples in the deck include falls below the immediate-dispatch height, road traffic collisions, entrapment, impalement, amputation, stabbing or shooting, burns or scalds, electrocution, industrial or building-site incidents, explosions, hangings and drowning. Information can be gathered by calling the incident origin back or by listening to the 999 call.'],
      ['08', 'Using the trauma decision tree', 'The major trauma decision tool is designed to identify and triage patients who may benefit from a specialist trauma centre. There are adult and child versions. The current trauma tree should be checked before conveyance, inappropriate use can block beds and delay ambulance availability, and the step triggered must be documented on the patient report form.'],
      ['09', 'Local hospital stabilisation', 'The trauma tree does not mean that every patient should go directly to a major trauma centre. The deck identifies a local hospital as an option for stabilisation when required, such as for an unmanageable airway problem. Pre-hospital teams must therefore balance the need for specialist trauma care with immediate stabilisation and transport decisions.'],
      ['10', 'Major trauma centre assessment', 'At a major trauma centre, a specialist trauma team conducts a focused assessment and performs necessary life-saving interventions. Major trauma centres are open 24 hours a day, provide consultant-led care and have rapid access to diagnostic and treatment facilities such as CT scanning.'],
      ['11', 'The right place at the right time', 'Good trauma care means identifying the seriousness of injury as early as possible, transferring the patient to a major trauma centre when major trauma is recognised, completing investigations promptly on arrival and starting an appropriate rehabilitation programme. The central principle is the right place, at the right time, for the right care.'],
      ['12', 'Rehabilitation after trauma', 'Severe injury may require reconstructive surgery and a personalised rehabilitation programme. The deck names physiotherapists, occupational therapists and speech and language therapists, alongside other specialist services, as part of the multidisciplinary support that helps a patient return to an active life.'],
      ['13', 'Mechanism of injury', 'Mechanism of injury, or MOI, is the sum of the physical forces exerted on the body that result in injury. Establishing the MOI helps guide the history and assessment, but MOI alone cannot predict or exclude injury. A concerning mechanism should increase attention to the primary and secondary survey rather than replace them.'],
      ['14', 'Establishing the MOI', 'Build the MOI from the events leading up to the incident: what moved, what stopped, what struck the patient, where the patient was positioned, how far the patient travelled and what happened to the surrounding object or vehicle. The history may not match the scene or the examination, which is why the findings must be checked against a thorough assessment.'],
      ['15', 'Types of mechanism', 'The deck groups mechanisms as falls, abnormal flexion, extension or rotation such as whiplash or cervical-spine injury, blunt trauma caused by deceleration or compression, penetrating trauma and entrapment. Different forces can occur together, so more than one mechanism may need to be considered.'],
      ['16', 'Significant mechanism', 'Significant mechanisms include a fall over 6 metres or two storeys in an adult, a fall over 3 metres or twice the child height, entrapment, vehicle intrusion of more than 30 centimetres on the occupants side, ejection, death in the same passenger compartment, a motorcycle crash over 20 mph and a pedestrian or cyclist struck by a vehicle over 20 mph. Consult the LAS major trauma tree when these are present.'],
      ['17', 'Special considerations', 'Use a lower threshold for a trauma alert in a patient over 55, pregnancy over 20 weeks, anticoagulant use, a known bleeding or clotting disorder, morbid obesity, a child under 12 or whenever clinical judgement raises concern. These factors can change the risk associated with the same mechanism or make injuries harder to identify.'],
      ['18', 'Kinetic energy', 'The deck presents kinetic energy as one half multiplied by mass multiplied by velocity squared. In trauma, both the mass of the object and its velocity matter, but velocity has a particularly large effect because it is squared. A relatively small increase in speed can therefore produce a much larger change in energy.'],
      ['19', 'Applying kinetic energy to collisions', 'The presentation compares a 1,500 kilogram car travelling at 30 mph with the same car travelling at 40 mph. It gives approximately 675,000 kinetic-energy units at 30 mph and 1,200,000 at 40 mph. The figures illustrate why speed, not only vehicle mass, must be considered when interpreting a collision.'],
      ['20', 'Collision factors', 'When assessing a road traffic collision, consider the angle of impact, the movement and impact of the patient, the difference in size between vehicles, restraints such as seatbelts and safety mechanisms such as airbags. Crumple zones may dissipate energy before the passenger compartment shows obvious damage, so visible vehicle damage does not by itself describe the patient injuries.'],
      ['21', 'Conservation of energy', 'Energy can neither be created nor destroyed; it changes form. In the deck example, braking produces thermal energy, the crash produces mechanical energy, protective devices manipulate and dissipate energy, and injury represents mechanical energy transferred to the patient.'],
      ['22', 'Laws of motion and trauma', 'The first law describes the tendency of a body at rest to remain at rest and a moving body to continue moving unless acted on by an outside force. The second law links force with mass and acceleration using F = M x A. In a head-on collision, the speeds and directions of both vehicles contribute to the forces involved.'],
      ['23', 'Thermodynamics and burns', 'The deck introduces the laws of thermodynamics because heat transformation into thermal energy can cause burn injury. It describes energy added to a system as becoming increased heat energy and work, and explains that heat does not transfer from a lower temperature to a higher temperature without added energy.'],
      ['24', 'What is triage?', 'Triage categorises patients by the severity of their conditions and prioritises care accordingly. It is used when the number of patients outweighs the resources available on scene, such as during a major incident or road traffic collision. Triage is a prioritisation process, not a complete clinical assessment of every patient.'],
      ['25', 'Initial scene organisation', 'The first person on scene coordinates and promotes casualty care. The second person completes a scene survey and casualty care. The third person establishes a casualty clearing area. Subsequent responders should report to the first person or the Ambulance Scene Commander so that tasks and resources remain coordinated.'],
      ['26', 'Ten Second Triage purpose', 'Ten Second Triage is a rapid, non-physiological triage process focused on life-threatening injuries. It identifies the most severely injured, prioritises patients who need immediate life-saving interventions and allows responders to move quickly from one casualty to the next in order to save as many lives as possible.'],
      ['27', 'Ten Second Triage categories', 'The categories are Not Breathing, P1 immediate, P2 urgent, P3 delayed and Dead. Not Breathing and Dead are specific to Ten Second Triage and require clinical decision-making and reassessment. The deck also notes that Ten Second Triage supports CPR where resources allow.'],
      ['28', 'Life-saving intervention: haemorrhage', 'The two key Ten Second Triage interventions are haemorrhage control and airway management. For external haemorrhage, use pressure, packing or a tourniquet as appropriate. A junctional bleed should still be packed and have pressure applied for at least three minutes. Capable bystanders may help with this so that the responder can continue triage.'],
      ['29', 'Life-saving intervention: airway', 'Airway management may include positioning and airway adjuncts. The purpose at this stage is to maintain the airway sufficiently to address an immediate life threat while the responder continues the wider triage process. The deck presents airway maintenance alongside external haemorrhage control as a core life-saving intervention.'],
      ['30', 'Reassessment of not breathing patients', 'Patients marked Not Breathing during Ten Second Triage must be reassessed by a clinical responder or healthcare professional as soon as possible. If ongoing resuscitation is considered appropriate, the patient is re-tagged P1 and prioritised for treatment and evacuation. If resuscitation is futile or resources are unavailable, the patient may be recognised as Dead with the appropriate black tag.'],
      ['31', 'Secondary triage decision', 'Once life-saving interventions and Ten Second Triage are complete, the LAS Scene Commander decides whether to continue Ten Second Triage, return to business-as-usual clinical practice or commence secondary triage. Secondary triage may be led by a senior clinician on scene or use the Major Incident Triage Tool.'],
      ['32', 'Major Incident Triage Tool', 'The Major Incident Triage Tool is secondary to life-saving interventions and Ten Second Triage and is undertaken by clinical responders only. It is recorded using full-size secondary triage tags and is intended for casualty collection points or clearing stations rather than being performed in situ.'],
      ['33', 'Secondary triage tags', 'The secondary tag is a multifold card with P1, P2 and P3 panels. It is folded so that the required priority is visible and provides space to record casualty information, observations and interventions. A separate Dead tag is used in both Ten Second Triage and secondary triage.'],
      ['34', 'Avoiding over and under-triage', 'Over-triage places pressure on limited resources on scene and has been linked to critical mortality. Under-triage is dangerous because a patient may not receive medical attention when it is required. Triage decisions should therefore be structured, recorded and revised when new information changes the picture.'],
      ['35', 'METHANE: major-incident report', 'METHANE provides initial communication at a major incident: M is Major incident declared, E is Exact location, T is Type of incident, H is Hazards known or potential, A is Access and egress for vehicles, N is Number of casualties and E is Emergency services required.'],
      ['36', 'SCENE: initial trauma assessment', 'SCENE supports assessment of the initial trauma scene: S is Safety, C is Cause or MOI, E is Environment, N is Number of patients and E is Extra resources needed. Together, METHANE and SCENE help communicate the incident, identify risks and organise an effective response.'],
      ['37', 'Windscreen reporting', 'The deck describes a windscreen report for ambulance crews: press priority on the airway radio, give the Emergency Operations Centre your call sign and CAD number, state exactly what you can see and follow with a METHANE report. The report should communicate the situation before detailed patient handover begins.'],
      ['38', 'ATMIST trauma handover', 'ATMIST is used for trauma handover: A is Age, T is Time of incident, M is Mechanism, I is Injuries, S is Signs and symptoms and T is Treatment given or immediate needs. It keeps the receiving team focused on the incident, the injury pattern, current findings and actions already taken.'],
      ['39', 'CATMIST trauma blue call', 'CATMIST is used for a trauma blue call. C is Call sign or CAD, A is Age, T is Time, M is Mechanism, I is Injuries found, S is vital Signs and T is Treatment given. The additional call-sign or CAD detail supports communication with the receiving trauma system.'],
      ['40', 'SBAR handover', 'SBAR provides a logical sequence for concise information: Situation, Background, Assessment and Recommendation. It can be used for hospital handover, on-scene handover to arriving crews and other situations where important information must be passed to the person responsible for the patient.'],
      ['41', 'Good handover practice', 'Speak to the person responsible for the care of the patient. Make notes in advance so that vital information is not missed, ensure everyone is listening, keep the handover clear and concise, and write the information on the patient report form for the hospital. Missing information during a high-stress handover can lead to treatment errors and harm.'],
      ['42', 'The complete trauma pathway', 'Effective trauma care connects scene safety, MOI, early recognition, appropriate dispatch, structured triage, rapid life-saving intervention, transfer to the correct facility, specialist trauma care, clear handover and rehabilitation. Reassess as the patient\'s condition changes and communicate new risks or needs promptly.'],
      ['43', 'Trauma Overview session focus', 'The overview is designed to support management of trauma in emergency and urgent care. It complements existing knowledge of simple wounds and minor injuries by applying those skills to major trauma, where rapid assessment, immediate intervention and efficient transfer are priorities.'],
      ['44', 'Key messages before starting', 'Prioritise scene safety and minimal on-scene time. Anticipate what the paramedic will do, have equipment ready, expedite packaging and removal, avoid unnecessary interventions and tell the paramedic about all relevant findings.'],
      ['45', 'When trauma deaths occur', 'The overview divides trauma deaths into immediate fatal injury, early deaths related to A, B, C and D problems, and late deaths such as infection or acute respiratory distress syndrome. The practical question is what can be done at each phase to reduce preventable deaths.'],
      ['46', 'Blood loss and compensation', 'The overview identifies blood loss as the major cause of death in trauma. Many trauma patients are young and healthy and compensate for blood loss through vasoconstriction, increased heart rate and increased respiratory rate. These responses can make serious blood loss difficult to recognise early.'],
      ['47', 'Do not rely on normal-looking vital signs', 'A young patient may compensate until late in the process. A heart rate of 100 may be almost twice the patient resting rate even though it is only slightly outside a textbook range of 60 to 80. A respiratory rate of 24 may similarly be double a resting rate despite being only slightly above the textbook range of 12 to 20. Interpret observations in context and look for trends.'],
      ['48', 'Who is at risk of serious injury?', 'The overview highlights young healthy adults, especially when alcohol or drugs are involved, older people with reduced resilience or multiple medical problems and medications, and children who may have little concept of danger. Risk depends on the patient as well as the mechanism.'],
      ['49', 'Low-mechanism injury in older patients', 'Older patients can sustain significant injury after a fall from standing or a fall downstairs. Anticoagulant medication can complicate the injury. The overview stresses careful neurological assessment, maintaining the patient in a neutral position when indicated and recognising that not applying a collar is not the same as clearing the spine.'],
      ['50', 'Why serious injuries are missed', 'Serious injury may be missed when the forces are not witnessed or appreciated, the scene takes too long, experience is limited, the mechanism is underestimated or the patient compensates well. A patient who looks relatively well can still have life-threatening injury.'],
      ['51', 'Time-critical trauma', 'The overview teaches minimal on-scene time for major trauma: minutes for penetrating trauma and no more than 20 minutes for blunt trauma. Penetrating injury cannot be fixed on scene or in the ambulance, so keep the scene time to the absolute minimum while completing essential life-saving actions.'],
      ['52', 'Prepare for packaging and removal', 'When immediate movement is not yet possible, get everything ready: kit out, trolley out, ambulance pointing in the right direction, heating on, exit route planned and clear, and family and police briefed. Preparation reduces avoidable delays once the patient is ready to move.'],
      ['53', 'Scene management is teamwork', 'Involve everyone and match the skill level to the task. Maintain an overview, anticipate and multitask, communicate clearly and allocate key roles such as who is holding the head. Good trauma care is a team activity in which each person helps keep the process moving.'],
      ['54', 'The first 10 minutes', 'The first 10 minutes are vital. Use a consistent approach, identify life-threatening injuries rapidly, assess and manage at the same time, and assume the worst until it has been excluded or treated. A stable-looking patient still needs continual reassessment.'],
      ['55', 'The major trauma approach', 'The overview presents a linked sequence: scene assessment, primary survey, life-saving interventions, initiate transport, secondary survey, management as needed and pain control. If the patient fails at any stage, return to the immediate assessment and treatment pathway rather than progressing as if stable.'],
      ['56', 'DR CAcBCDE primary survey', 'The primary survey is Danger, Response, catastrophic haemorrhage, Airway, C-spine consideration, Breathing, Circulation including blood on the floor plus four more, Disability, and Expose and evaluate. Assessment and treatment are simultaneous, with life-threatening findings managed as they are identified.'],
      ['57', 'Airway, oxygen and the injured brain', 'The overview emphasises effective airway management and states that seriously injured patients should receive 100 percent oxygen as soon as the airway is clear. It also teaches that a patient with serious head injury or loss of consciousness should be treated as having a spinal injury until proven otherwise. Hypoxia or hypotension can worsen brain injury.'],
      ['58', 'Exposure and movement', 'Expose the patient so injuries are not missed, then keep the patient warm afterwards. Minimise movement of the seriously injured patient, but do not allow movement precautions or equipment problems to create unnecessary delay in transport.'],
      ['59', 'The supporting crew role', 'Supporting staff should anticipate, monitor, prepare and plan. Pre-empt the paramedic, prepare the equipment, keep packaging and removal moving, share findings promptly and think about additional support early when tasked to significant trauma.'],
      ['60', 'Trauma Priority Call: CATMIST', 'The overview gives the Trauma Priority Call format as CATMIST: Call sign, Age, Time of injury, Mechanism of injury, Injuries found and suspected, Signs including vital signs, and Treatment given or required. This complements the CATMIST trauma-blue-call detail in the process-and-systems material.'],
      ['61', 'Overview key messages', 'Be thorough with airway management, do not get distracted, expose the patient and keep them warm afterwards, recognise that hypoxia and hypotension can harm the brain-injured patient, protect the first clot and keep movement minimal without slowing the patient journey. Get the patient to the right hospital, at the right time, first time.']
    ]
  },
  'chest-injuries': {
    source: 'AAP Chest Injuries May25 v6.ppsx',
    intro: 'This topic covers the recognition and initial management of chest injuries in the emergency and urgent care setting. Work from the mechanism of injury and the patient presentation, identify threats to ventilation and circulation early, and remember that a chest injury may be associated with injuries to the abdomen, spine and other structures. Assessment and treatment happen together, with urgent escalation and time-critical transfer when the problem cannot be corrected locally.',
    sections: [
      ['01', 'Why chest injuries matter', 'Chest trauma is directly responsible for a substantial proportion of trauma deaths and contributes to many more. Most serious chest injuries can be managed with resuscitation and chest drainage, while a smaller proportion require surgery. The major early problem is hypoxia, caused by impaired ventilation or by hypovolaemia.'],
      ['02', 'Structures at risk', 'Chest trauma can injure the heart, blood vessels, lungs, airways, soft tissues, bones, spinal cord and nerves. Rib injury should also prompt consideration of associated injury to the liver, kidneys, spleen, stomach, pancreas and gall bladder. Always consider whether a neck or spinal injury could also be present.'],
      ['03', 'Mechanism of injury', 'Mechanism of injury helps estimate the likelihood of significant chest trauma. Consider the force and energy delivered, its duration and direction, and whether the injury was blunt or penetrating. Examples include road traffic collisions, falls, sports and industrial injuries, stabbing, shooting, crushing and assaults.'],
      ['04', 'Scene priorities', 'Ask what happened and what forces were involved: whether the patient was stabbed or shot, whether the chest was crushed, whether a vehicle struck a wall at speed, whether the patient was kicked or hit, and the height and landing surface of a fall. Consider age, request senior clinical help or other emergency-service support early when needed, and keep the patient journey moving.'],
      ['05', 'Recognising chest injury', 'Possible findings include hypoxia, dyspnoea, tachypnoea or bradypnoea, shallow breathing, accessory-muscle use, abnormal chest-wall movement, deformity, bruising, swelling or wounds, pain and tenderness, blood loss, shock, reduced consciousness and anxiety. Severity varies widely, so continually ask whether the patient is time critical.'],
      ['06', 'Rib fractures', 'Rib fractures are the most common chest injury. Pain can make the patient breathe shallowly, causing inadequate ventilation. A fracture may cause bleeding and may damage the lung or other soft tissue, so do not treat an apparently isolated rib injury as proof that no serious injury exists. Remain alert for associated injuries.'],
      ['07', 'Sternal fracture', 'A fractured sternum occurs in a minority of patients with blunt thoracic trauma. Although the fracture itself may not be the main problem, the force required to break the sternum indicates a significant probability of other major injuries. Evaluate the patient carefully, request clinician intervention when indicated and treat a time-critical presentation urgently.'],
      ['08', 'Flail chest', 'Flail chest occurs when two or more ribs and/or the sternum are fractured in two or more places, creating a segment of chest wall that has lost its normal rigid support. The segment may move inwards during inspiration and outwards during expiration. This paradoxical movement can impair ventilation, and bleeding may accompany each fracture.'],
      ['09', 'Pneumothorax', 'The pleura has parietal and visceral layers with a pleural space between them. A pneumothorax is air in that space. It may be spontaneous or traumatic, following blunt or penetrating injury. Symptoms depend on the amount of air and the effect on ventilation and circulation; some pneumothoraces become time critical and life threatening.'],
      ['10', 'Tension pneumothorax', 'In a tension pneumothorax, air enters the pleural space but cannot escape. Pressure rises, the lung collapses, mediastinal structures may shift and venous return to the heart can be obstructed, producing obstructive shock. The patient may become increasingly dyspnoeic, tachycardic and hypotensive, with reduced or absent breath sounds, reduced chest movement, distended neck veins or tracheal deviation. This requires advanced clinical intervention.'],
      ['11', 'Open pneumothorax', 'An open pneumothorax follows penetrating trauma when a chest-wall wound allows air to enter the pleural space. With inspiration, air may move through the wound; if the wound is large enough, air can move freely in and out. This is commonly described as a sucking chest wound and needs urgent assessment and appropriate chest-wound management.'],
      ['12', 'Haemothorax', 'A haemothorax is blood in the thoracic or pleural cavity. It commonly follows penetrating injury to major vessels, but blunt deceleration can also cause shearing injury. As blood fills the chest it occupies space needed by the lungs, creating a dual problem of hypoxia and hypovolaemia. Severity determines how rapidly it becomes time critical and life threatening.'],
      ['13', 'Cardiac tamponade', 'Cardiac tamponade occurs when fluid, commonly blood after penetrating injury, accumulates in the pericardial sac and compresses the heart. The pressure limits cardiac filling and pumping. The deck identifies tamponade as time critical and life threatening, requiring urgent recognition, escalation and advanced management.'],
      ['14', 'Primary survey', 'Assess the patient using C ABCDE: catastrophic haemorrhage, airway, breathing, circulation, disability and exposure, while considering the mechanism and spinal injury. Major chest injuries may cause failure at breathing. If the problem cannot be corrected in the current setting, arrange a time-critical transfer to a major trauma unit and request appropriate clinical support.'],
      ['15', 'Breathing assessment', 'Expose the chest and count the respiratory rate. Assess effort and oxygen saturation, inspect for wounds, swelling, deformity and bruising, and look for symmetrical or paradoxical movement. Inspect the back and axilla as well as the front of the chest. Look at the neck for distended veins or tracheal deviation, listen once to both lungs to confirm air entry, and feel for injury, instability, tenderness, depth and symmetry of movement.'],
      ['16', 'Subcutaneous emphysema and the TWELVE mnemonic', 'Subcutaneous emphysema may be associated with significant thoracic injury. Focus on ABC management and use the local TWELVE mnemonic as taught in JRCALC guidance to support assessment. Report abnormal chest sounds and other findings clearly, and do not let a mnemonic replace clinical judgement or urgent escalation.'],
      ['17', 'Secondary survey', 'If the patient is time critical, complete the secondary survey en route rather than delaying transfer. Assess for other injuries with a top-to-toe examination, review the mechanism, obtain a pain score, listen to and report chest sounds, record relevant past medical history and perform an ECG when indicated. Reassess whenever the patient changes.'],
      ['18', 'Oxygen and escalation', 'The deck directs initial oxygen management for major trauma according to JRCALC guidance: use a reservoir mask at 15 litres per minute, then reduce the dose once vital signs are normal and target a saturation of 94-98 percent. Request APP, HEMS or paramedic support as appropriate, particularly when the patient is time critical or the chest problem is not responding to initial care.'],
      ['19', 'Analgesia', 'Follow JRCALC guidance for analgesia. Do not give nitrous oxide to a patient with chest injuries and a clinically suspected pneumothorax. The deck also advises caution with Entonox because it may enlarge a pneumothorax. Request paramedic intervention for options such as morphine or intravenous paracetamol when required.'],
      ['20', 'Chest-wound dressings', 'The deck lists chest-seal products including Russell Chest Seal, Corius Seal Dressing and Fox Chest Seal. Use the equipment and procedure authorised by local policy and the clinician responsible for the patient. Continue to reassess breathing, chest movement, oxygenation and circulation after dressing a wound.'],
      ['21', 'Time-critical chest trauma', 'Chest trauma can deteriorate quickly through hypoxia, blood loss, obstructive shock or impaired ventilation. Use the mechanism, examination and response to initial treatment to decide how urgent the transfer is. Keep on-scene time focused on essential life-saving assessment and treatment, request advanced help early and communicate clearly what was found, what was done and what is still required.'],
      ['22', 'Key revision points', 'Expose and examine the whole chest, including the back and axilla. Look, listen and feel; compare both sides and reassess. Consider pneumothorax, tension pneumothorax, haemothorax, tamponade, flail chest and associated abdominal or spinal injury. A patient with a chest injury may fail at breathing before other parts of the primary survey, so identify the problem early and escalate without delay.']
    ]
  },
  drowning: {
    source: 'AAP drowning v2 Dec25.ppsx',
    intro: 'This topic covers drowning as a time-critical emergency in which immersion or submersion causes respiratory impairment. The priorities are safety, rapid rescue without creating more casualties, early recognition and treatment of hypoxia, prevention and treatment of hypothermia, and transfer for medical assessment because complications can develop after the incident.',
    sections: [
      ['01', 'Why drowning matters', 'Drowning is a common cause of accidental death, particularly at the extremes of age. The supplied lesson uses London water-related incidents to show the scale of the problem and highlights the risks around rivers, streams and other blue spaces. Every incident requires a safety-led, structured response.'],
      ['02', 'Definition of drowning', 'Drowning is a process of respiratory impairment caused by submersion or immersion in liquid. The mouth and nose are occluded by liquid, preventing normal inhalation of air. Drowning is therefore primarily a hypoxia and airway-breathing emergency, although other causes may have triggered the incident.'],
      ['03', 'Submersion and immersion', 'Submersion describes the airway being occluded by liquid; the whole body does not necessarily need to be below the surface. Immersion means being covered or surrounded by a liquid medium and does not necessarily mean the entire body is underwater. The key clinical question is whether breathing has been impaired.'],
      ['04', 'How drowning develops', 'A conscious person may initially hold their breath, then swallow water while trying to avoid inhaling it. The urge to breathe eventually takes over and water may enter the lungs. Laryngospasm, an involuntary closure of the larynx, can also occur. The result may be asphyxia followed by hypoxia.'],
      ['05', 'Aspiration and surfactant', 'Aspirated water can wash away surfactant. Surfactant helps keep the alveoli open; when it is lost, the work of breathing increases and higher ventilation pressures may be needed. Respiratory deterioration can therefore continue even after the person has been removed from the water.'],
      ['06', 'Other causes and aggravating factors', 'An immersion incident may have been precipitated by a medical event such as a convulsion, or by traumatic injury. Alcohol and drugs can increase risk and may impair judgement, consciousness, coordination and the ability to self-rescue. Look for the cause as well as the consequences of the water incident.'],
      ['07', 'Survival after submersion', 'The duration of hypoxia is a key factor in survival. Water temperature and associated hypothermia also influence the picture, and cold water may extend the period in which successful resuscitation is possible. Treat every patient urgently; do not use time or temperature alone to decide that treatment is futile.'],
      ['08', 'Fresh water and salt water', 'The historic distinction between fresh-water and salt-water drowning does not change the initial management described in the lesson. Prioritise safety, airway and breathing assessment, oxygenation, circulation, disability, temperature and escalation rather than focusing on the type of water.'],
      ['09', 'Dynamic risk assessment', 'Before attempting rescue, keep a safe distance and assess the number of patients, their age and size, the time in the water, possible air pockets and the approximate water temperature. Consider your own capability and the equipment or additional support needed. Do not create another casualty by entering water without appropriate training and resources.'],
      ['10', 'Communication at the scene', 'Use SBAR to communicate the situation and request support. Include the location and hazards, the number and condition of patients, the estimated duration and type of immersion or submersion, water temperature when known, the rescue status and the help required. Early escalation is part of the clinical response.'],
      ['11', 'Removing the casualty', 'Conscious casualties should be encouraged to keep fighting for survival while rescue is arranged and performed safely. A casualty with a reduced level of consciousness should be removed from the water in a horizontal position where possible. Horizontal removal helps limit the effects of hydrostatic pressure and supports blood pressure and core circulation.'],
      ['12', 'Immediate priorities after rescue', 'After rescue, use a structured DR ABCDE approach. Safety comes first, then response and catastrophic haemorrhage, airway, breathing, circulation, disability and exposure. Drowning patients may be profoundly hypoxic, so monitor and address oxygenation early while continuing to look for trauma, medical causes and hypothermia.'],
      ['13', 'Airway and breathing', 'Consider cervical-spine injury when the mechanism suggests it, particularly after a jump, fall or collision. Address hypoxaemia, consider bag-valve-mask ventilation when indicated, monitor airway and breathing closely, measure respiratory rate and prepare to suction. Pulse oximetry may be unreliable in a cold or poorly perfused patient, so interpret it alongside the clinical assessment.'],
      ['14', 'Spontaneously breathing patients', 'A patient who is spontaneously breathing after drowning still needs close monitoring and medical assessment. Remove wet clothing, address hypothermia, reassess airway and breathing, and watch for deterioration. Tympanic temperature readings may be unreliable after cold-water exposure. Always transfer these patients to appropriate care as directed by local policy and clinical guidance.'],
      ['15', 'Patients who are not breathing', 'If a drowning patient is not breathing normally, commence resuscitation in line with current local guidance and request senior clinical support. The lesson specifically emphasises commencing resuscitation on children unless a senior clinician states otherwise. Use the appropriate submerged-person guidance and continue reassessment throughout the response.'],
      ['16', 'Hypothermia', 'Anticipate and address hypothermia in patients recovered from water. Remove wet clothing, protect the patient from further heat loss and use the available clinical assessment rather than relying on a single temperature reading. Cold water can alter physiology and may make observations, including pulse oximetry and tympanic temperature, less reliable.'],
      ['17', 'Delayed complications and transfer', 'Complications may develop hours after a drowning incident, including worsening respiratory problems. A patient who looks well immediately after rescue is not necessarily safe for discharge. Follow the Submerged Person Tool and local clinical guidance, escalate concerns and ensure the patient is transferred for appropriate medical assessment.'],
      ['18', 'Key learning points', 'Safety first: protect rescuers and patients, address hypoxia, anticipate hypothermia, consider trauma and medical causes, and monitor closely after rescue. Water temperature and prolonged submersion do not remove the need for resuscitation. Structured communication, early escalation and appropriate transfer are essential.']
    ]
  },
  'suspension-blast-ballistics': {
    source: 'AAP Suspension Blast Ballistics v4 Oct25.ppsx',
    intro: 'This topic brings together three high-risk trauma presentations: suspension trauma, hanging, and blast or ballistic injury. The common priorities are dynamic scene safety, early escalation, rapid recognition of life threats, structured ABC management and transfer to appropriate specialist care. Mechanism matters, but patient care remains the priority once the scene is safe.',
    sections: [
      ['01', 'Topic overview', 'Suspension, hanging, blast and ballistic injuries can produce rapidly fatal airway, breathing, circulation and neurological problems. Incidents may be accidental, intentional, occupational, domestic, industrial or related to warfare or terrorism. Approach every scene dynamically, request help early and keep reassessing the patient and the hazards.'],
      ['02', 'Suspension trauma: definition', 'Suspension trauma describes a casualty who develops pre-syncopal symptoms or becomes unconscious while suspended in a harness. The supplied guidance states that the casualty should be rescued as soon as it is safely possible. A suspended, immobile casualty can deteriorate even when there is no obvious external injury.'],
      ['03', 'Why immobility is dangerous', 'Venous circulation in the lower limbs depends heavily on muscle contraction. When a person is suspended and immobile, the leg muscles no longer assist venous return. Venous pooling and harness pressure can then trigger a rapidly worsening cascade that reduces effective circulation and may lead to loss of consciousness.'],
      ['04', 'The suspension cascade', 'With the legs dangling and unable to move, venous pooling may begin within approximately 3-6 minutes. Over the following 6-12 minutes, obstruction of venous return can worsen as the harness compresses the femoral veins and abdominal pressure compresses the inferior vena cava. Loss of consciousness may follow. Treat these timeframes as warnings, not as a reason to delay rescue.'],
      ['05', 'Suspension trauma management', 'Use standard ABC management while planning a safe rescue. If a conscious casualty cannot be released immediately, elevation of the legs by the casualty or rescuer may prolong tolerance of suspension where this can be done safely. The priority remains rescue as soon as safely possible, with early escalation and continuous monitoring.'],
      ['06', 'Suspension scene safety', 'A dynamic risk assessment is essential before approaching a suspended casualty. Consider height, fall risk, equipment, access, structural stability, rescuers required and how the casualty will be released. Do not create additional casualties by rushing an unsafe rescue. Request specialist support early when the rescue is beyond the team capability.'],
      ['07', 'Hanging: context and mechanism', 'Hanging may be accidental or deliberate, and may be fully or partially supported. The lesson describes short-drop and long-drop mechanisms. A short drop may cause death through compression of the airway and neck blood vessels; a long drop may cause fracture-dislocation of the upper cervical vertebrae. There is no fixed height definition, so record and hand over the approximate height and mechanism.'],
      ['08', 'Initial hanging assessment', 'Begin with a dynamic risk assessment and identify whether there are signs of life or a need for resuscitation. Call for help early. If safe and clinically required, remove or cut the ligature and move the patient into an appropriate position for assessment and management. Consider cervical-spine care, while remembering that the airway takes priority if airway management is required.'],
      ['09', 'Airway risks after hanging', 'Airway management may be difficult because the neck may have sustained injury and airway swelling may develop. Look for airway compromise, protect the airway and escalate for specialist support. Cervical-spine precautions should be considered, but must not delay a necessary airway intervention.'],
      ['10', 'Scene preservation when appropriate', 'If CPR is not commenced, the lesson advises that staff should avoid disturbing the scene where possible to support police investigation. This must be balanced with patient care, safety and any intervention required to assess or manage the patient. Patient care remains the priority.'],
      ['11', 'Post-ROSC and neurological concerns', 'After return of spontaneous circulation, patients may remain hypoxic and agitated and may need specialist on-scene care. Continue structured assessment, monitor airway and breathing, and consider possible spine or spinal-cord injury. Do not assume that return of circulation means the danger has passed.'],
      ['12', 'Blast injury: sources', 'Blast incidents may occur in warfare or terrorism, industrial or residential settings, gas leaks, factories, commercial sites, fuel refineries and transport. The scene may contain ongoing fire, collapse, hazardous substances, secondary devices or unstable structures. Scene safety and dynamic risk assessment come before casualty contact.'],
      ['13', 'The five blast-injury categories', 'The lesson groups blast injuries into primary, secondary, tertiary, quaternary and quinary categories. More than one category may affect the same patient. Use the categories to anticipate hidden injury, but assess and treat the actual patient presentation rather than relying on the label alone.'],
      ['14', 'Primary blast injury', 'Primary blast injury is caused by the blast shock wave. Possible injuries include blast lung, ruptured tympanic membranes and bowel perforation. These injuries may be present even when external wounds are limited, so consider the pressure exposure, the distance and the patient symptoms.'],
      ['15', 'Secondary blast injury', 'Secondary blast injury is caused by flying fragments and debris. It can produce penetrating trauma and lacerations. Look for wounds that may be multiple or concealed, control life-threatening haemorrhage and assess for injury along possible fragment paths.'],
      ['16', 'Tertiary blast injury', 'Tertiary blast injury occurs when the blast wave propels the patient against an object. It causes blunt trauma and fractures. Assess the patient as you would after a significant impact, including consideration of head, spinal, chest, abdominal and limb injuries.'],
      ['17', 'Quaternary and quinary blast injury', 'Quaternary injuries are other explosion-related injuries and illnesses, including burns, asphyxiation and toxic-substance exposure. Quinary injury refers to effects from additives such as bacteria and radiation, with possible chemical burns, radiation injury or infection. Consider environmental hazards and request the appropriate specialist support.'],
      ['18', 'Penetrating trauma overview', 'Penetrating trauma includes stab wounds and gunshot wounds. External appearance can underestimate internal damage. The mechanism, weapon or projectile, energy, wound sites and possible path should guide the assessment, while treatment follows the immediate life threats identified in the primary survey.'],
      ['19', 'Stab wounds', 'Stab wounds are described as low-velocity, low-energy penetrating trauma. A single weapon can cause multiple wounds. Injury may be limited to the depth and area of penetration, but a small external wound can conceal significant internal damage. Search systematically for additional wounds and consider the structures along the likely path.'],
      ['20', 'Gunshot wounds and energy', 'Higher-velocity weapons transfer much more energy into the patient. A projectile can create a permanent cavity along its path and a temporary cavity as energy is transferred to surrounding tissues. High-energy projectiles therefore have greater potential for tissue damage, even when the entrance wound appears small.'],
      ['21', 'Cavitation', 'The permanent cavity is formed by the pathway of the projectile. Temporary cavitation relates to the energy carried by the projectile; greater energy produces a larger temporary cavity. The lesson compares the effect with an explosion inside the body and notes that the exit wound may be larger than the entrance wound.'],
      ['22', 'Ricochet and erratic paths', 'A bullet may ricochet rather than follow a straight path, increasing tissue damage and making the pathway difficult to predict. If there is no exit wound, the lesson states that all of the projectile kinetic energy has been transferred to the body. Never assume the internal path from the external wounds alone.'],
      ['23', 'Entry and exit wounds', 'The entrance wound is often approximately the size of the projectile, while the exit wound can be considerably larger and may not be in the expected location. Examine the whole patient, preserve relevant evidence where possible and communicate all wound locations and findings to the receiving team.'],
      ['24', 'Ballistic scene considerations', 'Consider scene safety, weapon type, the crime scene, kinetic energy and the anatomical structures along the potential projectile path. There may be an ongoing threat or additional weapons. Once the scene is safe, patient care is the priority; control catastrophic haemorrhage, manage airway and breathing, assess circulation and arrange appropriate escalation.'],
      ['25', 'Handover and staff welfare', 'Communicate the mechanism, hazards, number of casualties, injuries found or suspected, observations, interventions and immediate needs. Incidents involving hanging, blast or ballistic injury may affect staff, witnesses, families and bystanders. Offer debriefing and signposting to wellbeing services after the incident, and recognise that the impact may be short or long term.'],
      ['26', 'Key learning points', 'Rescue suspended casualties as soon as it is safely possible. Use ABC management and escalate early. In hanging, prioritise the airway while considering cervical-spine injury and preserve the scene when this does not conflict with patient care. For blast and ballistic injury, anticipate hidden internal damage, classify the mechanism, control immediate life threats and never let the external wound appearance reassure you prematurely.']
    ]
  },
  'abdominal-conditions': {
    source: 'Abdominal Conditions AAP v2 Dec25.ppsx',
    intro: 'This topic covers the assessment of abdominal pain and common abdominal conditions in the emergency and urgent care setting. Abdominal presentations range from minor, self-limiting problems to time-critical vascular, gastrointestinal, obstetric and surgical emergencies. Use a structured primary survey, a full history, a review of systems, focused examination, baseline observations, continuous reassessment and early escalation when red flags are present.',
    sections: [
      ['01', 'Why abdominal pain needs careful assessment', 'Abdominal pain is common, but the specific cause can be difficult to identify before hospital assessment and advanced testing. Many cases are relatively minor, while a smaller proportion are serious or life threatening. Older patients may have atypical presentations and a substantially higher mortality, so do not let a mild or unusual presentation create false reassurance.'],
      ['02', 'Localised and referred pain', 'Abdominal pain may be localised to the affected area or referred to another site because organs share overlapping innervation. Pain location supports assessment but does not establish a diagnosis. Consider the patient history, associated symptoms, examination findings, observations and the likely worst-case condition together.'],
      ['03', 'Abdominal quadrants', 'Dividing the abdomen into four quadrants supports diagnosis, helps highlight red flags and provides a consistent way to document findings. Record where the pain is located, whether it moves or radiates, and whether symptoms are related to position, meals, movement or opening the bowels.'],
      ['04', 'The nine abdominal regions', 'For more precise localisation, the abdomen can be divided into nine regions: right hypochondrium, epigastric, left hypochondrium, right lumbar, umbilical, left lumbar, right iliac, hypogastric and left iliac. This is particularly useful when describing central or less clearly localised symptoms.'],
      ['05', 'Primary survey: DR CAcBCDE', 'Use Danger, Response, catastrophic haemorrhage, Airway, Circulation, Breathing, Disability and Evaluate. Correct primary-survey problems as they are found. Summarise and evaluate the findings after the primary survey. If the patient is time critical, provide continuous assessment and treatment during transfer; complete a secondary survey when clinically appropriate.'],
      ['06', 'SAMPLE history', 'Use SAMPLE to structure the history: Signs and symptoms, Allergies, Medications, Past medical history, Last oral intake and Events. Ask what the patient feels and how it has changed, prescribed, over-the-counter and recreational medication, relevant medical and surgical history, recent food and fluid intake, and what led to the emergency call.'],
      ['07', 'Review of systems', 'A review of systems can identify associated features that change the level of concern. Ask about headache, visual disturbance, appetite change, nausea and vomiting, dehydration, indigestion, belching, breathlessness, cough, chest or abdominal pain, bowel habit, urine output, ankle swelling, rashes, itching, weakness, pallor and altered sensation.'],
      ['08', 'SOCRATES pain assessment', 'SOCRATES covers Site, Onset, Character, Radiation, Associated signs, Timescale, Exacerbating or alleviating factors and Severity. Ask the patient to point to the pain, establish how it began and what it feels like, ask whether it spreads, identify associated symptoms, clarify duration and triggers, and record a 0-10 severity score.'],
      ['09', 'Common abdominal and genitourinary conditions', 'The deck lists a wide differential, including constipation, urinary tract infection, renal or gall stones, bowel obstruction, appendicitis, diverticular disease, inflammatory bowel disease, peptic ulcer, pancreatitis, gall-bladder disease, GI bleed, hernia, trauma, ectopic pregnancy, miscarriage, ovarian or testicular torsion and urinary retention. Use the history and examination to identify the most likely and most dangerous possibilities.'],
      ['10', 'Acute abdomen and non-specific abdominal pain', 'Acute abdominal pain may be severe and time critical. The deck describes an acute abdomen as lasting less than seven days with no diagnosis after examination and investigations, and identifies non-specific abdominal pain as a common outcome. A lack of a pre-hospital diagnosis does not exclude serious disease; reassess and safety-net through appropriate transfer and escalation.'],
      ['11', 'Bowel obstruction', 'Bowel obstruction is partial or complete obstruction of the small or large intestine. Possible features include abdominal pain, nausea and vomiting, bile-stained vomit, faecal vomiting, absolute constipation and absence of flatus. Causes listed include adhesions, hernias, inflammatory bowel disease, tumours and diverticulitis.'],
      ['12', 'Obstruction red flags', 'Faecal vomiting, persistent vomiting, progressive distension, severe or worsening pain, absolute constipation and inability to pass flatus should raise concern. A so-called currant-jelly stool may also be a warning sign. Treat the presentation as potentially serious, monitor for deterioration and escalate for hospital assessment.'],
      ['13', 'Abdominal aortic aneurysm', 'An abdominal aortic aneurysm occurs when the aortic wall weakens and stretches, increasing the risk of rupture as it expands. Suspected rupture is a time-critical vascular emergency. Possible features include pallor, tachycardia, a thready pulse, hypotension and tearing or ripping pain in the abdomen, back, loin, groin or chest.'],
      ['14', 'Suspected AAA precautions', 'Do not palpate the abdomen if an AAA is suspected. Record blood pressure and heart rate in both arms where clinically appropriate and follow current JRCALC vascular-emergency guidance. A pulsatile mass is not present in every case, so its absence does not exclude AAA. Escalate and transfer urgently.'],
      ['15', 'Aortic dissection', 'Aortic dissection begins with a tear in the intimal layer of the aorta. Blood then tracks into the media, creating a false lumen while flow may continue through the true lumen. Dissection can occur rapidly and commonly involves the ascending aorta, where pressures are higher. Consider it in sudden severe pain and other compatible presentations.'],
      ['16', 'Gastrointestinal bleeding', 'GI bleeding is divided into upper and lower sources using the duodenum as the reference point. Haematemesis or melaena suggests an upper GI source, while bright-red or dark blood with clots passed per rectum suggests a lower GI bleed. Either can cause significant blood loss and deterioration.'],
      ['17', 'Upper and lower GI bleed causes', 'Upper GI bleeding may be caused by peptic ulcer disease, gastritis, oesophageal varices or tumours. Lower GI causes include diverticular disease, inflammatory or other bowel disease, haemorrhoids and tumours. Upper GI bleeding is more common in the deck and can lead to hypovolaemic shock.'],
      ['18', 'Assessing GI blood loss', 'Ask when bleeding began, the quantity and appearance of blood, whether there has been syncope, and whether the patient takes aspirin, NSAIDs, anticoagulants or antiplatelet medication. Ask about iron tablets and foods or dyes that may alter stool or vomit appearance. Review bleeding disorders, liver disease, previous abdominal surgery and alcohol use.'],
      ['19', 'Appendicitis', 'Appendicitis is swelling and infection of the appendix and commonly causes pain in the right lower abdomen. Associated features may include nausea, vomiting, loss of appetite, constipation, diarrhoea, a low-grade temperature, tachycardia, pallor or dehydration. It requires urgent hospital treatment and may not present typically in every patient.'],
      ['20', 'Peptic ulcer', 'A peptic ulcer is an erosion of the lining of the stomach or small intestine. Pain is often in the upper abdomen and may feel burning, frequently worsening at night. Use SOCRATES, ask about GI bleeding and relevant medication such as aspirin or NSAIDs, and assess for signs of deterioration or perforation.'],
      ['21', 'Diverticular disease and diverticulitis', 'Diverticula are small bulges or pouches in the bowel wall. Diverticulosis means diverticula are present without symptoms; diverticulitis describes symptomatic inflammation. Possible features include left or sometimes right lower-quadrant pain, pain after eating, relief after passing wind or opening the bowels, constipation, diarrhoea, blood in the stool and bloating.'],
      ['22', 'Hernias and strangulation', 'A hernia occurs when an internal part of the body pushes through a weakness in the surrounding muscle or tissue wall. A strangulated hernia can compromise the blood supply and lead to ischaemic bowel. Swelling at a hernia orifice may indicate obstruction. Strangulated hernia and obstructed bowel are medical emergencies requiring urgent hospital treatment.'],
      ['23', 'Ulcerative colitis', 'Ulcerative colitis is a long-term inflammatory condition affecting the colon and rectum. Ulcers may develop in the bowel lining and bleed or produce pus. Symptoms can include recurrent diarrhoea with blood, mucus or pus, abdominal pain, fatigue, loss of appetite, weight loss, painful joints and mouth ulcers.'],
      ['24', 'Crohn disease', 'Crohn disease is a long-term inflammatory bowel condition that is managed rather than cured. Possible features include diarrhoea, blood or mucus in the stool, abdominal pain, rectal or anal pain, fatigue, loss of appetite, weight loss and feeling generally unwell. History taking is important because symptoms overlap with other bowel conditions.'],
      ['25', 'Gastroenteritis', 'Gastroenteritis commonly causes diarrhoea and vomiting and is usually bacterial or viral. It affects all ages and is particularly common in young children. The deck gives typical durations of approximately five to seven days for diarrhoea and one or two days for vomiting, but serious pathology can be difficult to exclude pre-hospitally.'],
      ['26', 'Abdominal examination', 'Obtain consent and consider a chaperone. Inspect skin colour, pallor, bruising, scars, masses and any pulsation. Auscultation is performed in the right lower quadrant to confirm bowel sounds and may take up to two minutes. Palpation is not recommended in the deck except as very light palpation by a senior clinician, avoiding painful areas and following local scope of practice.'],
      ['27', 'Pain location and possible sources', 'Pain location can suggest, but cannot prove, the underlying source. The deck maps possible causes across the abdomen, including gall bladder, stomach, duodenum, pancreas, kidneys, small bowel, appendix, caecum, bladder, uterus, adnexae and sigmoid colon. Use location alongside history, associated symptoms, examination and observations.'],
      ['28', 'Management and escalation', 'Correct primary-survey problems as they are identified, take a full SAMPLE and SOCRATES history, complete a review of systems, obtain baseline observations and offer analgesia for pain or discomfort within scope and local guidance. Position the patient for comfort and condition, monitor continuously for trends, escalate early using SBAR and pre-alert when needed.'],
      ['29', 'Red flags and reassessment', 'Abdominal signs and symptoms can overlap and conditions can change quickly. Red flags include shock, pallor, severe or worsening pain, GI bleeding, persistent vomiting, faecal vomiting, absolute constipation, a suspected AAA, peritonism, significant dehydration, altered consciousness and pregnancy-related or testicular or ovarian emergencies. Do not be complacent when initial observations appear normal.'],
      ['30', 'Key learning points', 'Always gain a full history and review of systems. Localise pain without relying on location alone, use SAMPLE and SOCRATES, inspect carefully and obtain baseline observations. Consider the worst-case scenario, identify red flags early, manage problems as they appear, provide appropriate analgesia, monitor trends and escalate promptly for time-critical abdominal, vascular, gastrointestinal, obstetric, urological or surgical conditions.']
    ]
  },
  burns: {
    source: 'Burns AAP PP July2026 V2.ppsx',
    intro: 'This topic covers burns as injuries caused by energy, including thermal, electrical, chemical and radiation exposure. Burn care begins with scene safety and a complete primary survey, then moves to stopping the burning process, cooling the burn without cooling the patient, estimating extent and depth, identifying inhalation or other major injuries, providing appropriate analgesia, escalating early and conveying the patient to definitive care.',
    sections: [
      ['01', 'What is a burn?', 'A burn is an injury caused by exposure to energy. The deck identifies thermal, electrical, chemical and radiation energy as causes. A scald is a burn caused by contact with hot liquid or steam. The mechanism, duration and environment help determine the likely depth, extent and associated injuries.'],
      ['02', 'Common causes and prevention', 'Burns occur mainly in the home and workplace and many are preventable. Thermal injuries include flame, scald and contact burns. Scalds are common in kitchens, may affect unsupervised children who reach towards hot pans, and can affect older adults after falls. Consider practical home-safety advice as part of prevention.'],
      ['03', 'Thermal burns', 'Thermal burns include scalds, flame injuries and contact burns. Ask what caused the heat exposure, how long contact lasted, whether clothing was involved, whether the incident occurred indoors or outdoors, and whether there was smoke, an enclosed environment or another injury. Stop the burning process before focusing on detailed burn description.'],
      ['04', 'Electrical burns', 'Electrical burns occur when electricity flows through the body from an entry point to an exit point. The external injury may be small while deeper tissue, muscle, cardiac or neurological injury is significant. Treat the scene as hazardous, identify the source and follow the electrical-injury pathway. A 12-lead ECG is essential because arrhythmias may develop after the incident.'],
      ['05', 'Chemical burns', 'Chemical burns result from corrosive agents such as acids, alkalis in cleaning products, bleaches and cement, or organic products such as bitumen. Protect yourself and others, do not investigate containers or spillages, remove contaminated clothing by cutting it away where appropriate, and irrigate with clean water for 20 minutes. Prioritise the eyes, hands and face.'],
      ['06', 'Radiation and unusual mechanisms', 'Radiation is another form of energy that can cause burns. Ask about the source, duration and environment and identify whether there may be contamination or other hazards. Use specialist support and local guidance. Do not assume that an apparently small skin injury represents the full extent of the exposure.'],
      ['07', 'Superficial or first-degree burns', 'Erythema, or a superficial burn, involves the epidermis only. The skin is painful and red, without blistering, and usually heals without scar formation. Systemic response is minimal. Still assess the mechanism, location, extent and associated injuries rather than judging severity from redness alone.'],
      ['08', 'Partial-thickness burns', 'Partial-thickness burns involve the epidermis and dermis. They are commonly blistered, very painful and moist in appearance. The amount of skin involved, the location and the mechanism influence the urgency of care and the risk of fluid loss, infection and functional problems.'],
      ['09', 'Full-thickness burns', 'Full-thickness burns extend through all layers of the dermis. They may appear white, waxen or charred, are non-blanching and may be painless because nerve endings have been destroyed. Deeper structures such as nerves, muscles and tendons may be damaged. They do not heal without intervention and require definitive specialist assessment.'],
      ['10', 'Burn depth can be difficult to judge', 'Burn depth may be difficult to assess early and may evolve. A full-thickness burn on white skin can be mistaken for unburnt skin, while a superficial epidermal or dermal burn on pigmented skin may not appear red. Assess more than colour: consider sensation, blistering, moisture, blanching, mechanism and progression.'],
      ['11', 'Burn red flags', 'Red flags include airway burns, soot, oedema, respiratory distress, hot-gas inhalation, significant facial burns, other major injuries and circumferential burns of the chest, neck or a limb. The deck also highlights burns above 15 percent TBSA in adults and above 10 percent TBSA in children. Escalate early when red flags are present.'],
      ['12', 'Inhalation injury', 'Look and listen for airway burns, soot, facial burns, hot-gas exposure, stridor, black sputum, wheezing, hoarseness and respiratory distress. Inhalation injury can worsen after the initial incident through airway oedema, bronchoconstriction or respiratory failure. Address airway and breathing in the primary survey and seek senior clinical support early.'],
      ['13', 'Circumferential burns', 'Circumferential burns can affect the chest, neck or a limb. As swelling develops, a circumferential chest burn can restrict ventilation, while a limb burn can compromise distal circulation. Check respiratory effort, distal pulses and circulation, monitor for deterioration and escalate urgently. Do not wrap dressings circumferentially.'],
      ['14', 'Burn complications', 'Complications include shock, hypotension, end-organ hypoperfusion, infection, respiratory-tract damage, bronchoconstriction, acute respiratory distress syndrome, pain, psychological effects, scarring and associated injury. Co-morbidities can increase risk. Monitor continuously for trends rather than relying on one normal observation.'],
      ['15', 'Primary survey: DR CAcBCDE', 'Complete Danger, Response, catastrophic haemorrhage, Airway, Circulation, Breathing, Disability and Evaluate. Correct problems as they are found. Consider scene safety, other agencies, AVPU, airway burns, soot, inhalation injury, stridor, black sputum, respiratory rate, capillary refill, shock, distal pulses, wheeze, hoarseness, blood glucose and associated trauma.'],
      ['16', 'Scene safety and communication', 'Make the scene safe for yourself, colleagues, patients and others. Consider fire, electricity, chemicals, structural hazards and ongoing exposure. Use SBAR or a windscreen report, request appropriate support, expedite removal when needed and keep assessing stability or deterioration. Do not allow burn care to distract from a continuing hazard.'],
      ['17', 'Burn history', 'Ask the time of the burn, what treatment or cooling has already been provided and how long it lasted, the mechanism and source, contact time, chemical or oil exposure, electrical source, the environment and associated injuries. Use SAMPLE and SOCRATES, and ask about respiratory or cardiovascular disease, diabetes, mobility, safeguarding and other vulnerabilities.'],
      ['18', 'Secondary survey examination', 'Assess the head for injury, the neck with the relevant local approach, the chest for injury, pain, equal air entry and wheeze, and the abdomen for injury. Reassess AVPU or GCS, pupils and motor, sensory and circulation findings in all four limbs. Expose and assess all wounds and burns while protecting the patient from heat loss.'],
      ['19', 'Observations and monitoring', 'Monitor AVPU for change, airway and breathing for deterioration, respiratory rate and depth, repeat auscultation, response to oxygen, SpO2 and ETCO2 where available, heart rate, blood glucose and temperature. Obtain a complete set of baseline observations where possible and use trends to identify deterioration. Perform a 12-lead ECG for electrical injury.'],
      ['20', 'Estimating total body surface area', 'Estimate total body surface area using Wallace Rule of Nines, the Mersey Burns Chart app or the patient palm method. Record the extent of the burn as a percentage and distinguish partial- from full-thickness areas. Account for skin pigmentation because colour alone can make burn depth difficult to recognise.'],
      ['21', 'Patient palm method', 'For small or extensive patchy burns, the patient palmar surface including the fingers is used as an estimate of approximately 1 percent TBSA. Use the patient palm rather than the clinician hand, and document the estimate clearly. For larger or more complex burns, use an appropriate chart or app where available.'],
      ['22', 'Wallace Rule of Nines', 'Wallace Rule of Nines uses multiples of nine to estimate body surface area: head and neck 9 percent, each upper limb 9 percent, each lower limb 18 percent, anterior torso 18 percent, posterior torso 18 percent and genitals 1 percent. Irregular or patchy burns can be estimated using the patient palm method.'],
      ['23', 'Cooling the burn', 'Cool the burn for up to 20 minutes using running water, or saline if water is not available. Use tepid or lukewarm water rather than ice-cold water. Cool the burn, not the patient: avoid causing hypothermia, especially in children, older adults or patients with extensive burns. Continue monitoring the primary survey while cooling.'],
      ['24', 'Dressings and cling film', 'After cooling, use cling film as a temporary dressing when appropriate. Discard the first length, place sheets in a patchwork rather than wrapping around a limb or body part, and never apply it circumferentially. Do not put ointments, creams or similar products onto the burn unless directed by appropriate clinical guidance. Elevate the injured area where possible.'],
      ['25', 'Analgesia and removing constriction', 'Assess pain with SOCRATES before and after analgesia. Offer analgesia within local guidance, including Entonox where appropriate, and consider paramedic support for morphine or intravenous paracetamol. Cut away burned or smouldering material and remove constrictive items such as jewellery before swelling makes removal difficult. Correct ABCD problems first.'],
      ['26', 'Chemical-burn management', 'For chemical burns, prioritise safety and avoid investigating containers or spillages. Remove contaminated clothing by cutting it away, irrigate with clean water for 20 minutes and prioritise eyes, hands and face. Do not use cling film for chemical burns. Protect against hypothermia and request specialist hazardous-area support when required.'],
      ['27', 'Electrical and Taser-related injury', 'A successful Taser deployment is treated in the deck as an electrical injury and may also create penetrating injury if the skin is broken. Low voltages can affect implantable cardioverter-defibrillators. A 12-lead ECG and monitoring are essential because arrhythmias can develop after the incident. Assess for falls, burns and other trauma.'],
      ['28', 'Advanced clinical interventions', 'Some burn patients may require advanced interventions including early endotracheal intubation, intermittent positive-pressure ventilation, early opiate analgesia and early fluid resuscitation. These are clinician-led interventions. Prepare equipment, assist with extrication, communicate clearly and escalate early using SBAR.'],
      ['29', 'Triage and definitive care', 'Use the Major Trauma Triage Decision Tool and work through its steps in order. The deck highlights burns or scalds above 30 percent TBSA, facial burns with complete skin loss to the lower half of the face and circumferential flame burns as important triage features. Do not triage directly to a burns unit unless the applicable pathway directs this; convey to the correct definitive care.'],
      ['30', 'Key learning points', 'Complete the primary survey, stop the burning, cool the burn rather than the patient, assess depth and TBSA, identify airway or inhalation injury, treat other injuries and provide appropriate analgesia. Dress the wound safely, monitor for deterioration, escalate appropriately and convey the patient to the correct definitive care.']
    ]
  },
  'cardiac-conditions': {
    source: 'L3 Cardiac Conditions v4 Dec 2025.ppsx',
    intro: 'This topic covers common cardiac conditions and the priorities for early recognition and management. It links coronary artery disease and atherosclerosis with stable angina, heart failure and acute coronary syndrome, then introduces sickle cell crisis as a high-risk presentation that can include cardiac and respiratory complications. Use a structured DR ABCDE assessment, obtain an ECG early when indicated, follow local clinical guidance and escalate time-critical concerns without delay.',
    sections: [
      ['01', 'Topic overview', 'The supplied lesson covers coronary artery disease, acute coronary syndromes, stable angina, heart failure and sickle cell. These conditions can present with chest discomfort, breathlessness, fatigue, collapse, sweating, pallor or reduced exercise tolerance. Some patients have atypical symptoms, so assess the whole clinical picture rather than relying on chest pain alone.'],
      ['02', 'Coronary artery disease', 'Coronary artery disease develops when the arteries supplying the myocardium become narrowed or blocked. Reduced coronary blood flow limits oxygen delivery to cardiac muscle. The result may be myocardial ischaemia during increased demand, or infarction when blood flow is completely obstructed.'],
      ['03', 'Risk factors that cannot be changed', 'The deck identifies heredity and family history, sex-related risk, increasing age, diabetes mellitus, hypertension and high LDL cholesterol as important coronary artery disease risk factors. Risk factors often occur together, so take a broad history rather than treating any single factor as decisive.'],
      ['04', 'Modifiable risk factors', 'Modifiable factors in the lesson include obesity, a diet high in refined carbohydrates or saturated fat, smoking, excess emotional stress, a sedentary lifestyle and excess alcohol consumption. These factors increase long-term cardiovascular risk and should be recorded as part of the patient history where relevant.'],
      ['05', 'Atherosclerosis', 'Atherosclerosis involves the build-up of plaque within the arterial wall. Cholesterol becomes lodged in the tunica media and white blood cells surround it, forming a fibrous cap. Progressive plaque formation narrows the vessel. If plaque ruptures, material can travel and obstruct another vessel.'],
      ['06', 'Ischaemia and infarction', 'A narrowed coronary artery reduces blood flow and produces ischaemia. A complete obstruction stops flow and causes infarction, meaning tissue death. Cardiac muscle has limited ability to regenerate, so the duration and severity of reduced blood flow matter.'],
      ['07', 'Ischaemic chest pain', 'Ischaemic pain results from reduced blood supply to the myocardium, often because coronary arteries have narrowed through coronary artery disease. The deck describes pain that is absent at rest and provoked by exertion or emotional stress. Always consider the wider assessment and do not use one feature alone to exclude acute disease.'],
      ['08', 'Stable angina', 'Stable angina, or angina pectoris, may feel like chest discomfort, pain, tightness or indigestion. It can radiate to the back, epigastrium, arms, neck or jaw. Belching may occur. Symptoms are typically provoked by exertion or emotional stress and relieved by rest or prescribed nitrate medication.'],
      ['09', 'Stable angina management', 'Use DR ABCDE and place the patient at rest. Reduce the workload of the heart by helping the patient avoid unnecessary walking or movement. Provide analgesia such as Entonox when appropriate and authorised, obtain an ECG and continue to reassess symptoms, observations and response to treatment.'],
      ['10', 'Heart failure: definition', 'Heart failure is an abnormality of cardiac structure or function in which the heart cannot deliver oxygen at the rate the body requires. It may be acute or chronic. The clinical presentation depends on which part of the heart is affected and how severely cardiac output has fallen.'],
      ['11', 'Right, left and combined failure', 'Heart failure may affect the right side, the left side or both sides. Reduced cardiac output means that less blood is pushed around the body each minute. Left-sided failure commonly creates pulmonary congestion and breathlessness, while right-sided failure can produce systemic venous congestion. Both sides may be involved.'],
      ['12', 'Cardiac output', 'Cardiac output is the amount of blood pumped around the body per minute. The deck gives the relationship: cardiac output equals stroke volume multiplied by heart rate. A fall in stroke volume may reduce perfusion even when the heart rate increases, so interpret pulse, blood pressure, mental state, skin signs and symptoms together.'],
      ['13', 'Preload, contractility and afterload', 'Preload is the volume of blood stretching the relaxed heart muscle at the end of diastole. Contractility is the force of myocardial contraction. Afterload is the pressure the left ventricle must overcome to eject blood around the body. These factors influence stroke volume and therefore cardiac output.'],
      ['14', 'Heart failure symptoms', 'Symptoms vary with the extent and type of failure, but the lesson highlights fatigue including exercise intolerance, dyspnoea and congestion. Breathlessness may be worse when the patient lies flat. Ask about onset, progression, exercise tolerance, position, swelling and associated chest discomfort.'],
      ['15', 'Congestion and breathlessness', 'Congestion can impair gas exchange and increase the work of breathing. A patient who is breathless when lying down may adopt an upright position to ease symptoms. Look for respiratory distress, abnormal respiratory rate, reduced oxygen saturation, poor perfusion and signs of worsening failure, then escalate promptly.'],
      ['16', 'Heart failure management', 'Use DR ABCDE, place the patient at rest and obtain a full set of observations. Give oxygen when required to maintain oxygen saturation above 94%, following local guidance and the patient’s clinical needs. Obtain an ECG and reassess the response to positioning, oxygen and other authorised interventions.'],
      ['17', 'Acute coronary syndrome', 'Acute coronary syndrome is a group of conditions caused by acute reduction of coronary blood flow. The deck includes unstable angina, non-ST-segment-elevation myocardial infarction and ST-segment-elevation myocardial infarction. Treat suspected ACS as time-critical because ongoing ischaemia can cause permanent myocardial damage.'],
      ['18', 'Unstable angina and myocardial infarction', 'Unstable angina and myocardial infarction sit within the acute coronary syndrome spectrum. A blocked coronary branch reduces blood flow to cardiac tissue, producing ischaemia and potentially infarction. Effects and complications are greatest when the left ventricle is involved. The clinical picture and ECG help guide escalation and destination.'],
      ['19', 'Common ACS symptoms', 'Common symptoms include heavy, squeezing, crushing or tight chest pain. Pain may radiate to the left or right arm, chest, jaw, neck or upper back. Nausea, vomiting, marked sweating, shortness of breath, pallor, a cold clammy skin and a feeling of impending doom may occur. These symptoms are not present in every patient.'],
      ['20', 'Atypical ACS presentations', 'Atypical presentations are not uncommon. The deck notes that up to a third of patients having a myocardial infarction may present with shortness of breath or collapse without chest pain. Atypical presentations are particularly important in younger adults, older adults, people who are unwell with diabetes and female patients.'],
      ['21', 'ACS immediate management', 'Use DR ABCDE and correct major airway, breathing or circulation problems. Obtain a 12-lead ECG as soon as possible. Give oxygen when saturation is below 94% on air, follow local practice guidelines for authorised medication, provide reassurance and arrange rapid transport to the appropriate hospital. Continue reassessment while preparing to move.'],
      ['22', 'ECG and handover', 'An early 12-lead ECG supports recognition of acute coronary syndrome and helps guide the receiving pathway. Record the time of symptoms, the patient’s observations, ECG findings, treatments given, response to treatment and any change in condition. Communicate concern for ACS clearly and avoid delaying transport for non-essential tasks.'],
      ['23', 'Sickle cell and cardiovascular risk', 'The deck includes sickle cell as a cardiac-conditions topic and highlights that a crisis can include chest pain, breathlessness, reduced oxygen saturation and other signs of serious illness. A patient’s own care plan and specialist pathway are important. Treat the immediate presentation while considering hypoxia, infection, dehydration, acute chest complications and acute coronary syndrome.'],
      ['24', 'Sickle cell symptoms beyond pain', 'Symptoms listed in the lesson include high temperature, difficulty breathing, reduced oxygen saturation, cough, chest pain, pallor, tiredness or weakness, dehydration, headache and priapism. These findings should not be dismissed as ordinary pain alone. Reassess the patient, repeat observations and escalate respiratory or neurological concerns.'],
      ['25', 'Sickle cell crisis triggers', 'The deck identifies factors that may precipitate sickling, including dehydration, hypoxia, cold, infection, pregnancy, anaesthesia, high altitude, post-operative states, mental stress and hard exercise. It also lists constrictive clothing, smoking, alcohol, vaso-constrictive drugs, exercise and strong emotion. Ask about recent illness, exertion, fluid intake, exposure and the patient’s usual plan.'],
      ['26', 'Sickle cell crisis management', 'Follow the patient’s own plan when available and provide authorised pain relief, including Entonox or other treatment within local guidance. Repeat observations and the pain score, document changes, obtain a 12-lead ECG because of the risk of acute coronary syndrome, and consider fluid replacement according to current guidance. Move the patient carefully from chair to ambulance to avoid worsening hypoxia.'],
      ['27', 'Transport and escalation', 'The deck advises conveying a patient in sickle cell crisis to their own specialist unit unless a higher-priority pathway takes precedence, such as a HASU, major trauma centre or PCI pathway, or there is an airway or breathing problem. Escalate early when oxygenation is reduced, the patient is deteriorating or the appropriate destination is uncertain.'],
      ['28', 'Key learning points', 'Coronary artery disease reduces coronary blood flow and may progress from ischaemia to infarction. Stable angina is usually provoked by exertion and relieved by rest or prescribed nitrates. Heart failure reduces effective cardiac output and commonly causes fatigue, dyspnoea and congestion. ACS may present atypically, so obtain an early ECG and arrange rapid transport. In sickle cell crisis, follow the patient’s plan, treat pain and hypoxia, reassess frequently and use the correct specialist pathway.']
    ]
  },
  'respiratory-conditions': {
    source: 'Respiratory Conditions AAP Jul25 v7.ppsx',
    intro: 'Respiratory conditions can deteriorate quickly, and the first clinician may need to identify the immediate threat before the exact diagnosis is known. Use a primary survey, a focused respiratory examination, a complete history and trend-based observations. This topic covers breathlessness, chest infection and pneumonia, pulmonary embolism, COPD, asthma, peak flow and hyperventilation. Follow JRCALC and local guidance for oxygen, medicines and escalation.',
    sections: [
      ['01', 'Respiratory assessment framework', 'Start with Danger, Response and the ABCD primary survey. Decide whether the patient has a time-critical problem, then complete a secondary survey with history, examination and observations. Work stepwise, reassess frequently and escalate early when the patient is deteriorating or outside your scope of practice.'],
      ['02', 'History and observations', 'Use SAMPLE and SOCRATES where appropriate, and ask what happened, when symptoms began, how they have changed and what the patient normally experiences. Focus observations on respiratory rate, rhythm and depth, oxygen saturation, ETCO2 where available, heart rate, blood pressure, temperature, ECG, pain and level of consciousness.'],
      ['03', 'Inspecting the chest', 'With consent and a chaperone where appropriate, look at skin colour, rashes, bruising, wounds, chest shape and equal rise and fall. Be mindful that cyanosis and other colour changes may be less obvious in brown or black skin. Look for work of breathing, accessory-muscle use, exhaustion and posture.'],
      ['04', 'Palpating and auscultating', 'Palpate the bony landmarks and the whole chest for tenderness, injury and subcutaneous emphysema. Feel for equal chest movement. Auscultate with the diaphragm pressed firmly, ask the patient to open their mouth, compare left with right and listen during inspiration and expiration for reduced air entry or added sounds.'],
      ['05', 'Classifying breathlessness', 'Classify breathlessness by speed of onset: acute develops suddenly or within minutes, subacute over hours or days, and chronic over weeks or months. The classification helps guide the history and differential diagnosis, but the underlying cause may require hospital investigation.'],
      ['06', 'Chest infection', 'Chest infection affects the lungs or airways. The main types highlighted in the deck are bronchitis and pneumonia. Bronchitis is commonly viral, while pneumonia is commonly bacterial. Infection can spread through coughs and sneezes or by touching contaminated surfaces.'],
      ['07', 'Risk groups for chest infection', 'Higher-risk groups include smokers, people with asthma, heart disease, diabetes, kidney disease, cystic fibrosis or COPD, and people with weakened immunity. Babies, young children, older adults, pregnant people and those who are very overweight may also be at increased risk of complications.'],
      ['08', 'Chest infection presentation', 'Possible features include persistent cough, yellow or green sputum, haemoptysis, breathlessness, rapid shallow breathing, wheeze, fever, tachycardia, chest pain or tightness and confusion. Assess the whole patient and do not assume that one symptom identifies the cause.'],
      ['09', 'Pneumonia and consolidation', 'In pneumonia, inhaled bacterial or viral agents produce an inflammatory response. If lung defences cannot overcome the infection, fluid and blood cells build up in the affected area; this is called consolidation. Fever, productive cough, tachypnoea, tachycardia, chest or muscle pain and crackles or bronchial breath sounds may occur.'],
      ['10', 'Pneumonia management', 'Use ABCD, assess for tachypnoea such as a respiratory rate of 30 or more or below 10, and look for tachycardia, hypoxia and other red flags. Position the patient for comfort, consider oxygen in line with JRCALC, assess pain and use authorised analgesia where appropriate. Monitor trends, obtain a 12-lead ECG when cardiac chest pain is possible and pre-alert when time-critical features are present.'],
      ['11', 'Pulmonary embolism', 'A pulmonary embolism is an obstruction of pulmonary vessels. Consider it in unexplained breathlessness, tachypnoea, pleuritic chest pain, tachycardia, haemoptysis, syncope or apprehension, particularly when risk factors are present. A thorough history, examination and observations help distinguish it from panic, pneumothorax, pleurisy and cardiac chest pain.'],
      ['12', 'Pulmonary embolism risk factors', 'Risk factors in the deck include trauma, recent surgery, spinal-cord injury, chronic cardiac or respiratory disease, previous PE, DVT or stroke, neurological disease, pregnancy or the post-partum period, hormone replacement or oral contraceptive use, smoking, obesity, intravenous catheters and immobility. Ask targeted questions rather than relying on one risk factor.'],
      ['13', 'Signs suggesting PE or DVT', 'Look for respiratory distress, an increased respiratory rate and unexplained tachycardia. Examine the lower limbs for unilateral swelling, warmth, a change in skin colour and calf or femoral tenderness. These findings support the differential but do not confirm PE, and their absence does not safely exclude it.'],
      ['14', 'Time-critical PE features', 'Time-critical features include major ABCDE problems, extreme breathing difficulty, cyanosis and severe hypoxia, including oxygen saturation below 90% that does not respond to oxygen. Pre-alert when these features are present, monitor continuously and be prepared for rapid deterioration.'],
      ['15', 'Suspected PE management', 'Maintain the ABCDs, provide oxygen according to JRCALC, position for comfort and ease of respiration, and obtain baseline observations with continuous monitoring. Complete a tailored history, use SOCRATES for pain, record a pain score and ask about PE symptoms and risk factors. Escalate and pre-alert for time-critical features.'],
      ['16', 'COPD overview', 'COPD is a chronic progressive disorder characterised by airflow obstruction. The deck uses COPD as the preferred term covering chronic bronchitis, emphysema and chronic obstructive airway disease. A clinical suspicion is important in people over 35 with a risk factor and persistent or progressive breathlessness, chronic cough, regular sputum, recurrent lower respiratory infections or wheeze.'],
      ['17', 'Chronic bronchitis', 'Chronic bronchitis is described as cough and sputum production for at least three months in each of two consecutive years. It involves long-term airway inflammation and lung damage. Acute bronchitis is usually a short-lived viral or bacterial infection. Distinguish the patient’s baseline symptoms from an acute deterioration.'],
      ['18', 'Emphysema', 'Emphysema involves distension and destruction of alveoli. As lung tissue loses elasticity and the airways lose muscular integrity, the airways can collapse and trap air. A barrel-shaped chest may occur. Pursed-lip expiration, a thin appearance, wheeze, tachypnoea, accessory-muscle use, anxiety, confusion, arrhythmia or oedema may be present.'],
      ['19', 'COPD assessment and red flags', 'Assess the full history, respiratory examination, baseline observations and the patient’s usual oxygenation. Time-critical features include extreme breathing difficulty, cyanosis, exhaustion and oxygen saturation below 88% that is unresponsive to oxygen. Be mindful of the patient’s personal baseline and escalate for senior clinical support when required.'],
      ['20', 'COPD management', 'Position the patient for comfort, complete baseline observations and monitor continuously. Follow the JRCALC flowchart and ask about an individual care plan. Oxygen-driven nebulisation should be limited to six minutes; if the journey is significant, a further six minutes should only be considered when clinically indicated. The deck gives a target saturation range of 88–92% for relevant COPD patients.'],
      ['21', 'Asthma and triggers', 'Asthma is an inflammatory airway condition in which bronchial muscle contracts and mucus production can increase obstruction. Symptoms include breathlessness, cough, wheeze and chest tightness. Common triggers include respiratory infection, allergy, cold weather, exertion, dust mites, pets, cigarette smoke, mould, pollen, stress and obesity.'],
      ['22', 'Asthma history and examination', 'Ask about increasing wheeze or breathlessness, night or early-morning symptoms, previous emergency attendances, intensive-care admission, medication adherence and annual reviews. Ask how the patient normally responds and whether an allergy trigger is possible. Focus on respiratory assessment, respiratory rate, oxygen saturation, peak flow and ETCO2 where available.'],
      ['23', 'Peak expiratory flow rate', 'Peak flow is a rapid measurement of the degree of airway obstruction during forced exhalation. Check the pointer is at zero, position the patient comfortably upright, hold the meter horizontally, take a deep breath, seal the lips around the mouthpiece and blow as hard as possible. Reset and repeat three times, record the highest reading and document it on the PRF.'],
      ['24', 'When to use peak flow', 'Obtain peak flow before and after nebulised treatment where possible, especially in mild to moderate asthma, to help assess response. Use a single-use mouthpiece and check the meter expiry. Take care in severe or life-threatening asthma, and do not use PEFR in pre-school children because they cannot reliably perform the technique. If it is not obtained, document why, such as severe breathlessness.'],
      ['25', 'Asthma severity', 'The deck describes mild, moderate, acute severe and life-threatening asthma. Severe features include inability to complete sentences, raised pulse or respiratory rate and PEFR 33–50% of best or predicted. Life-threatening features include altered consciousness, exhaustion, cyanosis, a silent chest, poor respiratory effort, PEFR below 33%, SpO2 below 92%, arrhythmia or hypotension.'],
      ['26', 'Severe and life-threatening asthma', 'A silent chest is a pre-terminal sign and severe asthma is time-critical. After establishing ABCDs, complete targeted assessment and initiate immediate interventions within scope. Aim to complete assessment, begin treatment and reach a triage decision within 10 minutes, while keeping on-scene time under 30 minutes where possible.'],
      ['27', 'Asthma interventions', 'Follow JRCALC and local scope for oxygen, salbutamol, ipratropium bromide, IM adrenaline 1:1000 and hydrocortisone. Use the drug administration cross-check for every medicine, record peak flow before and after treatment where possible, document symptom progression and escalate to a senior clinician for interventions such as SLB or IPR when indicated.'],
      ['28', 'Hyperventilation', 'Hyperventilation is characterised by an irregular breathing pattern and depth and may be caused by life-threatening conditions including myocardial infarction, PE, diabetic ketoacidosis, asthma or sepsis. In children it is more likely to be caused by an underlying medical condition than anxiety. Look for the cause before labelling it as a panic or hyperventilation syndrome.'],
      ['29', 'Managing hyperventilation', 'Assess for an underlying cause and correct hypoxaemia; the deck identifies oxygen therapy when SpO2 is below 94%. Reassure the patient and coach their breathing. Continue to monitor the ABCDs and observations, because the breathing pattern may be a sign of serious disease rather than the primary diagnosis.'],
      ['30', 'Key learning points', 'Use the primary survey first, then complete a focused respiratory history, examination and observations. Recognise pneumonia, PE, COPD and asthma patterns, but keep dangerous differentials in mind. Treat oxygenation and life threats within JRCALC and scope, monitor continuously, use peak flow appropriately, document clearly and escalate early when the patient is time-critical or deteriorating.']
    ]
  },
  'abdominal-injuries': {
    source: 'L3 Abdominal Injuries v3 Feb2024.pdf',
    intro: 'Abdominal trauma can be difficult to assess because the abdomen provides little protection for its organs and serious injury may have few early signs. The immediate priority is to recognise that abdominal trauma has occurred, identify time-critical problems and manage the patient within scope. Use the mechanism of injury, C-ABCDE assessment, exposure, reassessment and appropriate escalation to guide care rather than trying to name the injured structure prehospitally.',
    sections: [
      ['01', 'Why abdominal trauma is difficult', 'The abdomen offers little protection to internal organs and structures. Serious injury may initially produce few obvious signs. Recognising that abdominal trauma has occurred is more important than identifying the exact organ involved, because definitive diagnosis may require hospital investigation.'],
      ['02', 'Anatomical areas', 'The deck divides the abdomen into the abdominal cavity, the pelvis and the retroperitoneal area. Injury in any of these areas can cause internal bleeding or damage to organs, vessels and other structures. Assess the whole mechanism and patient rather than focusing only on visible abdominal marks.'],
      ['03', 'The retroperitoneal area', 'The retroperitoneal area lies against the posterior abdominal wall. It contains the kidneys, ureters, pancreas, abdominal aorta, vena cava and part of the duodenum. These structures are attached to the posterior wall and may be injured by shearing during rapid-force mechanisms.'],
      ['04', 'Types of abdominal injury', 'The two broad types are blunt and penetrating injury. Blunt trauma commonly causes a closed abdominal injury, while penetrating trauma causes an open abdominal injury. Both may produce internal bleeding, organ damage, vascular injury and associated injuries outside the abdomen.'],
      ['05', 'Blunt abdominal trauma', 'Blunt injury is the most common pattern of abdominal injury in the deck. It may result from a direct blow, rapid deceleration or a blast shockwave. The external skin may be intact even when internal organs or blood vessels have been seriously damaged.'],
      ['06', 'Structures injured in blunt trauma', 'Structures highlighted as vulnerable include the spleen, liver, small bowel, duodenum and aorta. Tethered structures are commonly injured. Consider associated diaphragmatic injury, fractured ribs and pregnancy when assessing the mechanism and patient.'],
      ['07', 'Causes of blunt injury', 'Blunt abdominal trauma may follow an assault without a weapon, an assault involving a weapon, a fall, a road traffic collision or a blast shockwave. Ask how the force was applied, where it landed, how fast the event occurred and whether restraint or vehicle damage changed the energy transfer.'],
      ['08', 'Blunt-trauma complications', 'Potential complications include internal bleeding, ruptured organs such as the spleen, diaphragmatic injury, vascular injury such as aortic damage and fractured ribs. Pregnancy must also be considered. The deck emphasises that clinicians cannot diagnose the exact injury by appearance alone.'],
      ['09', 'Penetrating abdominal trauma', 'Penetrating injury creates an open abdominal injury and may damage adjacent structures, including the chest. The deck identifies a higher risk of death than with blunt trauma. Treat the mechanism as significant even when the external wound appears small.'],
      ['10', 'Structures injured in penetrating trauma', 'Penetrating abdominal trauma may injure the spleen, liver, small bowel, duodenum or aorta. The track may also involve the lungs or heart if it extends into the chest. Consider genital injury and pregnancy where relevant, and look for both entry and exit wounds.'],
      ['11', 'Causes of penetrating trauma', 'Causes include assault with weapons, road traffic collisions, blast material or shrapnel, gunshot wounds and evisceration injuries. Scene safety is essential, and the mechanism should be described clearly to help the receiving team anticipate associated injury.'],
      ['12', 'Impaled objects', 'Do not remove a penetrating object that remains in situ. Secure it before transport, but allow movement if the object is pulsating, because movement may be transmitted through a vessel or other structure. Manage the primary survey and request appropriate senior or specialist support early.'],
      ['13', 'Evisceration', 'Evisceration is the protrusion of abdominal organs outside the body. It is a time-critical open abdominal injury that may be associated with contamination, bleeding, shock and damage to underlying structures. Do not attempt to replace the protruding organs into the abdominal cavity.'],
      ['14', 'Evisceration dressing', 'Apply an appropriate dressing such as a blast dressing. Place the plastic sheet against the protruding organ, apply the dressing to the wound and secure it with a conforming bandage. Do not wet the dressing because a wet dressing can become cold and contribute to heat loss.'],
      ['15', 'Scene safety', 'Personal safety comes first. The deck specifically warns that the scene may be dangerous and states that police should declare it safe. Do not enter or remain in an unsafe environment to assess an abdominal injury. Reassess hazards as the incident develops.'],
      ['16', 'Mechanism of injury', 'The mechanism of injury provides an important guide to the likelihood of significant damage. For a stabbing, consider the length of the object, angle of insertion and whether it entered the thoracic cavity. For a gunshot, consider firearm type and range, distance from the victim and entry or exit wounds.'],
      ['17', 'Road traffic collision mechanism', 'For an RTC, consider impact speed, seat-belt use and vehicle damage. These features help indicate the forces transferred to the patient and the areas that may have been injured. Use the mechanism to guide examination and escalation, not to rule out injury when the vehicle appears relatively undamaged.'],
      ['18', 'Initial C-ABCDE assessment', 'Assess C-ABCDE and look for catastrophic haemorrhage, airway problems, breathing problems and circulation problems. Identify whether the patient requires interventions from an APP, HEMS or paramedic, and request additional support as soon as it is needed.'],
      ['19', 'Time-critical assessment', 'Decide whether the patient is time critical and refer to the Major Trauma Tool. Assess disability, expose and examine the abdomen, and look for bruising, swelling, lacerations, evisceration and impaled objects. Continue to reassess because early signs may be subtle.'],
      ['20', 'Exposure and examination', 'Expose the abdomen sufficiently to examine it while protecting dignity and preventing heat loss. Look for bruising, swelling, lacerations, protruding organs and impaled objects. Do not let an obvious abdominal injury distract from airway, breathing, circulation or other associated injuries.'],
      ['21', 'Secondary survey', 'Complete the secondary survey en route when the patient is time critical. Perform a top-to-toe assessment for other injuries, paying particular attention to the chest, spine and pelvis. Obtain a pain score and review the mechanism thoroughly.'],
      ['22', 'Associated injuries', 'Abdominal trauma may coexist with chest, spinal and pelvic injury. Penetrating tracks may cross anatomical boundaries, and blunt mechanisms can transfer force widely. Examine associated areas, compare the mechanism with the clinical findings and escalate concerns that exceed the initial abdominal presentation.'],
      ['23', 'Oxygen and analgesia', 'Administer oxygen according to JRCALC Plus guidance when indicated. Provide analgesia as required within scope; the deck states that AAP clinicians can give Entonox. Reassess pain, respiratory status, circulation and mental state after interventions.'],
      ['24', 'Monitoring and transport', 'Monitor vital signs and obtain an ECG when required. Apply relevant dressings, monitor trends rather than relying on a single observation set and transport to the appropriate hospital. Pre-alert or escalate early when the patient is time critical or deteriorating.'],
      ['25', 'Key learning points', 'Treat abdominal trauma as potentially serious even when external signs are limited. Use the mechanism to guide suspicion, do not remove penetrating objects, protect eviscerated organs without replacing them, complete C-ABCDE, assess associated injuries, involve senior clinicians early, follow JRCALC and transport to the appropriate definitive care.']
    ]
  },
  'chemotherapy-radiotherapy': {
    source: 'L3  - Chemotherapy and Radiotherapy v1 September 2020.pdf',
    intro: 'Cancer treatment can affect both malignant and normal cells, leaving patients vulnerable to fatigue, gastrointestinal symptoms, skin and mucosal problems, infection and neutropaenic sepsis. This topic introduces common cancer types, chemotherapy, radiotherapy, treatment side effects and prehospital priorities. Use a primary survey, identify recent treatment, consider neutropaenic sepsis in any unwell patient who has received chemotherapy within the last six weeks and follow current local sepsis guidance.',
    sections: [
      ['01', 'Cancer overview', 'The deck states that there are many different types of cancer and introduces common groups by the tissue or cells involved. A patient’s cancer type, treatment, recent therapy and baseline health can all affect the emergency presentation.'],
      ['02', 'Carcinomas', 'Carcinomas arise from skin or tissues that line organs. The location of the primary tumour, spread of disease and treatment can affect symptoms and complications. Ask the patient or care team about the known cancer and current treatment.'],
      ['03', 'Lymphomas', 'Lymphomas arise from cells of the immune system. Patients may be receiving chemotherapy, radiotherapy or other treatments that alter immune function. Consider infection risk and treatment-related complications when an unwell patient presents.'],
      ['04', 'Leukaemia', 'Leukaemia affects blood-forming tissues and blood cells. Disease and treatment may impair immune defence and contribute to infection, fatigue, bleeding or other systemic complications. Review the patient’s oncology history and current symptoms.'],
      ['05', 'Brain tumours', 'Brain tumours affect the central nervous system. Presentations may relate to neurological function, raised intracranial pressure, seizures, treatment effects or other complications. Assess the primary survey and neurological status, and escalate deterioration promptly.'],
      ['06', 'Sarcomas', 'Sarcomas arise from connective or supportive tissues such as bone, cartilage, fat and muscle. Consider pain, swelling, pathological fracture, reduced mobility and treatment-related complications in the wider assessment.'],
      ['07', 'Chemotherapy', 'Chemotherapy is used to treat and kill cancer cells or stop them spreading further through the body. It may be used alone or combined with radiotherapy. The timing and type of chemotherapy are important when assessing an acutely unwell patient.'],
      ['08', 'Chemotherapy and normal cells', 'Chemotherapy can affect normal cells as well as cancer cells. This contributes to side effects and can reduce the patient’s ability to fight infection. Ask what treatment was given, when it was given and whether the patient has an individual oncology or emergency plan.'],
      ['09', 'Chemotherapy side effects', 'The deck lists tiredness, nausea or vomiting, hair loss, increased infection risk, sore mouth, dry or itchy skin, diarrhoea and constipation. These may be treatment effects, but new or severe symptoms still require assessment for infection, dehydration, obstruction or another emergency.'],
      ['10', 'Treatment-related infection risk', 'A lower immune response after cancer treatment increases infection risk. Do not dismiss fever, rigors, confusion, reduced consciousness, breathlessness, hypotension or general deterioration as routine treatment effects. Consider sepsis and follow the current screening pathway.'],
      ['11', 'Neutropaenic sepsis', 'Neutropaenic sepsis is a potentially fatal complication of cancer treatment such as chemotherapy. It can progress quickly because the patient’s ability to fight infection is reduced. The deck states that it should always be suspected in an unwell patient who has received chemotherapy within the last six weeks.'],
      ['12', 'Recent chemotherapy history', 'Ask whether chemotherapy has been received within the last six weeks, when the last treatment occurred and whether the patient has been told they are neutropaenic. Record the oncology unit, treatment plan, usual baseline and any written emergency information available.'],
      ['13', 'Neutropaenic sepsis assessment', 'Use the LAS sepsis screening tool and NEWS2 as directed by current local guidance. Complete a primary survey, assess the patient’s appearance and observations, look for an infection source and treat the presentation as time critical when the patient is deteriorating.'],
      ['14', 'Hospital communication', 'Inform the receiving hospital when neutropaenic sepsis is suspected. The deck notes that the patient may need to be placed in isolation. Provide a clear pre-alert with recent chemotherapy, suspected infection, observations, NEWS2 where applicable, interventions and response.'],
      ['15', 'Radiotherapy', 'Radiotherapy uses radiation, usually X-rays, to damage the DNA of cancer cells. It can also affect normal cells in the treatment area. Radiotherapy may be used at some stage during cancer treatment and can be combined with chemotherapy.'],
      ['16', 'Radiotherapy side effects', 'The deck highlights tiredness and skin changes, including skin that is sore, red or darker than normal. Ask which body area has been treated, when treatment occurred and whether the skin change is expected or worsening.'],
      ['17', 'Skin and mucosal symptoms', 'Chemotherapy may cause dry or itchy skin and a sore mouth, while radiotherapy may produce sore, red or darker skin in the treated area. Assess severity, hydration, pain, infection signs and the patient’s ability to eat, drink and manage symptoms.'],
      ['18', 'Gastrointestinal symptoms', 'Nausea, vomiting, diarrhoea and constipation can follow chemotherapy. Assess onset, severity, fluid intake, urine output, abdominal pain, distension, blood loss and medication. Look for dehydration, electrolyte disturbance, infection or obstruction rather than assuming symptoms are uncomplicated side effects.'],
      ['19', 'General management', 'Manage symptoms within scope, monitor observations and consider whether the patient needs conveyance. Contact or communicate with the patient’s care team where appropriate. Escalate immediately when there are airway, breathing or circulation problems, altered consciousness, severe pain, dehydration or suspected sepsis.'],
      ['20', 'Key learning points', 'Know the patient’s cancer type and recent treatment, recognise common chemotherapy and radiotherapy effects, always consider neutropaenic sepsis after chemotherapy within the last six weeks, use the appropriate sepsis screening and NEWS2 pathway, inform the receiving hospital when indicated and convey for further care when needed.']
    ]
  },
  'medicines-management': {
    source: 'AAP Meds Mng Jun25 v4.ppsx',
    intro: 'Safe medicines management depends on legislation, agreed ways of working, individual accountability, accurate checks and use of current JRCALC guidance. This topic introduces pharmacology, medication classes, routes, indications, contraindications, cautions, side effects, paediatric considerations, equipment and documentation. Apply the DACC process and work within role, competence, scope and local policy every time.',
    sections: [
      ['01', 'Learning scope', 'The deck covers current legislation and agreed ways of working, the clinician\'s role in assisting with medication administration, medication labels and equipment, patient-safety procedures, medication classes and routes. It also emphasises checking JRCALC Plus every time and using DACC as mandatory practice.'],
      ['02', 'What is pharmacology?', 'Pharmacology is the branch of science that studies drugs and their effects on the body. It helps clinicians understand why a medicine is used, what effect it should produce, how it may be given and what unwanted effects or safety risks need to be considered.'],
      ['03', 'Pharmacodynamics', 'Pharmacodynamics describes what the drug does to the body. It includes the medicine\'s mechanism of action and its effects on cells and tissues. Understanding the intended action helps the clinician recognise whether the medicine is appropriate and whether the patient is responding.'],
      ['04', 'Pharmacokinetics', 'Pharmacokinetics describes what the body does to the drug. The deck presents the process as absorption, distribution, metabolism and excretion, often abbreviated as ADME. These processes influence how quickly a medicine acts and how long its effects may last.'],
      ['05', 'What is a medicine?', 'A medicine is a substance which, when taken into or applied to the body, has an effect on the composition or function of the body or on microbes within it. Medicines may relieve symptoms, cure infection, prevent illness, manage chronic conditions or support body functions.'],
      ['06', 'Benefits of medicines', 'The deck identifies benefits including relieving symptoms, curing infections, preventing illness, managing chronic conditions, slowing disease progression, improving quality of life and supporting body functions. The benefit must be balanced against indications, contraindications, cautions and possible side effects.'],
      ['07', 'Brand and generic names', 'A medicine may have a brand name and a generic name. The deck uses Panadol and paracetamol, and Ventolin and salbutamol, as examples. Confirm the actual medicine and formulation rather than relying only on a familiar brand name.'],
      ['08', 'Medication classifications', 'The deck introduces general sales list medicines (GSL), pharmacy-only medicines (P) and prescription-only medicines (POM). The classification affects how a medicine can be supplied, prescribed or administered and must be understood alongside current legislation and agreed ways of working.'],
      ['09', 'Parenteral routes', 'Parenteral routes involve a physical breach of the skin or mucous membrane. Examples in the deck include injection, intravenous and subcutaneous administration. These routes require appropriate equipment, training, infection control and adherence to the authorised scope of practice.'],
      ['10', 'Non-parenteral routes', 'Non-parenteral routes are those where the drug is absorbed passively. The deck gives the gastrointestinal tract, mucous membranes and skin as examples. Confirm the authorised route and the correct presentation before administration.'],
      ['11', 'Agonists and antagonists', 'An agonist activates a receptor to produce a response, while an antagonist blocks a receptor to stop a response. Drugs usually act by attaching to specific proteins called receptors. The distinction helps explain mechanism of action and expected effects.'],
      ['12', 'Legislation and guidance', 'The deck lists the Medicines Act 1968, the Misuse of Drugs Act 1971, the Health and Safety at Work Act 1974, COSHH 2002, JRCALC Ambulance Services Clinical Practice Guidelines, the Prescription Only Medicines Order 1997 and the Human Medicines Regulations 2012. Follow the current version of relevant law, policy and clinical guidance.'],
      ['13', 'MHRA', 'The Medicines and Healthcare products Regulatory Agency (MHRA) is described as the UK government agency responsible for protecting and improving public health by ensuring the safety, quality and efficacy of medicines and medical devices. Use safety information and alerts through approved organisational channels.'],
      ['14', 'Human Medicines Regulations', 'The Human Medicines Regulations 2012 establish the framework for licensing and regulating medicines and define circumstances in which certain healthcare professionals may supply or administer medicines without a prescription. The exact legal authority and schedule must be checked rather than assumed.'],
      ['15', 'Emergency administration schedules', 'The deck describes Schedule 19 as covering administration of certain drugs in an emergency and identifies that it can incorporate EMT staff, with restrictions including no IV administration. Schedule 17 is described as applying to registered paramedics for immediate necessary treatment. Always work within current law, role and local policy.'],
      ['16', 'Role and accountability', 'You are responsible for assisting with medication administration in line with agreed ways of working. Practice only within your competence, authorisation and scope. If the situation requires a medicine, route or decision outside your role, request the appropriate senior clinician and document the escalation.'],
      ['17', 'Agreed ways of working', 'Agreed ways of working include how medicines are stored, stocked, restocked and disposed of. Follow local policies for security, controlled drugs, expiry checks, clinical waste, sharps and environmental safety. Do not improvise a process because a medicine is familiar.'],
      ['18', 'Medication labels', 'Before administration, understand the information on the label, including the medicine purpose and its significance for the patient. Confirm the label against the clinical indication, authorised route, formulation, dose and current guidance. Never administer from an unidentified or compromised container.'],
      ['19', 'Equipment and aids', 'Use the equipment required for the authorised route and medication, including appropriate measuring, delivery and safety equipment. The deck also asks clinicians to describe aids that assist individuals to take medication. Choose equipment that supports safe administration and patient understanding.'],
      ['20', 'Identify the right patient', 'Confirm the individual for whom the medicine is prescribed or intended before administration. Use the approved patient-identification process and corroborate the medicine and patient details with the crewmate when required. Do not rely on recognition, room location or a verbal assumption alone.'],
      ['21', 'Safety checks before administration', 'Check patient allergies, the correct drug, correct presentation, correct dosage, intact packaging or ampoule, the appearance of any fluid and the expiry date. The deck summarises this as safety first. Stop and seek advice if any check is uncertain or fails.'],
      ['22', 'Indications', 'Indications are the conditions under which a medicine may be given, regardless of information on the packet or over-the-counter use. Check the indication in current JRCALC guidance and corroborate the clinical presentation before administration.'],
      ['23', 'Contraindications', 'Contraindications are the conditions under which a medicine must not be given. They must be checked and corroborated with the crewmate every time. A medicine that is usually familiar may still be unsafe for a particular patient or presentation.'],
      ['24', 'Cautions', 'Cautions are conditions that might affect administration. A medicine may still be used with care, monitoring or moderation as directed by JRCALC. The deck describes this as a clinical decision, so record the reasoning and seek senior advice when required.'],
      ['25', 'Side effects', 'Side effects are unintended effects of a drug that occur alongside the intended effect. They are not automatically a reason to withhold a medicine. Anticipate known side effects, warn the patient, monitor for deterioration and escalate if the reaction is severe or unexpected.'],
      ['26', 'Paediatric medicines management', 'For children, check the JRCALC Plus page for the correct age-based dose and volume. Explain what is happening and why, use age-appropriate language, get down to the child\'s height, avoid looming, engage them in conversation, involve parents or carers appropriately and consider pain relief where possible.'],
      ['27', 'Paediatric dose information', 'The deck identifies age pages covering birth to 11 years and information such as guide weight, heart rate, respiratory rate, blood pressure, defibrillation joules and medication doses. Dose and fluid information may be expressed in millilitres, micrograms or milligrams, so check units carefully.'],
      ['28', 'DACC', 'DACC is the required cross-check process shown in the deck. Use it with the patient, drug, JRCALC, indications, cautions, dose, contraindications, pain score, route, side effects, repeat dose, maximum dosage, documentation and whether senior clinician input is needed.'],
      ['29', 'Repeat and maximum doses', 'Before giving a repeat dose, check the correct interval and the maximum cumulative dose in current JRCALC guidance. Record what has already been administered, by whom and when. Do not assume that a previous dose can be repeated because symptoms persist.'],
      ['30', 'Documentation and summary', 'Document the medicine, indication, dose, route, time, response, side effects, advice and any escalation or senior-clinician involvement. The key message is to check JRCALC every time, work within agreed ways of working, complete the DACC cross-check and prioritise patient identification and safety before administration.']
    ]
  },
  'airway-management': {
    source: 'AAP airway May2026.ppsx',
    intro: 'Airway management is the process of opening, maintaining and protecting the airway while supporting breathing and oxygenation. This topic follows the supplied airway teaching deck: assess the patient and environment, achieve neutral alignment, use appropriate opening manoeuvres, recognise foreign-body airway obstruction, use suction and basic adjuncts correctly, provide effective bag-valve-mask ventilation, understand the role of supraglottic airways and capnography, and escalate early when the airway cannot be maintained.',
    sections: [
      ['01', 'The airway priorities', 'Every airway must be opened, maintained and protected. An airway adjunct is only part of that strategy: an oropharyngeal or nasopharyngeal tube does not open the airway by itself and does not protect against aspiration. Continue to reassess patency, ventilation and the patient’s response after every intervention.'],
      ['02', 'When airway management is required', 'Clinician airway management is required when a patient cannot maintain their own airway, for example an unconscious and unresponsive patient without a gag reflex or a patient in cardiac arrest. Apply the same essential airway standards to all patients, regardless of clinical grade, history or arrest circumstances, within current scope and guidance.'],
      ['03', 'Scene access and positioning', 'Obtain 360-degree access early where possible. Move the patient or clear the working area if it can be done safely, using handling aids such as a carry sheet. If the patient cannot be moved, provide the best possible basic life support in the available position, call for help early and continue reassessment until additional resources arrive.'],
      ['04', 'Initial airway assessment', 'Look for chest rise and fall, confusion or agitation, pallor or cyanosis, shortness of breath, difficulty breathing, facial oedema, tracheal deviation, bruising and wounds. Listen for airflow and abnormal respiratory sounds, including snoring, gurgling, wheeze, stridor, a silent chest or complete absence of breathing.'],
      ['05', 'Look, listen and feel', 'Use the patient’s appearance, sound and movement together rather than relying on one sign. Snoring may indicate the tongue is occluding the airway; gurgling suggests fluid or vomit; stridor suggests upper-airway narrowing; and no audible airflow may indicate severe obstruction or apnoea. Treat the patient’s clinical condition and reassess promptly.'],
      ['06', 'Neutral alignment', 'Neutral alignment aims to place the face parallel to the surface and the tragus in line with the sternal notch. This position maximises airflow and is the design position for basic adjuncts and supraglottic devices. Recheck the alignment regularly because the patient can move, and use padding where required.'],
      ['07', 'Adults, larger patients and children', 'Adults may need padding beneath the occiput or shoulders to achieve neutral alignment. Larger patients may need additional folded blankets or other suitable padding to ramp the airway and bring the landmarks into line. Babies and younger children have a relatively large occiput, so padding may be needed under the shoulders or upper back instead.'],
      ['08', 'Head tilt and chin lift', 'When cervical-spine injury is not suspected, inspect the airway and gently place one hand on the forehead and the other on the point of the chin. Tilt the head back and lift the chin using the mandible. Do not hyperextend the neck, lift from the soft tissues under the neck or close the mouth; use the bony mandible and reassess airflow.'],
      ['09', 'Jaw thrust', 'Use a jaw thrust when cervical-spine injury is suspected or when a difficult airway requires an alternative opening manoeuvre. Position at the head, place the fingers under the angles of the mandible and displace the jaw forwards/upwards without pressing into the soft tissues of the neck. Maintain the manoeuvre while checking airway patency and breathing.'],
      ['10', 'Airway protection and escalation', 'If you cannot open, maintain or protect the airway, call for help, consider every appropriate intervention, reassess and try changing the crew member performing the manoeuvre. Escalate early and consider moving to hospital when possible if the airway remains occluded or cannot be cleared safely.'],
      ['11', 'Foreign-body airway obstruction', 'Foreign-body airway obstruction (FBAO) often occurs during eating or play and is more common in children. It usually has a sudden onset of respiratory distress. Possible causes include food, teeth, blood, secretions, vomit and objects such as pen lids, coins or toys. Maintain a high index of suspicion when airway compromise begins suddenly.'],
      ['12', 'Mild and severe obstruction', 'In mild obstruction the patient can usually speak, breathe and cough. In severe obstruction the patient cannot speak or breathe and attempts to cough may be silent; the patient may become unconscious. Cyanosis, decreasing consciousness, stridor or wheeze and a witnessed choking episode support the diagnosis but should not delay action when the patient is deteriorating.'],
      ['13', 'Effective cough', 'A spontaneous effective cough is usually safer and more effective than a rescuer’s manoeuvre. Encourage the patient to continue coughing and monitor closely. Intervene when the cough becomes ineffective, silent or absent, or when the patient cannot breathe or speak. Do not leave the patient alone while waiting for the obstruction to resolve.'],
      ['14', 'Back blows and thrusts', 'For an ineffective cough, give up to five back blows between the shoulder blades, checking after each one. If the obstruction remains, give up to five abdominal thrusts in children and adults, checking after each thrust, then repeat the sequence as indicated by the current choking algorithm. Follow the current JRCALC algorithm and local training for exact technique.'],
      ['15', 'Infants with FBAO', 'For infants under one year, use back blows followed by chest thrusts rather than abdominal thrusts. If the infant is small enough, reposition them over the rescuer’s knee with the head lower than the body while maintaining support. Check after each manoeuvre and use the current paediatric algorithm.'],
      ['16', 'Unconscious patient with choking', 'If the patient loses consciousness, start CPR and do not stop to perform a separate breathing-and-circulation assessment. During airway management, use suction and/or McGill forceps when an obstruction is visible and can be removed easily. Do not perform blind finger sweeps because they can push an object deeper.'],
      ['17', 'After an object is removed', 'If the object is expelled, reassess the patient’s clinical condition and continue to monitor the airway and breathing. A fragment may remain in the respiratory tract and cause later complications. Abdominal thrusts can cause internal injury, so patients who have received them require further hospital assessment even if they initially appear well.'],
      ['18', 'Oropharyngeal airway: purpose', 'An oropharyngeal (OP) tube is a rigid, single-patient-use device that helps hold the tongue away from the posterior pharyngeal wall and keeps the teeth apart. It is for a deeply unconscious patient without a gag reflex. It does not protect the airway, so continue monitoring, oxygenation and positioning.'],
      ['19', 'Sizing an OP tube', 'Measure an OP tube from the mid-incisor line to the angle of the jaw. Select by measured size, not colour, because colours are not consistent between manufacturers. Have a size up and a size down available, and keep suction within reach before insertion. A tube that is too short may push the tongue backwards; one that is too long may sit in the hypopharynx and increase regurgitation or ventilation problems.'],
      ['20', 'Inserting an OP tube', 'Inspect the airway and open it using an appropriate manoeuvre. With the patient at the head end, insert the OP tube with the curve towards the roof of the mouth, glide it over the tongue and rotate it through 180 degrees once the tip passes the hard palate. Stop and reassess if there is resistance, coughing or gagging. Do not tape or tie the tube in place.'],
      ['21', 'OP tube complications', 'Do not insert an OP tube into a patient with a gag reflex. It may cause gagging, vomiting, pain, aspiration, raised intracranial pressure or vagal stimulation with bradycardia. If the patient reacts, remove or reassess the device as appropriate, reopen the airway and choose a safer strategy.'],
      ['22', 'Nasopharyngeal airway: purpose', 'A nasopharyngeal (NP) tube is a soft plastic or rubber device that can be used alone or with an OP tube. The flanged end rests against the nose to help maintain position. Use the device and size available under current local policy, replace it if soiled, and do not secure it with tape or ties.'],
      ['23', 'Sizing and preparing an NP tube', 'The deck identifies size 6 as broadly suitable for smaller adults and size 7 for larger adults, with both available as contingency sizes. Prepare the equipment, use a suitable lubricant, maintain sterility until insertion and position the patient in neutral alignment. Do not rely on colour; use the marked size and clinical judgement within guidance.'],
      ['24', 'Inserting an NP tube', 'Inspect the nostril for bleeding or obstruction and start with the right nostril where possible. Lift the tip of the nose gently, orient the tube to follow the hard palate and advance it perpendicular to the patient’s head, with the bevel next to the septum. Gentle rotation may help, but stop if resistance is met. Remove an incompletely advanced tube if its flange prevents an effective BVM seal.'],
      ['25', 'Suction equipment and infection prevention', 'Suction must be within reach when managing an airway. The LSU unit should be checked off its wall mount as part of vehicle checks so the internal battery is assessed; the deck gives an approximate battery run time of 45 minutes and a one-litre maximum capacity, with the bag changed before it is three-quarters full. Catheters, tubing and bags are single use and must be disposed of according to current IPC guidance.'],
      ['26', 'Safe suction technique', 'Use a lateral position where possible, which can also support drainage of vomit or blood. Keep the catheter tip in view and use the principle “only as far as you can see”. Be gentle, especially with a Yankauer catheter. Suction removes oxygen, so do not become fixated on prolonged suction: oxygenate, reassess and escalate if the airway cannot be cleared.'],
      ['27', 'Bag-valve-mask ventilation', 'Use the correct mask size and create a seal by forming a C with the thumb and index finger while the remaining fingers form an E on the mandible. Avoid pressure on the soft tissues beneath the jaw. Roll the mask from the eyes down onto the face, ventilate from the head end and watch for chest movement. Use only enough volume to produce visible chest rise.'],
      ['28', 'Two-person ventilation and JAWS', 'JAWS prompts coordinated two-person ventilation: jaw thrust, adjuncts, work together and squeeze slowly. One clinician maintains the jaw thrust and mask seal while the other delivers slow ventilations in time with the monitor prompt. Reassess chest rise, airway position and the patient’s response, and avoid hyperventilation.'],
      ['29', 'Paediatric assisted ventilation', 'If a child’s respiratory rate or tidal volume is below normal for age and breathing is not normal or sufficient, provide assisted ventilations at the normal age-appropriate rate. Use current JRCALC paediatric parameters and avoid both hyperventilation and hypoventilation. The ventilation rate, volume and response must be reassessed continuously.'],
      ['30', 'Supraglottic airway and i-gel', 'An i-gel is a supraglottic airway device designed to be quicker and easier to insert than an endotracheal tube. A laryngoscope may be used to view the hypopharynx and confirm that there is no obstruction before insertion. A small initial leak may occur during confirmation ventilations; if it does not reduce or worsens, use a stepwise troubleshooting approach and seek senior support.'],
      ['31', 'Capnography and confirmation', 'The deck describes capnography phases: dead-space gas first, then alveolar gas, the alveolar plateau and the inspiratory down-slope. The end of the alveolar plateau gives the peak exhaled carbon-dioxide value reported as EtCO2. Use the waveform and number alongside chest rise, clinical condition and other monitoring to assess ventilation and airway-device function.'],
      ['32', 'Key learning points', 'Assess early, achieve neutral alignment, open the airway with the appropriate manoeuvre, recognise and treat FBAO, use suction safely, size and insert adjuncts correctly, ventilate with an effective seal and visible chest rise, confirm advanced airway function, monitor continuously and call for help early. An airway that cannot be opened, maintained or protected is a time-critical emergency.']
    ]
  },
  'bls-ils': {
    source: 'AAP BLS and ILS PPt October 26 v7.ppsx',
    intro: 'Basic life support (BLS) and intermediate life support (ILS) are time-critical responses to cardiac arrest. This topic follows the supplied deck through recognition of arrest, the chain of survival, resuscitation decisions, the BLS algorithm, AED use, the pit-crew approach, cardiac-arrest rhythms, reversible Hs and Ts, special circumstances, return of spontaneous circulation (ROSC), post-resuscitation care, human factors and clinical handover. Always use the current JRCALC, RCUK and local LAS guidance, equipment instructions and scope-of-practice requirements in practice.',
    sections: [
      ['01', 'Cardiac arrest recognition', 'A cardiac arrest is an emergency that usually happens without warning. The patient has collapsed, is unconscious and unresponsive, and is not breathing normally. Agonal breathing or gasping noises are abnormal breathing and should not be mistaken for signs of life or effective circulation.'],
      ['02', 'The chain of survival', 'The deck links early recognition and calling for help with early BLS, early defibrillation, advanced life support and post-resuscitation care. Each link depends on the preceding one, so delays in recognising arrest, starting compressions or applying the AED reduce the opportunity for a good outcome.'],
      ['03', 'Resuscitation decision-making', 'Make an early decision about whether resuscitation should be attempted, using the patient’s circumstances, any ReSPECT or DNACPR information, best-interests considerations and current guidance. A senior clinician decision is required for the relevant discontinuation or termination-of-resuscitation decisions described in the deck.'],
      ['04', 'Conditions unequivocally associated with death', 'The deck identifies conditions unequivocally associated with death as a reason not to start CPR. If resuscitation is inappropriate or futile, follow the current decision-making framework, document the rationale and communicate clearly with relatives, colleagues and the receiving service as appropriate.'],
      ['05', 'Danger and personal safety', 'Before approaching, assess whether the scene is safe, identify trip hazards and other people who may present a risk, and put on appropriate PPE. Do not allow urgency to remove the need for a dynamic risk assessment. Reassess safety as the incident develops and additional responders arrive.'],
      ['06', 'Response and AVPU', 'Call to the patient, ask a question and wait for an answer. Look for spontaneous eye opening or a verbal response, then apply an appropriate painful stimulus such as a shoulder squeeze if needed. Use AVPU to describe the response while moving quickly to assess the airway and breathing.'],
      ['07', 'Airway during arrest', 'The airway must be opened, maintained and protected. Use neutral alignment and padding as needed, consider cervical-spine injury, and use head tilt/chin lift or jaw thrust as appropriate. Look for an obvious obstruction and use suction when required. Airway management must support, not delay, chest compressions and defibrillation.'],
      ['08', 'Confirming cardiac arrest', 'Assess breathing and circulation simultaneously for up to 10 seconds. Look for chest rise and fall, recognise that agonal breathing is abnormal, and check for a central pulse according to current local guidance. Also look for obvious haemorrhage or another cause of fluid loss before confirming arrest and starting compressions.'],
      ['09', 'Starting BLS', 'For an unresponsive patient with absent or abnormal breathing, call for help, start CPR, attach the defibrillator and follow its prompts. The deck presents BLS as 30 compressions followed by two ventilations, continued until further help arrives, the patient shows signs of life, or a senior clinician directs a change.'],
      ['10', 'Chest-compression position', 'Deliver compressions on the lower half of the sternum at the centre of the chest. Keep the hands in the correct position and use a stable posture so that compressions are effective and consistent. Confirm the position again if compressors change or the patient moves.'],
      ['11', 'Compression depth and rate', 'Compress the adult chest to a depth of approximately 5–6 cm at a rate of 100–120 compressions per minute. Allow complete chest recoil after every compression and avoid leaning on the chest, because recoil allows the ventricles to fill between compressions.'],
      ['12', 'Ventilations and oxygen', 'Use the BVM with oxygen and an effective mask seal when equipment and trained personnel are available. Provide two ventilations after each cycle of 30 compressions, watching for chest rise. Avoid excessive ventilation, continue to optimise the airway and titrate oxygen in line with current guidance when the patient is not in arrest or after ROSC.'],
      ['13', 'AED and defibrillator pads', 'Apply the pads promptly, turn the AED on and follow the audible and visual prompts. Use the correct pad placement and keep interruptions to compressions as short as possible. In the deck, AED mode is the standard approach for cardiac arrests unless a senior clinician directs otherwise.'],
      ['14', 'Shock safety', 'When the defibrillator is analysing or charging, follow the device prompts and ensure nobody is touching the patient. Give a clear verbal command such as “stand clear” before shock delivery, check visually that the team is clear, then resume compressions immediately after the shock or no-shock decision.'],
      ['15', 'Compressor rotation', 'Swap compressors approximately every two minutes, coordinated with the rhythm-analysis period, to limit fatigue and maintain compression quality. Plan the change before it is due so that the pause is brief. The incoming compressor should be ready and the team should communicate clearly during the transition.'],
      ['16', 'The pit-crew model', 'The LAS pit-crew model gives each team member a clear role, such as airway management, chest compressions, preparing to take over compressions, paramedic ALS drugs, team leadership and checklist management. Everyone should understand their own role and the roles of other team members.'],
      ['17', 'Crew resource management', 'Effective teams allocate tasks, participate in problem solving within their skill level, anticipate what the leader will need and share important information clearly. Situational awareness, leadership and followership, flexibility, respect and listening support safe resuscitation.'],
      ['18', 'Calling and updating the EOC', 'The deck advises an early report to the Emergency Operations Centre as soon as practicable. Share the key facts, request appropriate resources and update the team as the patient’s rhythm, response, interventions and destination plan change.'],
      ['19', 'Four cardiac-arrest rhythms', 'The deck describes ventricular fibrillation (VF), pulseless ventricular tachycardia (pVT), pulseless electrical activity (PEA) and asystole. VF and pVT are shockable. PEA and asystole are non-shockable and require the appropriate non-shockable algorithm, including attention to reversible causes.'],
      ['20', 'VF and pulseless VT', 'VF has a chaotic waveform with no discernible pattern. Pulseless VT has a regular waveform but no pulse; VT can be pulsed or pulseless. The AED can recognise VF and pVT and will advise a shock when appropriate. Follow the device rather than delaying CPR for unnecessary pulse checks after a shockable rhythm is identified.'],
      ['21', 'PEA and asystole', 'PEA may show an organised electrical waveform but the patient has no effective pulse, so the rhythm is not compatible with effective circulation. Asystole is a flat or nearly flat waveform without a discernible pattern. Neither is shockable; continue CPR, follow the algorithm and search for reversible causes.'],
      ['22', 'Reversible Hs and Ts', 'The deck identifies hypoxia, hypovolaemia, hypothermia, hypo- or hyperglycaemia, toxins, tension pneumothorax, tamponade and thrombus as potentially reversible causes. Use the cardiac-arrest checklist, history, examination and available monitoring to identify and address them without compromising high-quality CPR or defibrillation.'],
      ['23', 'Hypoxia and hypovolaemia', 'For possible hypoxia, check that oxygen is connected, the cylinder is on, the chest is rising and the airway is optimised. For possible hypovolaemia, ask about trauma including burns, vomiting or other fluid loss, look for blood and assess the abdomen. Treat the likely cause and escalate promptly.'],
      ['24', 'Hypothermia and hypoglycaemia', 'Consider the environment, season and weather, take a temperature where possible and keep the patient warm. Peripheral pulses and respiratory effort may be difficult to detect in hypothermia, so consider whether hypothermia caused or contributed to the arrest and follow current resuscitation guidance. Check blood glucose and consider diabetes or other causes of hypo- or hyperglycaemia.'],
      ['25', 'Toxins and tension pneumothorax', 'For possible toxins, examine the environment and medications, ask about accidental or intentional overdose and inspect the pupils. Alert the senior clinician promptly if opioid toxicity is suspected. For possible tension pneumothorax, assess chest rise, auscultate where possible, check tracheal position and consider trauma before escalating to a paramedic.'],
      ['26', 'Tamponade and thrombus', 'Consider tamponade after trauma or chest injury; prehospital treatment options are limited and senior or paramedic input is required. Consider thrombus, including myocardial infarction and pulmonary embolism, from the collapse history and preceding symptoms such as breathlessness, travel, trauma, cough, haemoptysis or neurological symptoms. Obtain a post-ROSC ECG when indicated.'],
      ['27', 'Pregnancy and cardiac arrest', 'Hypoxia and hypovolaemia are common causes of maternal cardiac arrest and should be considered early. Use manual uterine displacement where indicated and plan time-critical transfer. The deck states that a senior clinician should make the decision regarding the appropriate resuscitation adaptation, ideally within five minutes of arrival at the arrest.'],
      ['28', 'Drowning and cardiac arrest', 'Personal safety remains paramount during a drowning rescue. Once the patient is rescued, clear the airway, provide five initial ventilations and then commence CPR according to the current drowning algorithm. There is a high risk of regurgitation, so protect the airway and manage the patient appropriately while maintaining ventilation and compressions.'],
      ['29', 'Tracheostomy and laryngectomy', 'A tracheostomy is a surgical opening into the trachea, usually with a tube in place, while the larynx remains and the upper airway may still connect to the lungs. After laryngectomy, the trachea is brought out as a permanent end stoma and the patient breathes only through the stoma, not through the mouth or nose. This distinction changes how airway and ventilation support are delivered.'],
      ['30', 'Return of spontaneous circulation', 'After ROSC, check that the airway is secure, assess EtCO2, assess respiratory effort and determine whether it is sufficient. If ventilation is inadequate, ventilate with a BVM at the appropriate rate and titrate oxygen according to current guidance. Check a two-point central pulse, complete the ROSC checklist, reassess Hs and Ts and prepare for possible re-arrest.'],
      ['31', 'Post-resuscitation assessment', 'Continue ABC assessment followed by observations, monitor the patient closely and support the senior clinician. The probability of re-arrest is high immediately after ROSC, so maintain a clear plan, keep equipment ready and communicate any deterioration immediately.'],
      ['32', 'Clinical handover', 'Use SBAR to explain why the team was called, the background and pre-collapse events, the assessment and current ABC status, key concerns and the recommendation or immediate needs. For trauma or other relevant presentations, ATMIST can structure age, time, mechanism, injuries, signs and symptoms, and treatment.'],
      ['33', 'Information for arrest handover', 'Include the time arrest was confirmed and BLS started, presenting rhythm, number of shocks, medical history, Hs and Ts considered, drugs given, rhythm changes, current observations and the stage of any checklist. Accurate timing and a clear sequence help the receiving team continue care and audit the resuscitation.'],
      ['34', 'Human factors and graded assertiveness', 'Human factors include how people interact with each other, equipment, tasks, processes and the environment. Use graded assertiveness to speak up about a concern: probe, alert, express a concern, escalate and use a safety stop when necessary. A respectful challenge can prevent medication, equipment or treatment errors.'],
      ['35', 'LifePak and equipment readiness', 'Complete vehicle and equipment checks: confirm that devices are clean and undamaged, batteries are present and charged, cables and adult or paediatric pads are available, and the device is connected correctly. Follow current IPC guidance for cleaning. The deck states that AED mode is standard for LAS cardiac arrests; manual mode is only used under senior-clinician direction.'],
      ['36', 'Key learning points', 'Recognise arrest early, keep the scene safe, start high-quality BLS, use the AED promptly, minimise pauses, work in clear roles, search for reversible Hs and Ts, adapt care for special circumstances, assess and protect the patient after ROSC, communicate using SBAR or ATMIST and follow current JRCALC, RCUK and local policy.']
    ]
  },
  'ecg-interpretation': {
    source: 'Lead II ECG Interpretation AAP v4 Jan24.pdf',
    intro: 'An ECG is an investigation that must always be interpreted alongside the patient’s clinical findings. This topic follows the supplied Lead II ECG teaching material through electrode placement, ECG paper, normal adult intervals, the ECG waveform, a structured rhythm-interpretation method, common cardiac-arrest rhythms, artefact and additional 12-lead views. Lead II monitoring is useful for rhythm assessment, but it is not a substitute for a diagnostic 12-lead ECG when diagnosis is required.',
    sections: [
      ['01', 'The ECG is part of the assessment', 'An ECG is only an investigation. A severe cardiac condition may be present despite a normal ECG, so interpret the trace in the context of the patient’s history, examination, symptoms and observations. Do not allow a reassuring-looking trace to overrule clinical deterioration.'],
      ['02', 'Lead II and the 12-lead ECG', 'Lead II is useful for rhythm monitoring but is inadequate for diagnosis of many conditions. A 12-lead ECG is essential when a diagnostic assessment is required. Attach a paper copy to the PRF and photograph the ECG for the ePCR as described in the deck.'],
      ['03', 'Lead II monitoring electrodes', 'The deck uses the mnemonic “Ride Your Green Bike”: RA on the right arm/upper torso, LA on the left arm/upper torso, LL on the left lower torso and RL on the right lower torso. Follow current local placement guidance and keep the limb electrodes consistent.'],
      ['04', 'Preparing the skin', 'Use in-date, sealed electrodes. Prepare the skin so it is dry and free from loose skin, and avoid very hairy areas where possible. Good skin preparation improves electrode contact and reduces artefact. Replace electrodes or reposition them if the trace is poor.'],
      ['05', 'Electrode positioning and cables', 'Keep the electrodes equidistant where possible and avoid crossed wires. Place the trolley or bed so the patient is supported, usually semi-recumbent where clinically appropriate. Check the cables and connections before interpreting an abnormal-looking trace.'],
      ['06', 'Depolarisation and ECG direction', 'Depolarisation moving towards a positive electrode produces a positive deflection. Depolarisation moving away from a positive electrode produces a negative deflection. The direction and size of the waveform therefore depend on the electrical vector in relation to the lead.'],
      ['07', 'The ECG paper scale', 'On the ECG paper described in the deck, each small square represents 40 milliseconds or 0.04 seconds. Each large square represents 200 milliseconds or 0.2 seconds and contains five small squares. Use the correct paper scale when estimating intervals and rate.'],
      ['08', 'Normal adult intervals', 'The deck gives a normal adult PR interval of 120–200 milliseconds, or three to five small squares. The normal adult QRS interval is 80–120 milliseconds, or two to three small squares. The QTc interval is given as less than 500 milliseconds in the teaching material.'],
      ['09', 'The P wave', 'The P wave represents atrial depolarisation. When assessing a rhythm, examine whether P waves are present, their shape and whether they occur before each QRS complex. Also consider whether the P waves are regular and whether atrial activity is related to ventricular activity.'],
      ['10', 'The PR interval', 'The PR interval represents the time from atrial depolarisation to the start of ventricular depolarisation, including delay at the AV junction. Assess whether it is within the expected adult range, constant and related consistently to the QRS complexes.'],
      ['11', 'The Q wave', 'The Q wave represents septal depolarisation in the teaching sequence. Identify the Q wave as part of the QRS complex and avoid interpreting an isolated component without considering the complete waveform, lead and clinical context.'],
      ['12', 'The R wave', 'The R wave represents ventricular depolarisation. It is the main positive deflection of the QRS complex in many leads. Assess the overall QRS shape and duration rather than relying on the R wave alone.'],
      ['13', 'The S wave', 'The S wave represents the later stage of ventricular depolarisation. Together, the Q, R and S waves form the QRS complex. Assess its shape, width and relationship with P waves when interpreting a rhythm.'],
      ['14', 'The ST segment', 'The ST segment is described as isoelectric. Compare the segment with the baseline and interpret any apparent deviation in the context of the patient, the lead used and the quality of the recording. A Lead II trace alone is not a diagnostic substitute for a 12-lead ECG.'],
      ['15', 'The T wave', 'The T wave represents ventricular repolarisation. Assess its appearance as part of the complete ECG, while remembering that waveform shape and polarity depend on the lead and the direction of electrical activity.'],
      ['16', 'A structured interpretation sequence', 'The deck’s interpretation rules ask: Is there electrical activity? What is the rhythm? What is the rate? Are P waves present and what is their shape? Is there a P wave before every QRS and a QRS after every P wave? What is the PR interval? What is the QRS shape and duration?'],
      ['17', 'Electrical activity and pulse', 'Always relate electrical activity to the patient’s pulse and clinical condition. A rhythm strip can show electrical activity without effective mechanical circulation, as in pulseless electrical activity. Conversely, an apparent flat trace may be caused by technical problems, so check the patient, leads and equipment.'],
      ['18', 'Rate and rhythm regularity', 'Describe the rate and whether the rhythm is regular, regularly irregular or irregularly irregular. Use the ECG paper scale and the R-R intervals to support the assessment. Do not label a rhythm from rate alone; interpret the P waves, PR interval and QRS complex as well.'],
      ['19', 'P-wave and QRS relationship', 'Assess whether there is atrial activity and whether it is related to ventricular activity. In normal sinus rhythm, P waves precede and are associated with QRS complexes. A missing, abnormal or dissociated relationship can indicate a different rhythm and should be interpreted with the clinical picture.'],
      ['20', 'Normal sinus rhythm', 'The deck’s normal sinus rhythm example has electrical activity and a pulse, a regular rhythm, a rate around 80, narrow QRS complexes and P waves before and after each QRS in a consistent relationship. Use this as a structured example rather than relying on the label alone.'],
      ['21', 'Ventricular tachycardia', 'The deck describes ventricular tachycardia as regular, usually faster than 120 beats per minute, with difficult-to-identify P waves and a wide, bizarre QRS complex greater than three small squares. Determine whether the patient is pulsed or pulseless and escalate according to the current clinical algorithm.'],
      ['22', 'Ventricular fibrillation', 'Ventricular fibrillation has a chaotic waveform with no discernible pattern and no pulse. It requires CPR and defibrillation. Follow the cardiac-arrest algorithm and AED prompts; do not delay lifesaving treatment while trying to obtain a detailed rhythm description.'],
      ['23', 'Asystole', 'Asystole is described as having no pulse and no organised electrical activity; it is not necessarily a perfectly straight line. Check the patient, leads, gain and connections to exclude technical failure, then continue CPR and follow the non-shockable-arrest algorithm.'],
      ['24', 'Pulseless electrical activity', 'Pulseless electrical activity is electrical activity without an effective pulse. The QRS complexes are often wide in the teaching example. PEA is not shockable; continue CPR, follow the algorithm and search actively for reversible causes.'],
      ['25', 'ECG artefact', 'Muscular or electrical interference can disrupt the ECG. If the trace is unclear, check the patient and equipment, reduce movement where possible, move an electrode away from a large muscle mass and switch off interfering electrical equipment if necessary.'],
      ['26', '12-lead chest-electrode placement', 'The deck gives V1 at the fourth intercostal space at the right sternal border, V2 at the fourth intercostal space at the left sternal border, V4 at the fifth intercostal space on the mid-clavicular line, V3 midway between V2 and V4, V5 at the fifth intercostal space on the anterior axillary line and V6 at the fifth intercostal space on the mid-axillary line.'],
      ['27', 'The order of precordial placement', 'Place V1 and V2 first, then V4, followed by V3 between V2 and V4, and V5 and V6 at the same horizontal level as V4. Locating the intercostal spaces accurately is more important than placing electrodes by guesswork or by an incorrect horizontal line.'],
      ['28', 'Right-sided V4R recording', 'For a V4R trace, remove V4 from the left chest, attach a new electrode to the V4 lead and place it at the fourth intercostal space on the right mid-clavicular line. Reprint the 12-lead ECG and label the recording clearly according to current JRCALC guidance.'],
      ['29', 'Posterior ECG recording', 'For a posterior ECG, the deck describes removing V4, V5 and V6, placing new electrodes so that the leads become V7, V8 and V9, printing the trace at the top and crossing out the original V4, V5 and V6 labels. Follow current JRCALC guidance and label the trace accurately.'],
      ['30', 'Key learning points', 'Use the ECG as part of a complete clinical assessment, know the limits of Lead II, prepare the skin and place electrodes correctly, understand the paper scale and normal intervals, interpret P waves, PR interval, QRS, ST segment and T waves systematically, recognise arrest rhythms, troubleshoot artefact and obtain additional 12-lead views when indicated.'],
    ]
  },
  'medical-gases': {
    source: 'L3 Administration of Medical Gases v1 September 2020.pdf',
    intro: 'Medical gases are medicines and compressed-gas products that must be selected, checked, administered and stored safely. This topic follows the supplied material through oxygen physiology, hypoxia and oxygen therapy, indications and cautions, cylinders and regulators, equipment and flow rates, compressed-gas hazards, SpO2 measurement problems and Entonox. Treat oxygen as a medication, use current JRCALC and local guidance, and work within scope and competence.',
    sections: [
      ['01', 'Oxygen is a medication', 'Oxygen should be treated as a medicine: it should be prescribed or administered to patients with a clinical need, using the appropriate device and flow. The deck emphasises that oxygen is treatment for hypoxaemia, not simply a treatment for breathlessness.'],
      ['02', 'Oxygen and cellular function', 'Oxygen provides the fuel that cells need to produce the chemical energy required for normal body function. Oxygen is carried in the blood, principally by haemoglobin, and is delivered to tissues for cellular metabolism.'],
      ['03', 'Normal oxygen saturation', 'The supplied material gives a normal saturation range of approximately 94–98% for healthy adults. Interpret a saturation reading alongside the patient’s presentation, work of breathing, circulation, mental state and relevant history rather than using a number in isolation.'],
      ['04', 'Hypoxia and hypoxaemia', 'Hypoxaemia refers to an inadequate concentration of oxygen in arterial blood, while hypoxia describes inadequate oxygen availability at tissue or cellular level. A patient may have serious oxygen-delivery problems even when one measurement appears reassuring, so assess the whole patient.'],
      ['05', 'Clinical features of inadequate oxygenation', 'The deck links inadequate oxygenation with features such as tachycardia, hypertension, peripheral vasoconstriction, dizziness and confusion. More severe or prolonged hypoxia may be associated with cyanosis, breathlessness, reduced consciousness, abnormal respiratory patterns and respiratory or cardiac deterioration.'],
      ['06', 'Benefits of oxygen therapy', 'Oxygen therapy increases the oxygen available in the lungs, supports haemoglobin carriage and improves oxygen delivery to tissues. It can reduce the effects of hypoxia on the brain, heart and other organs when it is clinically indicated and delivered appropriately.'],
      ['07', 'Titrate oxygen to the patient', 'Oxygen therapy should be titrated to achieve the target saturation and clinical response required by current guidance. Avoid treating a symptom alone, avoid unnecessary high-concentration oxygen and reassess after changing the device or flow. Document the indication, device, flow and response.'],
      ['08', 'Indications and cautions', 'Use oxygen when the patient has a clinical indication such as hypoxaemia or inadequate oxygenation, following current JRCALC and local guidance. Consider conditions in which excessive oxygen may be harmful, use the appropriate target range and escalate when oxygenation remains inadequate or the patient deteriorates.'],
      ['09', 'Contraindications and decision-making', 'The deck requires clinicians to understand indications and contraindications. Check the current JRCALC Plus guidance and local policy for the patient’s condition, target saturation and permitted device or flow. Do not treat the supplied flow-rate table as a substitute for a clinical decision or current guidance.'],
      ['10', 'Oxygen cylinder checks', 'Before use, check the cylinder label and expiry information, confirm that the cylinder is appropriate for the intended gas, inspect its condition and ensure the valve and regulator are compatible. Check that the contents gauge shows an adequate supply and that the equipment is ready to work.'],
      ['11', 'Opening and closing the cylinder', 'Operate the cylinder valve correctly and check for leaks or unexpected loss of pressure. Open the valve carefully, confirm the pressure and flow, and close it after use according to local equipment procedure. Never force a valve or use equipment that appears damaged.'],
      ['12', 'Regulators and pressure reduction', 'A regulator reduces the high pressure in a cylinder to a usable working pressure. The deck describes cylinder pressure reducing from approximately 2,000 psi to around 60 psi before delivery through a flow meter. Use only the correct regulator and never connect incompatible equipment.'],
      ['13', 'Flow meters and delivery', 'A flow meter attached to the regulator allows the clinician to set the rate at which gas is delivered to the patient. Confirm that the selected device can deliver the required flow, check that gas is actually flowing and reassess the patient after application.'],
      ['14', 'Common oxygen-delivery equipment', 'The deck lists a bag-valve mask, non-rebreather mask, simple face mask, Venturi mask, nasal cannula and nebuliser as ambulance oxygen-delivery equipment. Select the device that matches the patient’s breathing, oxygenation, consciousness and clinical plan.'],
      ['15', 'Flow-rate examples in the deck', 'The equipment table gives examples of oxygen flows: BVM 15 L/min, non-rebreather mask 10–15 L/min, simple face mask 5–10 L/min, Venturi mask 4 L/min, nasal cannula 6 L/min and nebuliser 6–8 L/min. These are teaching values and must be applied with current JRCALC guidance and the device manufacturer’s instructions.'],
      ['16', 'Bag-valve-mask oxygen', 'A BVM connected to oxygen can support ventilation when the patient is not breathing adequately. Use the correct mask, maintain an open airway, check for chest rise and avoid excessive ventilation. A BVM is a ventilation device as well as an oxygen-delivery device, so assess airway and breathing continuously.'],
      ['17', 'Non-rebreather and simple masks', 'A non-rebreather mask can provide a high concentration of oxygen when the patient is breathing spontaneously and requires it. A simple face mask provides a lower-flow delivery option. Check the patient’s respiratory effort, mask fit, target saturation and response rather than choosing a device by habit.'],
      ['18', 'Venturi masks and controlled oxygen', 'A Venturi mask provides a controlled oxygen concentration at the flow specified for the device. It can be useful when a controlled concentration is required. Confirm the correct device, colour or entrainment setting according to the equipment and current clinical guidance.'],
      ['19', 'Nasal cannulae and nebulisers', 'Nasal cannulae provide oxygen through the nose and may be better tolerated by some patients. A nebuliser uses gas flow to deliver a medication aerosol when indicated. Check the prescription or clinical guideline for the drug, device, flow, response and any contraindication or caution.'],
      ['20', 'SpO2 is a measurement, not the whole assessment', 'Pulse oximetry estimates oxygen saturation and should be interpreted with the patient’s appearance and clinical condition. A poor trace, unexpected value or mismatch with the patient should prompt a probe, equipment, circulation and clinical reassessment rather than automatic escalation of oxygen.'],
      ['21', 'Problems recording oxygen saturation', 'The deck highlights heavily applied nail varnish and poor peripheral circulation from cold, disease or sudden illness as causes of unreliable readings. If needed, remove or work around the obstruction, warm or reassess the site where appropriate and check the probe connection.'],
      ['22', 'Improving a pulse-oximeter signal', 'When nail varnish or poor contact affects the reading, a quarter-turn of the finger probe may improve contact with the skin. Confirm that the displayed waveform or pulse signal is credible and compare it with the patient’s pulse and overall clinical picture.'],
      ['23', 'Compressed-gas hazards', 'Compressed gas can make materials burn more readily and can intensify a fire. Keep oxygen away from naked flames, smoking, sparks, oil, grease and other ignition sources. Do not allow oil or grease to contact outlets, valves or associated equipment because of the risk of fire or explosion.'],
      ['24', 'Oxygen and ambulance safety', 'Use oxygen with care around machinery and equipment that can create sparks. Smoking and naked flames must not be used in the presence of oxygen. Display and obey appropriate warning signs, communicate the hazard to the team and reassess the environment when oxygen is being used.'],
      ['25', 'Cylinder storage', 'Medical-gas cylinders should be stored securely in well-ventilated locations or designated cupboards. Full and empty cylinders should be separated, cylinders should not be used for other purposes and they should be supported upright so they cannot fall. Keep cylinders clean and protected from damage.'],
      ['26', 'Moving cylinders safely', 'Newly installed cylinders should be secured before use. Do not roll or drag cylinders along the ground, avoid dropping them and take care when turning valves or connecting equipment. Use the correct handling method and ask for help if a cylinder is heavy or difficult to move safely.'],
      ['27', 'Entonox properties', 'Entonox is a 50:50 mixture of nitrous oxide and oxygen. It is inhaled and self-administered by the patient, has a rapid onset and its analgesic effect reduces rapidly when the patient stops inhaling it. Use current JRCALC guidance for indications, cautions, contraindications and dosing or administration requirements.'],
      ['28', 'Entonox and low temperature', 'When the temperature of Entonox falls below 6°C, the two gases can separate. If the cylinder has been stored below this temperature, invert it the required number of times before use to remix the gases, following the current equipment and local procedure.'],
      ['29', 'Entonox assessment and monitoring', 'Before offering Entonox, assess whether the patient can understand and self-administer it, check the current indications and contraindications, explain how to use it and monitor the patient’s response. Stop and reassess if the patient becomes unable to hold the mouthpiece or shows deterioration.'],
      ['30', 'Key learning points', 'Treat oxygen as a medication, identify the clinical need and target, select the correct device and flow, check cylinders and regulators, understand compressed-gas fire hazards, troubleshoot SpO2 readings, store and move cylinders safely, and use Entonox only within current JRCALC, local policy and scope of practice.'],
    ]
  },
  'clinical-shock': {
    source: 'L3 - Clinical Shock v1 September 2020.pdf',
    intro: 'Shock is a life-threatening state in which the circulation cannot provide enough oxygenated blood to meet the body\'s needs. This topic covers hypovolaemic, distributive, cardiogenic, obstructive and dissociative shock, common causes and signs, groups who may compensate until significant blood loss has occurred, structured assessment and supportive prehospital management. Use a primary survey, identify the cause and arrange time-critical transfer.',
    sections: [
      ['01', 'What is shock?', 'Shock occurs when the circulation cannot deliver sufficient oxygenated blood to the tissues. The patient may deteriorate rapidly, so assess and treat immediate threats while looking for the underlying cause. Do not wait for every classic sign before escalating.'],
      ['02', 'Types of shock', 'The deck identifies five types: hypovolaemic, distributive, cardiogenic, obstructive and dissociative shock. The categories describe different problems with circulating volume, vascular tone, cardiac pump function, flow obstruction or oxygen carriage.'],
      ['03', 'Hypovolaemic shock', 'Hypovolaemic shock follows acute loss of circulating volume. Causes include severe internal or external haemorrhage, extensive burns where serum is lost, and severe diarrhoea or vomiting where water and electrolytes are lost. Search for the source and manage catastrophic bleeding immediately.'],
      ['04', 'Distributive shock', 'Distributive shock results from widespread dilation of the peripheral vascular system, creating the effect of a larger container for the same blood volume. In sepsis or anaphylaxis, leaky blood vessels can allow fluid to move into the interstitial space.'],
      ['05', 'Causes of distributive shock', 'Common causes listed are anaphylaxis, sepsis and nervous-system-related loss of vascular tone such as spinal cord injury. Identify clues to the cause because management must address both the shock state and the underlying emergency.'],
      ['06', 'Cardiogenic shock', 'Cardiogenic shock occurs when the heart cannot circulate enough blood to meet the body\'s metabolic needs. The deck identifies myocardial infarction, acute heart failure and arrhythmia as causes. Assess the patient for cardiac symptoms and obtain appropriate observations and ECG when indicated.'],
      ['07', 'Obstructive shock', 'Obstructive shock is caused by an obstruction to flow to or from the heart. Causes listed include tension pneumothorax, cardiac tamponade and pulmonary embolism. Look for the clinical features of these underlying time-critical conditions and escalate promptly.'],
      ['08', 'Dissociative shock', 'Dissociative shock results when the blood\'s oxygen-carrying capacity is impaired. This may occur because there are too few red blood cells available to carry oxygen or because competing molecules occupy binding sites on red blood cells. The deck gives anaemia and carbon-monoxide poisoning as causes.'],
      ['09', 'Signs and symptoms', 'Possible signs include tachycardia, tachypnoea or increased respiratory depth, agitation or confusion, dizziness or faintness, pale cold extremities, delayed capillary refill, sweating, dilated pupils and thirst. Reduced level of consciousness and bradycardia are late, near-fatal features in the deck.'],
      ['10', 'Compensation can mask shock', 'Some patients may not show abnormal vital signs until significant blood volume has been lost. The deck highlights young healthy or athletic people, pregnant patients and patients taking beta blockers. Interpret observations in context and do not use a normal pulse alone to exclude shock.'],
      ['11', 'Primary survey', 'Use the structured R-C ABCDE approach shown in the deck, assess whether the patient is time critical and correct immediate problems. Identify the likely type and cause of shock while treating life threats. Continue reassessment because the patient may worsen.'],
      ['12', 'Oxygen and positioning', 'The deck advises high-concentration oxygen during shock management. If conscious, position the patient flat with the head and shoulders slightly raised and consider raising the lower limbs. If unconscious, use the stable side position when appropriate and continue to protect the airway.'],
      ['13', 'Treat the cause', 'Treat other injuries and bleeding. Control external haemorrhage, address airway and breathing problems and seek appropriate senior or paramedic support. The patient needs definitive treatment for the cause as well as supportive management of shock.'],
      ['14', 'Temperature and handling', 'Do not overheat the patient or allow them to become cold. Avoid rough handling and be gentle, because movement may worsen pain, bleeding or injury. Provide constant reassurance and maintain dignity while exposing only as much as needed for assessment.'],
      ['15', 'Monitoring', 'Make constant observations of pulse, level of consciousness and respiration, alongside the wider primary survey. Monitor trends rather than relying on one set of observations. Reassess after each intervention and during transfer.'],
      ['16', 'Nil by mouth and transfer', 'Keep the patient nil by mouth because an anaesthetic may be required. Shock is time critical, so transfer to the appropriate facility without unnecessary delay, continuing management and assessment en route.'],
      ['17', 'Key learning points', 'Recognise that shock may be caused by volume loss, vasodilation, pump failure, obstruction or impaired oxygen carriage. Use a structured primary survey, search for and treat the cause, provide supportive care, monitor continuously, prevent heat loss and arrange time-critical transfer with appropriate clinical support.']
    ]
  },
  'allergic-reaction-anaphylaxis': {
    source: 'L3 Allergic Reaction  Anaphylaxis v2 September 2020.pdf',
    intro: 'Anaphylaxis is a severe, life-threatening, generalised or systemic hypersensitivity reaction. It can develop rapidly after injection, ingestion, skin contact or inhalation and may compromise the airway, breathing or circulation, often with skin or mucosal changes. This topic covers triggers, progression, presentation, oxygen, monitoring, pre-alerting and urgent escalation within scope and current guidance.',
    sections: [
      ['01', 'Allergic reaction and anaphylaxis', 'Allergic reactions range from milder localised symptoms, such as urticaria, to life-threatening anaphylaxis. Common triggers listed are food, drugs and venom, although the trigger is unknown in some cases. Assess the whole patient rather than focusing only on the skin.'],
      ['02', 'Definition of anaphylaxis', 'Anaphylaxis is a severe, life-threatening, generalised or systemic hypersensitivity reaction. It is characterised by rapidly developing life-threatening airway, breathing or circulation problems, usually associated with skin or mucosal changes.'],
      ['03', 'Routes of exposure', 'The allergen may enter by injection, ingestion, absorption through skin contact or inhalation. The time taken for symptoms to develop depends partly on how the trigger enters the body. Ask what the patient was exposed to and when.'],
      ['04', 'Potential triggers', 'The deck lists pharmaceutical products, foods, antibiotics, vitamins, venoms, cosmetics, plants, dusts and moulds, animals, toxins and vaccines. Ask about known allergies, recent exposures, medicines and any previous reaction.'],
      ['05', 'Systems affected', 'Anaphylaxis can affect the respiratory, cardiovascular, gastrointestinal and nervous systems as well as the skin. Symptoms may occur in more than one system, and the absence of a skin change should not distract from airway, breathing or circulation compromise.'],
      ['06', 'Progression', 'The session describes progression from an early allergic stage to developing anaphylaxis, followed swiftly by anaphylactic shock. Deterioration can be rapid, so call for appropriate support and prepare for urgent transport as soon as severe features appear.'],
      ['07', 'Early presentation', 'Early features may include an itchy rash, swelling around the eyes, prickly lips, a dry non-productive cough and restlessness. These findings require continued assessment because the reaction may progress to airway, breathing or circulation compromise.'],
      ['08', 'Airway and breathing features', 'Airway or breathing features include tongue and throat swelling, wheeze, hoarseness, dyspnoea, stridor, bronchospasm, cyanosis and respiratory collapse. Treat airway and breathing compromise as time critical and request senior or paramedic support.'],
      ['09', 'Circulation and systemic features', 'Circulatory features include tachycardia, hypotension and circulatory collapse. Other possible symptoms include nausea, vomiting, abdominal cramps, watery diarrhoea, abdominal distension, headache, dizziness, confusion and collapse.'],
      ['10', 'Primary survey', 'Use a structured primary survey and look for rapidly developing airway, breathing or circulation problems. Assess skin and mucosal changes, but do not wait for a rash before acting if the patient has life-threatening airway, breathing or circulation features.'],
      ['11', 'Oxygen', 'The deck describes anaphylaxis as time critical and advises an initial oxygen dose of 15 litres per minute through a non-rebreather mask. Continue until vital signs are normal, then reduce the dose and aim for a saturation target of 94-98% as stated in the material.'],
      ['12', 'Senior and paramedic support', 'Senior clinician or paramedic intervention may be needed for advanced airway management, intravenous fluids and drugs. Consider whether waiting for additional support would delay life-saving treatment or transport, and continue management within your own scope.'],
      ['13', 'Therapeutic drugs', 'The deck identifies oxygen as part of treatment and notes that drugs such as salbutamol and adrenaline 1:1000 may be given by senior clinicians. Follow current JRCALC and local guidance, scope of practice and the patient\'s clinical presentation.'],
      ['14', 'Monitoring', 'Continually monitor pulse, respirations, blood pressure, peak flow where appropriate, oxygen saturation, capnography and ECG. Reassess airway, breathing and circulation after each intervention because anaphylaxis can worsen quickly.'],
      ['15', 'Transport and pre-alert', 'Anaphylaxis requires rapid transportation to hospital. Make the pre-alert call described in the deck, communicate the suspected anaphylaxis, clinical findings, observations, treatment given and response, and continue monitoring during transfer.'],
      ['16', 'Reassurance and communication', 'Provide continual reassurance while maintaining close clinical observation. Ask about the trigger, time of exposure, previous reactions, medicines and any treatment already given. Explain where the patient is being taken and hand over concerns clearly.'],
      ['17', 'Key learning points', 'Recognise the difference between a localised allergic reaction and life-threatening anaphylaxis, identify airway, breathing and circulation compromise, understand the possible exposure routes and triggers, provide oxygen as directed, request appropriate senior support, monitor continuously and transport rapidly.']
    ]
  },
  'diabetes': {
    source: 'L3 Diabetes v1 September 2020.pdf',
    intro: 'Diabetes is a metabolic disorder associated with chronic high blood glucose. Acute glycaemic emergencies may present with confusion, abnormal behaviour, seizures, dehydration, shock or reduced consciousness. This topic covers diabetes, type 1 and type 2 mechanisms, blood glucose measurement, hypoglycaemia, hyperglycaemia, assessment and prehospital management.',
    sections: [
      ['01', 'What is diabetes?', 'The deck describes diabetes as a common metabolic disorder identified by chronic high blood sugar levels. Acute presentations may result from glucose that is too low or too high, another illness, infection, medication problems or inadequate intake.'],
      ['02', 'Risk and underlying factors', 'The source lists genetic factors, obesity, age and ethnicity among underlying causes or risk categories for diabetes mellitus. Take a complete history rather than assuming the patient\'s symptoms are caused only by their known diabetes.'],
      ['03', 'Type 1 diabetes', 'In type 1 diabetes, insulin is not being produced. Without sufficient insulin, glucose cannot be used normally by cells and the patient is vulnerable to metabolic disturbance when insulin is absent or inadequate.'],
      ['04', 'Type 2 diabetes', 'In type 2 diabetes, insulin is released but receptor sites have changed and insulin cannot enter cells effectively enough to allow sufficient glucose absorption. Patients may manage the condition with medication, diet and other measures according to their individual plan.'],
      ['05', 'Blood glucose units and ranges', 'Blood glucose is the amount of glucose in the blood and is expressed in millimoles per litre (mmol/L). The deck gives typical reference values of 3.0-5.6 mmol/L for non-diabetic people, 4-7 mmol/L before meals for people with diabetes, less than 10 mmol/L one and a half hours after meals and around 8 mmol/L at bedtime.'],
      ['06', 'Hypoglycaemia', 'Hypoglycaemia means low blood glucose. The deck identifies a BM below 4 mmol/L as hypoglycaemia and states that treatment should take place when a reading is suspected or confirmed to be below 4. Consider the clinical presentation as well as the number.'],
      ['07', 'Causes of hypoglycaemia', 'Causes include overdose of insulin or oral hypoglycaemic agents, hepatic or renal dysfunction, other illnesses including infection, gastrointestinal disturbance, inadequate food intake, increased physical activity, alcohol, pregnancy or breastfeeding and older age.'],
      ['08', 'Hypoglycaemia symptoms', 'Autonomic symptoms include sweating, palpitations, shaking and hunger. Neurological symptoms include confusion, drowsiness, odd behaviour, speech difficulty, incoordination, aggression, fitting and unconsciousness. Headache, nausea and general malaise may also occur.'],
      ['09', 'Hypoglycaemia in non-diabetic patients', 'Hypoglycaemia can occur in people with diabetes and in other unwell patients. Do not exclude low blood glucose because the patient has no diabetes history. Check a BM in altered behaviour, reduced consciousness, seizures or unexplained illness when appropriate.'],
      ['10', 'Hyperglycaemia', 'Hyperglycaemia means high blood sugar levels. Causes listed include undiagnosed diabetes, illness, infection, myocardial infarction, medication such as steroids and failure to administer diabetic medication.'],
      ['11', 'Hyperglycaemia symptoms', 'Features may include polyuria, polydipsia, polyphagia, ketone breath, Kussmaul\'s respiration, rapid weight loss, lethargy, confusion, dehydration, signs of shock, hyperventilation and unconsciousness. Consider a serious metabolic emergency when these features are present.'],
      ['12', 'Hypo versus hyperglycaemia', 'Hypoglycaemia tends to have rapid onset within minutes and may cause pale sweaty skin, rapid full pulse, irritability, confusion, incoordination, aggression, hunger, headache, dizziness and late seizures. Hyperglycaemia usually develops over hours to days and may cause dry skin, rapid weak pulse, lethargy, fruity breath, abdominal symptoms, excessive urine, thirst and hypotension.'],
      ['13', 'Assessing a glycaemic emergency', 'Start with ABC assessment and level of consciousness. Measure and record BM, ask about diabetes, medication, food, activity, alcohol and illness, and look for infection, trauma or other causes of altered behaviour. Obtain a full set of observations and reassess after treatment.'],
      ['14', 'Management of hypoglycaemia', 'Treat suspected or confirmed hypoglycaemia according to current JRCALC Plus guidance and the patient\'s level of consciousness. Protect the airway, do not give oral intake to a patient who cannot safely swallow and recheck the BM and clinical response after treatment.'],
      ['15', 'Management of hyperglycaemia', 'Correct ABC problems, measure and record BM, record an ECG, administer supplemental oxygen if hypoxaemic and pre-alert the receiving hospital when indicated. Continue observations and manage dehydration, shock or reduced consciousness within scope.'],
      ['16', 'Blood glucose sampling', 'Clean the patient\'s finger with soap and water or an alcohol swab and allow it to dry. Do not use Chloraprep. Obtain the sample with a capillary lancet, wipe away the first drop, use a fresh test strip and follow the meter procedure.'],
      ['17', 'Safe meter procedure', 'Wait for the blood-droplet screen after the meter self-test, apply a drop of blood to the sample end and read the result in mmol/L. Dispose of the used strip safely using the meter eject function over clinical waste.'],
      ['18', 'Reassessment', 'Reassess airway, breathing, circulation, disability, observations and blood glucose after intervention. Improvement in behaviour or consciousness does not remove the need to identify the cause, monitor the patient and consider conveyance or senior advice.'],
      ['19', 'Key learning points', 'Check BM in unexplained neurological or behavioural presentations, recognise both hypo- and hyperglycaemia, look for the different patterns of onset and skin, pulse and breathing changes, treat within current guidance, record ECG when required, give oxygen only when hypoxaemic and pre-alert when the patient is time critical.']
    ]
  },
  'spinal-pelvic-injuries': {
    source: 'L3 Spinal  Pelvic Injuries v1 October 2020.pdf',
    intro: 'Spinal and pelvic injuries can cause major haemorrhage, spinal cord damage, respiratory failure and permanent disability. This topic covers mechanisms, recognition, pelvic splinting, spinal cord injury, spinal and neurogenic shock, cauda equina syndrome, immobilisation, extrication, documentation and major-trauma referral. Minimise movement, maintain cervical-spine control when indicated and transport smoothly to definitive care.',
    sections: [
      ['01', 'Pelvic injury significance', 'Pelvic fractures can cause fatal haemorrhage and commonly occur with other traumatic injuries. The deck identifies high-energy trauma, falls from height and crush injury as causes, while simple falls can cause pelvic injury in older people, people with degenerative bone disease or people who have received radiotherapy.'],
      ['02', 'Mechanisms of pelvic injury', 'Consider road-traffic collisions, falls from height and crush mechanisms. Significant door intrusion into a vehicle passenger space or a dented motorcycle petrol tank should raise concern. Distracting injuries, such as a fractured ankle, may hide a pelvic injury.'],
      ['03', 'Pelvic complications', 'A pelvic fracture can increase pelvic volume and allow substantial haemorrhage. Complications include vascular injury, urogenital injury such as bladder rupture and associated intrathoracic or intra-abdominal injuries. Treat the mechanism as significant even when external signs are limited.'],
      ['04', 'Pelvic signs and symptoms', 'Possible findings include bruising, bleeding from the rectum, vagina or urethra, deformity, swelling, shortening of a lower limb, pain over the hips, groin or lower back, hypovolaemic shock and altered sensation in one leg. Mechanism and patient physiology are important clues.'],
      ['05', 'Time-critical pelvic management', 'Use DRABC and apply a pelvic splint when indicated. Consider cervical-spine management during airway care, control catastrophic haemorrhage, give high-flow oxygen as directed, immobilise the knees and ankles as well as the pelvis, request paramedic help, provide pain relief and arrange smooth rapid removal to a major trauma centre.'],
      ['06', 'Avoid repeated pelvic examination', 'Avoid repeated examinations and unnecessary movement. Never spring or repeatedly rock the pelvis because this may dislodge clots and exacerbate injury. Once a pelvic splint has been placed, do not remove it during the ambulance management described in the deck.'],
      ['07', 'Pelvic splints', 'The deck describes the pelvic splint as adjustable across a wide range of patient sizes and as a single-use device. Apply it according to current local equipment guidance, document its use and transport the patient for definitive care.'],
      ['08', 'Pelvic transport', 'Pelvic injuries require immediate splinting and transport to a major trauma centre. Limit movement and keep any log roll to 10 degrees as stated in the deck. Continue full observations and reassessment during smooth transport.'],
      ['09', 'Spinal mechanisms', 'Spinal injury can result from hyperflexion, hyperextension, rotation, compression or a combination of forces. Falls and road-traffic collisions are prominent mechanisms in the deck, with risk increased in older people and people with rheumatoid arthritis after falls.'],
      ['10', 'Types of spinal injury', 'Spinal injuries include dislocation of vertebrae, fracture of vertebrae and displacement or prolapse of vertebrae. These injuries can compress or stretch the spinal cord and cause spinal cord injury.'],
      ['11', 'Other spinal causes', 'Other causes include spinal tumours, vascular thrombosis or haemorrhage, infection and abscesses caused by conditions such as tuberculosis or meningitis. Consider non-traumatic spinal emergencies when symptoms do not fit the mechanism or when neurological deterioration is unexplained.'],
      ['12', 'Spinal cord complications', 'Complications include spinal-cord damage or compression, spinal shock, neurogenic shock, respiratory difficulties and cauda equina syndrome. Monitor neurological findings and physiology closely, because deterioration may affect airway, breathing or circulation.'],
      ['13', 'Spinal shock', 'After spinal cord injury, spinal shock is described as a state of complete loss of motor function, with possible loss of sensory function. It may continue for a considerable time, although recovery may occur. Document the neurological baseline and changes accurately.'],
      ['14', 'Neurogenic shock', 'Neurogenic shock follows spinal cord injury and is associated with poor tissue perfusion caused by loss of sympathetic tone. Consider it when hypotension occurs with features such as warm peripheries or vasodilation, while assessing for other causes of shock.'],
      ['15', 'Respiratory effects', 'Damage to the upper cervical cord can paralyse the diaphragm and respiratory muscles. Assess respiratory effort, rate, oxygenation and fatigue, and be prepared for deterioration in a patient with suspected high spinal injury.'],
      ['16', 'Cauda equina syndrome', 'Cauda equina syndrome is severe compression or inflammation of spinal nerves in the lowest part of the spinal canal. Onset may be acute over hours or days or chronic. It is a surgical emergency requiring urgent decompression.'],
      ['17', 'Cauda equina red flags', 'In the presence of lower-back or sciatic-type lower-limb pain, red flags include numbness in the groin, perineum or buttocks, bladder or bowel dysfunction, sexual dysfunction, lower-limb weakness or sensory deficit, disturbed gait or inability to walk and reduced or absent lower-limb reflexes.'],
      ['18', 'Spinal signs and symptoms', 'Possible findings include pain anywhere along the spine, loss of sensation or movement in the limbs and altered sensation in the trunk or limbs, including pins and needles, burning or electric-shock sensations. Compare both sides and document onset and progression.'],
      ['19', 'Associated physiological signs', 'Associated features may indicate the level or severity of injury. The deck includes hypotension with bradycardia in cervical-spine injury, diaphragmatic or abnormal breathing, hypotension with warm peripheries or vasodilation, flaccid muscles with absent reflexes and priapism.'],
      ['20', 'Initial spinal management', 'Prioritise safety and use C-ABCDE. Maintain cervical-spine control with a cervical collar or manual in-line stabilisation where indicated. Extricate the patient to a safe place for assessment, assess and manage pain and use the immobilisation algorithm.'],
      ['21', 'Immobilisation and extrication', 'Immobilise the entire spine if indicated using appropriate equipment such as a collar, blocks and scoop. Rescue or spinal boards are for extrication only. If full immobilisation is not possible, use padding and support. Self-extrication may be acceptable when the required conditions are met and it is safe.'],
      ['22', 'Referral and transport', 'Use the major trauma triage tool to determine conveyance and pre-alert the receiving hospital when necessary. If time permits, complete a secondary survey, but do not delay transfer of a time-critical patient. Convey smoothly and continue assessment en route.'],
      ['23', 'Management complications', 'Potential complications of managing spinal injury include airway problems and aspiration, increased intracranial pressure, restricted respiration, dysphagia, skin ulceration or pressure sores and pain. Reassess positioning, airway, breathing, skin and comfort throughout care.'],
      ['24', 'Major trauma referral', 'Patients with suspected spinal injury and abnormal neurology should be transferred to a major trauma centre. Use the major trauma pathway and continue to maintain spinal precautions and neurological assessment during transport.'],
      ['25', 'Documentation', 'Document whether manual in-line stabilisation was used, how the patient was extricated, the findings from the immobilisation algorithm, whether the entire spine or only the cervical spine was assessed, the equipment used and which step of the major trauma tool was triggered.'],
      ['26', 'Key learning points', 'When in doubt, treat for spinal injury, maintain cervical-spine control until the patient is cleared or fully immobilised, use the JRCALC immobilisation algorithm, minimise movement, recognise pelvic haemorrhage and cauda equina red flags, refer abnormal neurology to a major trauma centre and transport smoothly.']
    ]
  },
  'musculoskeletal-injuries': {
    source: 'L3 Musculoskeletal Injuries V3 May 2022.pdf',
    intro: 'Musculoskeletal injuries range from sprains and strains to fractures, dislocations, de-gloving injuries and amputations. Patients are often distressed and in significant pain, but dramatic limb injuries must not distract from airway, breathing, circulation or spinal threats. This topic covers mechanism of injury, assessment, neurovascular checks, limb protection, splinting, time-critical haemorrhage and complications. Follow current JRCALC Plus and local guidance within scope.',
    sections: [
      ['01', 'Topic overview', 'Musculoskeletal injuries include injuries to bones, joints, ligaments, tendons and muscles. The deck highlights sprains, fractures, dislocations, strains, amputations and partial amputations. Early pain management should be considered when clinically appropriate, while the primary survey remains the priority.'],
      ['02', 'Mechanism of injury', 'Determine the exact mechanism of injury (MOI). Build a mental image of the direction, magnitude and duration of the force applied to the limb or joint. A careful MOI assessment helps predict injury patterns and guides examination, immobilisation and escalation.'],
      ['03', 'Direct force', 'A direct force may cause a bone to break at the point of impact when the force is too great for the soft tissues to absorb and dissipate. Look for local pain, swelling, deformity and wounds, but continue to assess for less visible life-threatening injuries.'],
      ['04', 'Indirect force', 'An indirect force is transmitted through the skeleton until it reaches a structurally weak point, causing a bone or bones to fracture. The visible injury may not be at the point where the force was applied, so examine the whole limb and consider associated injuries.'],
      ['05', 'Do not be distracted', 'Dramatic musculoskeletal injuries can distract from airway obstruction, compromised breathing, spinal injury or other circulation problems. Use a structured C-ABCDE or ABCDE assessment and correct immediate life threats before focusing on detailed limb management.'],
      ['06', 'Amputation and partial amputation', 'Re-implantation or reconstruction may be possible if the amputated part is maintained and transported in the best condition possible. Protect the patient from haemorrhage, contamination, hypothermia and pain while preparing the amputated part for transport.'],
      ['07', 'Handling an amputated part', 'Remove gross contamination without damaging tissue. Cover the part with a moist field dressing, place it in a sealed plastic bag and place the bag on ice. Do not put the body part into direct contact with ice because freezing or cold injury can damage tissue.'],
      ['08', 'Managing the patient with amputation', 'Do not irrigate grossly contaminated wounds with saline. Immobilise a partially amputated limb in normal anatomical alignment, dress the injured limb where possible to prevent further contamination and apply a saline-soaked dressing covered with an occlusive layer. Provide pain management within scope and request senior support for advanced analgesia when needed.'],
      ['09', 'Fractures', 'A fracture is a break or breaks in a bone, caused when kinetic energy is sufficient to deform or disrupt the bone. Assess the mechanism, pain, swelling, erythema, deformity, wounds and neurovascular status. Do not allow an obvious fracture to distract from the primary survey.'],
      ['10', 'Closed fractures', 'A closed or simple fracture is a fracture where the skin has not been punctured. A closed fracture can still cause significant haemorrhage, neurovascular injury, pain, swelling and compartment complications, so treat it seriously and reassess after handling or splinting.'],
      ['11', 'Open fractures', 'A compound or open fracture has a wound at the injury site, caused for example by bone pushing through the skin. Open fractures carry contamination and infection risks. Protect the wound with an appropriate dressing, minimise further contamination and escalate for definitive care.'],
      ['12', 'Pathological fractures', 'Pathological fractures may occur after relatively minor trauma when bone is weakened. Causes highlighted include osteoporosis and cancers involving bone, such as breast, lung or prostate cancer. The mechanism may appear minor, but the injury and patient still require careful assessment and appropriate conveyance.'],
      ['13', 'Dislocations', 'A dislocation is abnormal separation of joint surfaces. It may occur alone or with a fracture, and muscle spasm can cause the joint to lock. Assess the limb and distal neurovascular status, protect the joint and follow local guidance regarding manipulation or reduction.'],
      ['14', 'Sprains and strains', 'Sprains and strains involve soft tissues around a joint without penetrating the joint capsule. A sprain is a partial or complete tear of a ligament. A strain, or pull, is a stretch or tear of muscle fibres or tendon.'],
      ['15', 'Assume fracture when uncertain', 'In the prehospital setting it may be difficult to distinguish a sprain from a fracture. The deck advises assuming there is a fracture and immobilising accordingly when the diagnosis is uncertain. Reassess motor, sensory and circulation status before and after immobilisation.'],
      ['16', 'Initial management', 'Assess C-ABCDE and identify time-critical features. Correct catastrophic haemorrhage, airway, breathing and circulation problems, then establish the MOI and examine the limb. Consider early analgesia, look and feel for injury, assess movement when appropriate and apply suitable immobilisation or splintage.'],
      ['17', 'Motor, sensory and circulation assessment', 'Assess motor function by asking the patient to move the limb when safe. Assess sensation with light touch. Assess circulation by checking a distal pulse and skin temperature. Document the findings before and after manipulation, handling or splinting.'],
      ['18', 'Removing constriction', 'Remove jewellery or constrictive clothing from the injured limb because swelling may develop. Do this early where possible, while maintaining alignment and avoiding unnecessary movement. Record any items removed and reassess the distal neurovascular status.'],
      ['19', 'The six P\'s of ischaemia', 'The six P\'s are pain, pallor, paralysis, paraesthesia, pulselessness and perishing cold. Pain out of proportion or not relieved by splinting or analgesia, loss of movement, altered sensation, absent pulses or a cold limb are warning signs of compromised circulation and require urgent escalation.'],
      ['20', 'Why splinting helps', 'Splinting can reduce pain, haemorrhage, further damage from bone fragments, muscle spasm and pressure on skin or adjacent neurovascular structures. It provides support and may reduce the risk of fat embolism. Apply it carefully and reassess the limb after application.'],
      ['21', 'Basic immobilisation principle', 'Assess and reassess motor, sensory and circulation status before and after manipulation or handling. Immobilise the joints above and below a fracture where appropriate. Maintain alignment, protect the skin and avoid worsening neurovascular compromise.'],
      ['22', 'Time-critical limb injuries', 'Use the Major Trauma Tool and transfer to a major trauma centre when the patient has time-critical features, continuing management en route. Mid-shaft femoral fractures are time critical because blood loss can be considerable. The deck also highlights substantial blood loss from open femoral fractures and pelvic fractures.'],
      ['23', 'Femoral and pelvic blood loss', 'The deck states that a mid-shaft femoral fracture may involve approximately 500-2000 mL of blood loss, with more possible in an open fracture. It also states that 1000 mL or more may be lost from a pelvic fracture. Monitor for shock and do not underestimate apparently closed injuries.'],
      ['24', 'Traction splint', 'A traction splint should be applied quickly when it does not delay transfer. If it cannot be applied promptly, use manual traction according to local guidance. Once traction has been applied, it should not be released during the immediate management described in the deck.'],
      ['25', 'Choosing splintage', 'Splintage depends on the injury. Examples in the deck include padding and a figure-of-eight around the ankles for a neck-of-femur fracture, a broad bandage for a shaft-of-femur fracture, long-leg box or vacuum splints for lower-limb injuries and short-leg splints for ankle or foot fractures. Follow current local equipment guidance.'],
      ['26', 'Upper-limb splintage', 'The deck gives self-splintage for a clavicle injury, a triangular sling for a humerus injury, a vacuum splint for a forearm fracture and a short box splint for an ulna injury. Reassess distal motor, sensory and circulation status after applying the selected support.'],
      ['27', 'Sprain and strain care', 'For strains and sprains, consider a cold compress and limb elevation where appropriate. Continue to assess for fracture, dislocation, neurovascular compromise and significant mechanism because a prehospital diagnosis of a simple sprain may be unreliable.'],
      ['28', 'Complications', 'Complications include bleeding, damage to blood vessels, nerves, muscles or arteries, infection, tissue loss, long-term disability and compartment syndrome. Pain out of proportion, increasing swelling, altered sensation, loss of movement, absent pulses or a cold limb should prompt urgent reassessment and escalation.'],
      ['29', 'Pain and transport', 'Musculoskeletal injuries commonly cause distress and significant pain. Consider early pain management within scope, immobilise to reduce movement and pain, monitor the patient and transport to appropriate definitive care. Continue reassessment on route, especially after analgesia or splinting.'],
      ['30', 'Key learning points', 'Complete C-ABCDE before focusing on the limb, establish direction, magnitude and duration of force, assess motor-sensory-circulation status before and after handling, manage amputated parts correctly, assume fracture when uncertain, splint appropriately, recognise the six P\'s and time-critical femoral or pelvic injuries, and escalate early when major trauma or neurovascular compromise is suspected.']
    ]
  },
  'neurovascular-conditions': {
    source: 'L3 Neurovascular Conditions V4 May 2022.pdf',
    intro: 'Neurovascular and neurological presentations range from long-term conditions to rapidly time-critical emergencies. This topic covers dementia, Parkinson\'s disease, multiple sclerosis, ataxia, paralysis, cerebrovascular events, transient ischaemic attack, convulsions, meningococcal disease and coma. Start with a structured primary survey, identify reversible threats, recognise red flags and transport or escalate promptly when the patient is time critical.',
    sections: [
      ['01', 'Topic overview', 'The session covers a range of neurovascular conditions encountered in prehospital care: Alzheimer\'s disease and dementia, Parkinson\'s disease, multiple sclerosis, ataxia, paralysis, cerebrovascular events, convulsions, meningitis or meningococcal disease and coma. These conditions may produce altered consciousness, weakness, speech disturbance, impaired coordination, seizures or airway and breathing problems.'],
      ['02', 'Dementia', 'Dementia affects memory, thinking and daily life. The deck identifies age, genetics and lifestyle as common risk groups and notes that medication may help control the condition but there is no known cure. Establish the patient\'s usual cognitive and functional baseline before deciding that a change is neurological deterioration.'],
      ['03', 'Parkinson\'s disease', 'Parkinson\'s disease develops as parts of the brain become damaged over many years. It is mainly treated with medication and physiotherapy. Ask about the patient\'s usual mobility, speech, cognition, medication schedule and baseline function, because an acute change may indicate another illness or complication.'],
      ['04', 'Multiple sclerosis', 'Multiple sclerosis affects the brain and spinal cord and interferes with the correct transmission of nerve impulses. The deck notes that it mainly affects women, is a common cause of disability in younger adults and has no cure, although medication can help manage it. Compare the current presentation with the patient\'s usual neurological function.'],
      ['05', 'Ataxia', 'Ataxia is a group of disorders affecting balance, coordination and speech. It is mainly hereditary but can develop later in life. There is no cure, but patients may respond well to physiotherapy and medication. New or worsening ataxia still requires assessment for acute neurological causes such as stroke or intoxication.'],
      ['06', 'Paralysis and paresis', 'Paralysis is partial or complete loss of muscle function. Plegia refers to paralysis, while paresis describes muscle weakness or incomplete loss of muscle function. Record what is affected, when it started, whether it is new and whether there are associated speech, facial, sensory or consciousness changes.'],
      ['07', 'Terms describing distribution', 'Monoplegia means paralysis of one limb. Hemiplegia means paralysis of one side of the body. Paraplegia means paralysis of both lower limbs, while quadriplegia or tetraplegia means paralysis of all four limbs. These terms describe distribution and do not by themselves identify the cause.'],
      ['08', 'Cerebrovascular event', 'A cerebrovascular event (CVE), commonly referred to as stroke in the deck, occurs when blood supply to the brain is interrupted by a bleed or blockage. Signs and symptoms often develop rapidly and may last for at least 24 hours. Stroke is a major cause of disability, so early recognition and transport are important.'],
      ['09', 'CVE risk factors', 'Non-modifiable risk factors include age, sex, ethnicity and family history. Modifiable risk factors include hypertension, smoking, atrial fibrillation, diabetes, diet, physical activity, alcohol consumption, high blood cholesterol, obesity and previous stroke, TIA or myocardial infarction. Risk factors support suspicion but do not rule stroke in or out.'],
      ['10', 'Transient ischaemic attack', 'A transient ischaemic attack (TIA) produces signs and symptoms that suggest stroke but resolve within 24 hours, usually within minutes or a few hours. If neurological signs are continuing during your management, treat the patient as having a stroke and follow the time-critical pathway.'],
      ['11', 'CVE symptoms', 'Symptoms can include numbness, weakness or paralysis, slurred speech, blurred vision, confusion or inability to communicate and severe headache. Symptoms may be subtle or occur together. Establish the time the patient was last known to be well and identify any new neurological deficit.'],
      ['12', 'ABCDE and FAST', 'Perform ABCDE for every patient. Ambulance crews use the FAST test for suspected stroke. If a patient fails one or more aspects, they are FAST positive and are regarded as having a stroke for the purpose of urgent assessment and referral.'],
      ['13', 'Facial weakness', 'Ask the patient to smile or show their teeth and look for new lack of symmetry. If you are unsure, ask someone who knows the patient because a subtle change may be easier to identify against the usual facial appearance.'],
      ['14', 'Arm weakness', 'Ask the patient to lift both arms together with palms uppermost, close their eyes and hold the position for five seconds. Look for one arm drifting or falling. Compare both sides and record whether the weakness is new, persistent or associated with other neurological signs.'],
      ['15', 'Speech impairment', 'Look for new speech disturbance, including slurred speech, difficulty finding words, difficulty constructing sentences or complete inability to speak. Speech impairment can be subtle, so compare with a reliable baseline when possible and ask someone who knows the patient if you are unsure.'],
      ['16', 'Why time matters', 'The deck highlights that approximately 1.9 million neurons are destroyed with each passing minute during stroke. Prompt treatment at a hyper-acute stroke unit (HASU) can save lives and reduce long-term disability. Record symptom onset or last-known-well time accurately and avoid unnecessary delay.'],
      ['17', 'CVE referral and transport', 'A patient is time critical if they have a major ABC problem or a positive FAST test. A major ABC problem requires the nearest emergency department, while a FAST-positive patient should be taken to a HASU. Transport to the correct receiving unit as soon as practicable and continue assessment en route.'],
      ['18', 'CVE management options', 'Protect the airway and assist ventilation if the respiratory rate is below 10, above 30 or ventilation is inadequate. Give oxygen only when hypoxic, with the deck identifying an SpO2 below 94% as the trigger. Keep the patient nil by mouth, position them comfortably, provide reassurance and tell relatives or friends where the patient is being taken.'],
      ['19', 'Convulsions and epilepsy', 'Convulsion, seizure and fit describe involuntary contraction and relaxation of muscles, often associated with a reduced or altered level of consciousness. Epilepsy describes a tendency to recurrent unprovoked convulsions. Not every seizure is caused by epilepsy, so consider other causes and the patient\'s context.'],
      ['20', 'Types of convulsion', 'Generalised convulsions affect a large part of the cerebral cortex. Partial or focal convulsions affect a localised area but may progress to become generalised. Focal presentations may include altered consciousness, behaviour or mood changes, repetitive actions or localised tonic-clonic activity.'],
      ['21', 'Generalised seizures', 'Absence seizures may look like staring or daydreaming, sometimes with repetitive eye blinking. They usually last a few seconds with immediate recovery. Bilateral tonic-clonic seizures have a tonic phase of body-wide rigidity followed by a clonic phase of regular jerking. Loss of consciousness, tongue biting, incontinence and a prolonged sleepy or disorientated post-ictal phase may occur.'],
      ['22', 'Status epilepticus', 'The deck defines status epilepticus as continuous convulsions lasting five minutes or longer, or a series of three or more convulsions in an hour close together without a recovery period. This is time critical. Request appropriate senior or paramedic support, protect the patient, assess ABCD and plan urgent transport.'],
      ['23', 'Convulsion causes', 'Causes other than epilepsy include hypoxia, hypoglycaemia, alcohol, arrhythmias, recreational drugs, eclampsia, kidney failure, systemic infection, brain infection, tumour and stroke. Consider trauma, medication, pregnancy where relevant and the wider scene. A seizure may be the presenting sign of a reversible life threat.'],
      ['24', 'Convulsion assessment and management', 'Use AVPU to assess whether the patient is actively convulsing, post-ictal or recovered. Protect a patient with reduced consciousness from injury and consider the recovery position. Do not use an oropharyngeal airway in an actively convulsing patient. Assess airway, breathing, circulation, disability and exposure, check blood glucose promptly, look for injuries or infection and reassess.'],
      ['25', 'Convulsion red flags', 'A patient is time critical when there is a major ABCD problem, serious head injury, status epilepticus or underlying infection. The deck also emphasises the rule: the first, the worst or the longest seizure should be transferred to hospital. Continue assessment and management during transport.'],
      ['26', 'Meningococcal disease', 'Meningococcal disease is a general term for bacterial infection of the meninges or infection of the bloodstream. Assessment may reveal reduced response, acute airway difficulty, breathing problems, circulation problems or neurological signs such as photophobia. Consider meningococcal disease in an unwell patient even when the presentation is not classic.'],
      ['27', 'Meningococcal rash', 'Expose the patient appropriately and look for a haemorrhagic, non-blanching rash. In an unwell patient, a non-blanching rash suggests meningococcal septicaemia. However, absence of a rash does not exclude meningococcal disease; the deck specifically notes that there may be no rash.'],
      ['28', 'Meningococcal assessment', 'Assess temperature and ask about vomiting, diarrhoea, abdominal pain and joint or muscle pain. A non-blanching rash or any suspicion of meningococcal septicaemia makes the patient time critical. Transport to hospital as soon as practicable and continue assessment en route.'],
      ['29', 'Coma and reduced consciousness', 'Coma is described as absence or loss of consciousness, with a GCS of 8 or below. The cause may not be immediately identifiable and management may initially be supportive, but some causes are reversible by ambulance clinicians. Treat airway, breathing and circulation problems immediately while searching for the cause.'],
      ['30', 'Causes and assessment of coma', 'Possible causes include stroke, convulsion, epilepsy, hypoglycaemia, hyperglycaemia, subarachnoid haemorrhage, overdose, meningitis, inadequate airway, inadequate respiration, persistent hyperventilation, hypovolaemia, cardiac arrhythmias, clinical shock, raised intracranial pressure, carbon monoxide and hypothermia. Obtain comprehensive vital signs, document GCS, assess pupils, perform a secondary survey, obtain a 12-lead ECG, consider trauma and reassess frequently.'],
      ['31', 'Key learning points', 'Use ABCDE for all neurological presentations. Recognise FAST-positive stroke and continuing neurological signs as time critical, transport to the correct unit, identify reversible seizure and coma causes, never assume a seizure is epilepsy, look for meningococcal disease even without a rash, protect the airway and patient from injury and continue reassessment throughout transport.']
    ]
  },
  'poisoning': {
    source: 'L3 Poisoning v2 March 2022.pdf',
    intro: 'Poisoning is exposure to a substance in a quantity that may cause illness or death. Exposure may be intentional, accidental or non-accidental, and poisons may enter through inhalation, ingestion, injection or absorption. Use a primary survey, identify the substance and route, decide whether the patient is time critical, begin appropriate treatment and seek specialist advice. Follow current JRCALC and local guidance for specific poisons.',
    sections: [
      ['01', 'What is a poison?', 'The deck defines a poison as exposure to a substance that may result in morbidity or mortality. Poisoning occurs when the quantity or exposure to a substance is sufficient to cause harm. The substance, dose, route, time and patient factors all influence risk.'],
      ['02', 'Intent and safeguarding', 'Poisoning may be intentional, accidental or non-accidental. Intentional exposure may represent self-harm, while non-accidental exposure may raise safeguarding concerns. Ask sensitively about what happened, preserve relevant information and escalate safeguarding concerns through the appropriate pathway.'],
      ['03', 'Routes of exposure', 'Poisons may enter the body by inhalation, ingestion, injection or absorption. Inhalation includes gases and fumes, ingestion includes liquids and solids, injection includes needles or bites, and absorption may occur through the eyes or skin.'],
      ['04', 'Inhalation', 'Inhaled poisons include gases and fumes. Consider the environment, ventilation, other affected people and whether the patient remains close to the source. Scene safety and removal from ongoing exposure come before entering or treating in a contaminated area.'],
      ['05', 'Ingestion', 'Ingested poisons may be liquids or solids, including household products, medicines, recreational substances, alcohol, chemicals, cosmetics, plants or fungi. Establish what was taken, how much, when, whether anything else was taken and whether the container or packaging is available.'],
      ['06', 'Injection and bites', 'Injection exposure may involve needles or bites. Consider the substance, route, puncture or bite site, contamination risk and any additional trauma. Protect the crew, avoid contact with contaminated sharps and seek specialist advice for the substance involved.'],
      ['07', 'Absorption', 'Absorption can occur through the eyes or skin. Identify the substance, remove the patient from the source and avoid exposing the crew. Follow current decontamination and treatment guidance for the specific chemical or product.'],
      ['08', 'Common types of poison', 'The deck lists household products, pharmaceutical or recreational substances, plants or fungi, alcohol, chemicals and cosmetics. Do not assume that a familiar product is harmless: identify the substance, concentration, amount and route where possible.'],
      ['09', 'Primary survey', 'Begin with a primary survey and manage immediate airway, breathing and circulation problems. Consider whether the patient is still being exposed, whether the scene is safe and whether other people are affected. Reassess because poisoning can deteriorate quickly.'],
      ['10', 'History of poisoning', 'Obtain a focused history of the substance, amount, route, time of exposure, intent, symptoms and treatment already given. Ask about other substances, alcohol, prescribed medicines, medical conditions, pregnancy where relevant and the patient’s usual level of function.'],
      ['11', 'Time-critical assessment', 'Evaluate whether the poisoned patient is time critical. Concerning features may include airway or breathing compromise, reduced consciousness, seizures, severe physiological disturbance, ongoing exposure or a substance known to cause rapid deterioration. Request senior support and specialist advice early.'],
      ['12', 'Treatment principles', 'Management should be based on identifying the poison, using specific treatment for the specific poison when available and obtaining rapid access to hospital. Avoid guessing the antidote or treatment, and follow current JRCALC, specialist toxicology and local guidance.'],
      ['13', 'Information for advice and handover', 'Keep packaging, labels, medication lists or other reliable substance information when safe to do so. Record the estimated amount, time, route, symptoms, observations, interventions and response. This information helps specialist advisers and the receiving hospital select appropriate treatment.'],
      ['14', 'Carbon monoxide', 'Consider carbon-monoxide poisoning when the patient is in a confined space with impaired ventilation or when a boiler may be defective. Carbon monoxide is an inhaled poison, so assess the environment and consider whether other people may also be affected.'],
      ['15', 'Carbon-monoxide safety', 'Remove the patient from the source before treatment, but do not enter an unsafe environment. Ensure the scene is safe and consider specialist assistance if the source or atmosphere remains hazardous. Protect the crew from becoming additional casualties.'],
      ['16', 'Carbon-monoxide treatment', 'The deck advises continuous high-concentration oxygen after removal from the source. It also states that SpO2 monitoring has no value in this situation, so do not use a reassuring pulse-oximeter reading to exclude carbon-monoxide exposure. Follow current specialist guidance.'],
      ['17', 'Carbon-monoxide presentation', 'Carbon-monoxide poisoning may cause disorientation, reduced consciousness or unconsciousness. Symptoms can be non-specific, so connect the presentation with the environment, ventilation, possible boiler defect and whether other occupants are affected.'],
      ['18', 'Transport and escalation', 'After the primary survey and appropriate immediate treatment, transport the patient for further care. Keep on-scene time appropriate, request senior support and use specialist poisoning advice when required. Provide a clear pre-alert for time-critical patients or hazardous exposures.'],
      ['19', 'Scope and specialist advice', 'Poisoning management depends on the specific substance and route. Begin treatment within scope, seek advice for substance-specific interventions and do not administer an antidote or treatment simply because the presentation resembles another poisoning.'],
      ['20', 'Key learning points', 'Prioritise scene safety and the primary survey, identify the poison and route, assess intent and safeguarding, decide whether the patient is time critical, remove ongoing exposure, use specific treatment only with appropriate guidance, seek specialist advice and transport rapidly for definitive care.']
    ]
  },
  'urinary-conditions': {
    source: 'L3 Urinary Conditions  v1 September 2020.pdf',
    intro: 'Urinary conditions range from painful but self-limiting problems to time-critical obstruction, infection and kidney injury. This topic covers acute urinary retention, urinary-tract infection, renal calculi, acute kidney injury and chronic kidney disease. Use the history, pain assessment, urine symptoms, vital signs, hydration and sepsis assessment to identify deterioration, then follow current JRCALC and local guidance within scope.',
    sections: [
      ['01', 'Topic overview', 'The supplied lesson covers acute urinary retention, urinary-tract infection, kidney stones, acute kidney injury and chronic renal failure or chronic kidney disease. These conditions may overlap: obstruction can damage the kidneys, infection can become systemic and reduced renal function can affect fluid, electrolyte and cardiovascular stability.'],
      ['02', 'Acute urinary retention', 'Urinary retention is the inability to empty the bladder and may be acute or chronic. Acute urinary retention is a medical emergency. Ask when the patient last passed urine, whether the problem is worsening, the volume and flow of urine and whether there is severe lower-abdominal pain or swelling.'],
      ['03', 'Causes of urinary retention', 'Causes highlighted in the deck include benign prostate enlargement, urethral stricture from inflammation or scar tissue, urinary-tract stones, nerve problems after vaginal childbirth or spinal or pelvic injury, and age-related difficulty emptying the bladder.'],
      ['04', 'Retention presentation', 'Acute retention may cause severe lower-abdominal pain, inability to pass urine, reduced flow, signs of infection and a swollen bladder. Treat the presentation as urgent because prolonged obstruction can cause complications beyond discomfort.'],
      ['05', 'Complications of retention', 'Without prompt treatment, urine may pass back up towards the kidneys and damage them. Consider acute kidney injury, infection, pain and deterioration. Escalate promptly and convey for assessment and bladder drainage according to the appropriate pathway.'],
      ['06', 'Urinary-tract infection', 'A urinary-tract infection develops when part of the urinary tract becomes infected, usually by bacteria. It may affect the lower urinary tract, including the bladder and urethra, or the upper urinary tract, including the kidneys and ureters.'],
      ['07', 'UTI in men and women', 'The deck states that UTIs mainly occur in women and are less common in men. A UTI in a man therefore requires investigation for an underlying cause such as urethral stricture, previous sexually transmitted infection, bladder stones or a prostate problem.'],
      ['08', 'Lower and upper UTI', 'Lower UTI affects the bladder or urethra. Upper UTI affects the kidneys or ureters and is potentially more serious because of the risk of kidney damage. Fever, rigors, vomiting or systemic illness should raise concern for an upper or complicated infection.'],
      ['09', 'UTI symptoms', 'Symptoms may include pain or burning when urinating, dysuria, increased frequency, cramping pain in the lower abdomen or back, strong-smelling urine and confusion in an older patient. Haematuria may appear as cloudy urine or frank blood.'],
      ['10', 'Possible kidney infection', 'A temperature above 38 degrees Celsius, rigors and vomiting may suggest kidney infection in the context of urinary symptoms. Assess for sepsis, measure observations, check the patient’s general appearance and escalate rapidly when the patient is systemically unwell.'],
      ['11', 'Kidney stones', 'Kidney stones, or renal calculi, are stone-like lumps that can form in one or both kidneys. A stone can move down a ureter and stretch its wall. Small stones may pass painlessly and go unnoticed, while a larger stone can block part of the urinary system.'],
      ['12', 'Renal colic', 'Renal colic is intense pain in the back or side of the abdomen that may occur in episodes lasting minutes or hours. Patients may feel restless and unable to lie still. Pain can radiate towards the lower abdomen or groin and may be accompanied by nausea or vomiting.'],
      ['13', 'Kidney-stone symptoms', 'Possible symptoms include a persistent ache in the lower back, side or groin, intense intermittent pain, increased frequency, dysuria, nausea or vomiting and blood in the urine. A stone can also lead to a urinary-tract infection, so assess for fever and systemic illness.'],
      ['14', 'Acute kidney injury', 'Acute kidney injury is a sudden episode of kidney failure or kidney damage occurring within hours or days. It ranges from a minor loss of function to complete kidney failure. Waste products build up in the blood and the kidneys cannot maintain the correct fluid balance.'],
      ['15', 'Why AKI is urgent', 'AKI must be detected early and treated promptly. Abnormal levels of salts and chemicals can build up and affect the function of other organs. Complete kidney shutdown may require temporary dialysis and can be fatal.'],
      ['16', 'AKI symptoms', 'Possible findings include nausea or vomiting, very low urine output, diarrhoea, dehydration, high blood pressure, confusion, drowsiness, abdominal pain and back ache. The deck gives less than 100 ml of urine in 24 hours as a concerning measure of reduced output.'],
      ['17', 'Reduced renal blood flow', 'Most AKI in the deck is described as being caused by reduced blood flow to the kidneys. Causes include low blood volume, reduced cardiac output, blood-vessel problems and certain medicines. Assess hydration, circulation, medication history and other possible causes.'],
      ['18', 'Obstructive AKI', 'AKI can result from a blockage affecting urine drainage. Examples include an enlarged prostate, a pelvic tumour such as an ovarian or bladder tumour, and kidney stones. Ask about retention, reduced flow, pain, previous obstruction and known disease.'],
      ['19', 'AKI treatment pathways', 'Treatment depends on the cause and severity. Hospital management may include fluids when dehydration is present, antibiotics when infection is present, stopping certain medicines until the problem resolves and urinary catheterisation when a blockage is causing retention. Prehospital clinicians should follow current scope and guidance.'],
      ['20', 'AKI complications', 'Serious complications include high potassium, which can cause muscle weakness, paralysis and heart-rhythm problems, pulmonary oedema from fluid overload and metabolic acidosis. Acidosis can cause nausea, vomiting, drowsiness and breathlessness. These complications require urgent escalation and monitoring.'],
      ['21', 'Chronic kidney disease', 'Chronic renal failure, also called chronic kidney disease, is a long-term condition in which the kidneys do not function as well as they should. It is commonly associated with ageing but can affect anyone. Early disease may cause no symptoms.'],
      ['22', 'Advanced CKD symptoms', 'At a more advanced stage, CKD may cause tiredness, swollen ankles, feet or hands, shortness of breath, feeling sick, nausea or vomiting, blood in the urine and hypertension. Consider fluid overload, anaemia, infection and cardiac complications in the wider assessment.'],
      ['23', 'Causes of CKD', 'The deck identifies high blood pressure, diabetes, high cholesterol, kidney infections, glomerulonephritis, polycystic kidney disease, urinary obstruction and long-term regular use of certain medicines as causes or associated conditions.'],
      ['24', 'CKD treatment and risk', 'Treatment depends on cause and severity and may include lifestyle change, medicines for associated problems, dialysis or kidney transplant. Even mild CKD increases the risk of serious problems such as cardiovascular disease, so record the history and medication accurately.'],
      ['25', 'Prehospital management', 'Use oxygen according to JRCALC, provide pain relief as required and consider senior assistance. Monitor observations continuously or at appropriate intervals, consider sepsis and follow relevant guidance, obtain a 12-lead ECG when indicated, particularly in an older patient or one with cardiac risk factors and upper-abdominal pain, and transfer for further care.']
    ]
  },
  'addisons-disease': {
    source: 'L3 Addisions v1 September 2020.pdf',
    intro: 'Adrenal insufficiency is a rare, potentially life-threatening but treatable condition. In primary adrenal insufficiency, also called Addison\'s disease, damage to the adrenal cortex reduces production of cortisol and aldosterone. Secondary adrenal insufficiency may result from pituitary dysfunction or prolonged steroid exposure. During illness, injury or other stress, the body may be unable to increase cortisol production and an adrenal crisis can develop rapidly. Follow current JRCALC and local guidance and request senior clinical support early.',
    sections: [
      ['01', 'What is adrenal insufficiency?', 'Adrenal insufficiency occurs when the body cannot produce enough essential adrenal hormones to meet its needs. The deck describes it as rare, potentially deadly but treatable. Acute deterioration can be rapid, particularly when the patient is under physiological stress.'],
      ['02', 'Addison\'s disease', 'Addison\'s disease is another name for primary adrenal insufficiency. Damage to the adrenal cortex means the adrenal glands cannot produce enough cortisol and aldosterone. The adrenal glands are small glands located above the kidneys.'],
      ['03', 'Primary adrenal insufficiency', 'Primary adrenal insufficiency results from damage to the adrenal cortex itself. Because the gland is damaged, production of both cortisol and aldosterone may be inadequate. Ask about a known diagnosis, steroid replacement and the patient\'s usual emergency plan.'],
      ['04', 'Secondary adrenal insufficiency', 'Secondary adrenal insufficiency may occur when a dysfunctional pituitary gland fails to signal the adrenal glands to produce cortisol. The deck also identifies long-term or high-dose steroid intake as a possible cause of adrenal suppression.'],
      ['05', 'Cortisol', 'Cortisol helps the body respond to stress and contributes to blood-pressure regulation, immune-system function and the balance between cortisol and insulin effects. During illness or injury, the body normally increases cortisol production; adrenal insufficiency limits that response.'],
      ['06', 'Aldosterone', 'Aldosterone helps regulate salts in the blood and contributes to blood-pressure control. Reduced aldosterone production can contribute to fluid and electrolyte disturbance and poor circulatory stability during adrenal crisis.'],
      ['07', 'Hormone functions together', 'Cortisol and aldosterone help regulate blood pressure, support immune function, balance the effects of insulin, assist the stress response and regulate salts in the blood. A deficit can therefore affect several body systems at once.'],
      ['08', 'Appearance and skin findings', 'The deck illustrates hyperpigmentation and vitiligo as possible clinical clues. Hyperpigmentation may be noticeable in skin creases or exposed areas. These findings are supportive rather than diagnostic, so combine them with the history and current presentation.'],
      ['09', 'History of acute deterioration', 'The deck identifies vomiting, diarrhoea and flu-like illness among reported histories associated with adrenal deterioration. Major infection, surgery, blackout or loss of consciousness, injury, severe pain, shock and inadequate medication may also be relevant.'],
      ['10', 'Triggers of adrenal crisis', 'Possible triggers include infection, hypoglycaemia, stress, traumatic injury, surgery, burns, pregnancy, medical emergencies such as myocardial infarction and sudden cessation of steroid treatment. Ask about recent illness, injury, procedures, medication changes and the patient\'s usual steroid plan.'],
      ['11', 'Steroid-related adrenal suppression', 'The deck warns that tablet or intramuscular steroid treatment can suppress the body\'s own cortisol production. It gives examples of dose and duration thresholds associated with risk and notes that the effect may persist for up to a year after stopping steroids. Confirm the actual medication, dose, duration and prescriber advice.'],
      ['12', 'Stress and cortisol demand', 'During infection, trauma, surgery, burns, pregnancy or another major physiological stress, the body should increase cortisol production. In adrenal insufficiency it may be unable to do so, and the patient may enter crisis. A relatively ordinary illness can therefore become dangerous.'],
      ['13', 'Adrenal crisis recognition', 'Suspect adrenal crisis in a patient with known or possible adrenal insufficiency who becomes acutely unwell after a trigger. Look for collapse, altered consciousness, hypotension or shock, hypoglycaemia, vomiting, diarrhoea, severe weakness and symptoms that do not fit a simple minor illness.'],
      ['14', 'Hypoglycaemia', 'Hypoglycaemia is listed as a possible trigger and feature requiring attention. Assess blood glucose in an unwell patient where indicated, look for confusion or reduced consciousness and manage hypoglycaemia within current scope and clinical guidance.'],
      ['15', 'Circulatory compromise', 'Cortisol and aldosterone contribute to blood-pressure regulation. During crisis, the patient may develop hypotension and circulatory instability. Assess circulation, capillary refill, heart rate, blood pressure and mental state, and treat immediate threats while requesting senior support.'],
      ['16', 'Primary assessment', 'Assess and manage the ABCs symptomatically. Identify airway, breathing and circulation problems, assess disability including consciousness and blood glucose, and expose the patient as needed to identify other causes or triggers. Continue reassessment because deterioration can be rapid.'],
      ['17', 'Oxygenation', 'The deck advises maintaining SpO2 above 94% during management. Apply current JRCALC and local oxygen guidance, consider the patient\'s overall clinical condition and monitor response rather than treating a number in isolation.'],
      ['18', 'Vascular access and senior care', 'The deck identifies IV access if indicated as a paramedic intervention. Request senior clinical support early when IV access, fluid treatment, steroid administration or management of shock is required. Do not perform interventions outside scope.'],
      ['19', 'Hydrocortisone', 'The deck lists 100 mg hydrocortisone IV or IM as a treatment intervention but states that it is not within AAP scope of practice. Follow the current clinical guideline and ensure an appropriately authorised clinician is requested when steroid treatment is indicated.'],
      ['20', 'Managing hypoglycaemia', 'The deck identifies IV glucose where required as a paramedic intervention. Assess glucose when clinically indicated, request the appropriate clinician and follow current guidance for treatment, reassessment and documentation.'],
      ['21', 'ECG and monitoring', 'Obtain an ECG as indicated and monitor vital signs continuously or at appropriate intervals. Trends in heart rate, blood pressure, respiratory status, oxygen saturation, mental state and glucose can show deterioration even when a single reading appears reassuring.'],
      ['22', 'Transport', 'Adrenal crisis requires rapid transport to the nearest suitable emergency department. Keep on-scene time appropriate to the patient\'s immediate needs, provide a clear pre-alert when indicated and communicate the known diagnosis, suspected trigger, steroid history, observations, glucose and interventions.'],
      ['23', 'Medication history', 'Ask what steroid medication the patient takes, the dose, when it was last taken and whether doses have been missed or recently stopped. Establish whether the patient carries an emergency plan, steroid card or other alert information, and include it in the handover.'],
      ['24', 'Differential diagnosis and associated illness', 'Adrenal crisis can resemble sepsis, hypoglycaemia, shock, gastroenteritis or another medical emergency. Do not anchor on Addison\'s disease alone. Identify and manage immediate ABC problems, assess likely triggers and consider concurrent infection, injury, myocardial infarction or other illness.'],
      ['25', 'Key learning points', 'Recognise primary and secondary adrenal insufficiency, ask about steroid exposure and emergency plans, identify infection, injury, surgery, burns, pregnancy, hypoglycaemia and sudden steroid cessation as possible triggers, assess ABCs and glucose, request senior support, follow current scope and guidance, and transport rapidly to a suitable emergency department.']
    ]
  },
  'head-injuries': {
    source: 'L3 Head Injuries v1 October 2020.pdf',
    intro: 'Head injuries can cause immediate primary brain damage and later secondary injury from hypoxia, hypotension, swelling and raised intracranial pressure. Use the mechanism, level of consciousness, pupils, airway and breathing assessment, cervical-spine precautions and repeated neurological observations to identify deterioration. Avoid harmful delay, request senior support early and follow current local clinical guidance for definitive care.',
    sections: [
      ['01', 'Common mechanisms', 'Common causes of traumatic brain injury include road traffic collisions, falls, assaults and direct blows to the head. The mechanism helps estimate the likelihood of significant injury and should be considered alongside the patient’s examination and observations.'],
      ['02', 'High-risk mechanisms and outcomes', 'The deck highlights penetrating injuries, non-accidental injury in children younger than five, pedestrians and pedal cyclists, and ejection from a vehicle as mechanisms associated with worse outcomes. These mechanisms should lower the threshold for senior support and definitive assessment.'],
      ['03', 'Traumatic brain injury', 'Traumatic brain injury is brain injury caused by trauma to the head. It may be obvious from the examination or may initially be subtle. Recognise the possibility of TBI from the mechanism, symptoms, altered consciousness, pupil findings and associated injuries.'],
      ['04', 'Primary brain injury', 'A primary brain injury occurs instantaneously as a result of the impact itself. Once it has occurred, the damage cannot be undone by prehospital treatment. Management therefore focuses on recognising the injury and preventing additional secondary brain injury.'],
      ['05', 'Secondary brain injury', 'Good prehospital management can influence secondary brain injury. The priorities are to recognise TBI, understand the relevant pathophysiology, maintain oxygenation and perfusion, manage the patient appropriately and transport promptly for definitive care.'],
      ['06', 'Complications of TBI', 'Complications highlighted in the deck include hypoxaemia, hypotension and intracerebral haematoma. These complications can reduce cerebral oxygen delivery and worsen neurological injury, so they require early recognition and treatment within scope.'],
      ['07', 'Scalp injuries', 'Scalp lacerations, contusions, haematomas and boggy areas may suggest head injury. The scalp is highly vascular, so injuries can look dramatic and may distract from deeper or associated injury. Significant blood loss is possible if an artery is damaged, and scalp injury in children can cause hypovolaemia.'],
      ['08', 'Skull fractures', 'A skull fracture indicates significant force and may occur with or without a scalp injury. The major concern is damage to the brain structures underneath. Look for visible fracture or deformity and manage the patient as having potentially serious head injury.'],
      ['09', 'Basilar skull-fracture signs', 'Periorbital ecchymosis, known as panda eyes, and bruising behind the ear over the mastoid process, known as Battle’s sign, may indicate a skull-base injury. These signs may not become apparent for several hours, so absence at the first assessment does not exclude injury.'],
      ['10', 'Cerebrospinal-fluid leakage', 'Blood or cerebrospinal fluid leaking from the nose or ears is a concerning sign of head injury. Do not attempt to stop the flow from the nose or ears. Protect the patient, maintain the airway and arrange prompt definitive assessment.'],
      ['11', 'Other suggestive symptoms', 'Symptoms that may suggest TBI include dizziness, nausea or vomiting, severe headache, visual disturbance, amnesia, repetitiveness, altered consciousness, abnormal pupils, paraesthesia or paralysis, convulsions, posturing and abnormal respirations.'],
      ['12', 'Pupil assessment', 'Frequently assess pupil size, equality and reactivity. Pupillary nerves are sensitive to changes in intracranial pressure. A sluggish pupil may be an early sign of raised ICP or cerebral hypoxia. Unequal or bilaterally fixed and dilated pupils are later, more ominous signs that may indicate pressure on the oculomotor nerves.'],
      ['13', 'Level of consciousness', 'The deck identifies level of consciousness as the best indicator of a head-injured patient’s condition. Monitor for a changing state, assess using AVPU and GCS where appropriate, examine pupil responses and repeat the assessment to identify deterioration.'],
      ['14', 'AVPU', 'AVPU provides a baseline neurological assessment of basic consciousness: alert, responds to voice, responds to pain or unresponsive. AVPU is useful for rapid assessment but is not a substitute for calculating a full GCS when a detailed neurological assessment is required.'],
      ['15', 'Glasgow Coma Scale', 'GCS provides an objective way to record conscious state during initial and subsequent assessments. It is based on eye opening, verbal response and motor response. The score ranges from 3 to 15, with a lower score indicating more severe brain injury.'],
      ['16', 'GCS injury classification', 'The deck classifies a GCS of 13–15 as mild traumatic brain injury, 8–12 as moderate traumatic brain injury and 3–8 as severe traumatic brain injury. The patient’s trend and changing level of consciousness are clinically important, not only the single score.'],
      ['17', 'Neurological stimulation', 'The deck illustrates trapezius squeeze and supra-orbital pressure as neurological-stimulation methods. Use only appropriate trained assessment techniques, record the response accurately and avoid causing unnecessary injury or distress.'],
      ['18', 'Posturing', 'Posturing is an abnormal motor response associated with serious neurological injury. It should be documented as part of the neurological assessment, alongside consciousness, pupils, airway, breathing and other signs of deterioration.'],
      ['19', 'Cervical-spine injury', 'Head injuries are often complicated by cervical-spine injury. Reduced consciousness has a significant association with cervical-spine injury, which may be easily overlooked. Assume cervical-spine injury where indicated and protect the spine in line with current guidance.'],
      ['20', 'Cerebral contusion', 'A cerebral contusion is bruising and swelling caused when the brain hits the inside of the skull. It may produce coup or contrecoup injury. Because the brain lies within a closed skull, swelling can increase pressure and reduce blood flow to brain tissue.'],
      ['21', 'Intracranial haemorrhage', 'Intracranial bleeding is a complication of TBI. The deck identifies extradural haemorrhage, acute or chronic subdural haemorrhage and subarachnoid haemorrhage. Clinical deterioration may be delayed, so repeated assessment is essential.'],
      ['22', 'Autoregulation and cerebral perfusion', 'After traumatic injury, cerebral autoregulation can fail and blood flow in affected brain areas can change. The brain may rely on higher-than-normal mean arterial pressure to maintain perfusion, making hypotension particularly concerning in a head-injured patient.'],
      ['23', 'Raised intracranial pressure', 'Early detection of increasing intracranial pressure is critical. If pressure inside the skull exceeds average blood pressure, cerebral blood flow can stop. Increasing pressure may force the brain downward into the spinal canal and cause catastrophic injury.'],
      ['24', 'Cushing’s response', 'The deck describes the body’s response to rising intracranial pressure as raised blood pressure with a falling heart rate. Irregular respirations may also occur. This combination is a late and concerning sign requiring urgent escalation.'],
      ['25', 'Prehospital management aims', 'Prehospital care aims to maintain adequate brain perfusion. Prevent hypoxia, treat hypotension, maintain ETCO2 within the appropriate normocapnic range and transport promptly for definitive care. Reassess airway, breathing, circulation and neurological status repeatedly.'],
      ['26', 'Senior clinical support', 'Serious head injuries require early specialist input. A paramedic may assist with advanced airway management. An advanced paramedic practitioner may provide additional medication, including sedation when clinically required for a combative or agitated patient. BASICS or HEMS may provide anaesthesia, airway protection, ventilation and other specialist interventions.'],
      ['27', 'Open and depressed skull fractures', 'Do not apply pressure to an open or depressed skull fracture. Avoid actions that could worsen the injury, protect the area as appropriate within current guidance and request senior or specialist support early.'],
      ['28', 'Nose and ear bleeding or CSF', 'Do not attempt to stop blood or cerebrospinal-fluid flow from the nose or ears. Do not remove penetrating objects. Avoid prolonged scene time and continue to protect the cervical spine while arranging definitive care.'],
      ['29', 'Vital signs and associated injuries', 'An isolated head injury does not cause hypotension or tachycardia according to the deck. Signs of shock in a head-injured patient should prompt consideration of other injuries or additional causes. Continue a complete trauma assessment rather than attributing all abnormalities to the head injury.'],
      ['30', 'Anticoagulation and key points', 'The deck states that adults and children taking warfarin after a head injury should be conveyed to an emergency department for CT assessment within the stated NICE pathway, even without other CT indications. Overall, assess AVPU, GCS and pupils repeatedly, prevent hypoxia, treat hypotension, protect the cervical spine, request senior input early and transport promptly.']
    ]
  },
  'sepsis': {
    source: 'AAP Sepsis v2 Sep24.pdf',
    intro: 'Sepsis is a life-threatening clinical syndrome in which infection triggers a dysregulated immune and coagulation response. Inflammation, leaky blood vessels, abnormal clotting, reduced perfusion and organ dysfunction can develop quickly. Suspected sepsis is not a specific diagnosis, so use the primary survey, history, examination, observations, screening tools and clinical judgement to recognise risk, keep on-scene time short and escalate early.',
    sections: [
      ['01', 'What is sepsis?', 'Sepsis is life-threatening organ dysfunction caused by a dysregulated host response to infection. The deck describes suspected sepsis as a syndrome rather than a specific illness. The infection source may be obvious or may not be identified during the initial assessment.'],
      ['02', 'Sepsis and the immune response', 'The immune and coagulation systems are switched on by infection. The response is intended to fight infection, but it can become uncontrolled. Widespread inflammation may then damage the body’s own tissues and organs.'],
      ['03', 'Septic shock', 'Sepsis with shock is life-threatening. The deck characterises it by low blood pressure despite adequate fluid replacement together with organ dysfunction or failure. Treat shock and organ compromise as time critical and escalate without waiting for a definitive diagnosis.'],
      ['04', 'Blood-vessel tone and integrity', 'Inflammation can impair the ability of blood vessels to constrict and dilate appropriately and can damage the vessel lining. Leaky vessels allow fluid to shift into tissues. Swelling, tachycardia, shortness of breath and hypotension can result.'],
      ['05', 'Perfusion and organ failure', 'Low blood pressure and leaky vessels reduce the amount of blood and oxygen reaching organs. Organs may begin to fail because they do not receive the oxygen and nutrients needed to function. Multiple organ failure is often what leads to death in severe sepsis.'],
      ['06', 'Disseminated intravascular coagulation', 'Sepsis-related inflammation can cause abnormal clotting within blood vessels, known as disseminated intravascular coagulation or DIC. Overactive clotting may block blood flow and damage organs. Platelets can then be used up, leaving the patient unable to stop bleeding effectively.'],
      ['07', 'Signs of abnormal clotting', 'DIC may lead to excessive or catastrophic bleeding, including after a minor injury. Examination may reveal petechiae, hypoxia, hypotension, gastrointestinal bleeding or confusion. Early recognition and rapid transfer are critical.'],
      ['08', 'Possible sources of sepsis', 'The deck identifies meningitis or encephalitis, pneumonia or another chest source, urinary tract infection, abdominal pain or distension, cellulitis, septic arthritis, infected wounds and device-related infection. Infection may be suspected even when the source is not obvious.'],
      ['09', 'Risk groups', 'Higher-risk groups include people over 75, people with impaired immunity, those receiving chemotherapy, people who have had surgery within the past six weeks, people with indwelling lines or catheters, and people with a breach of the skin such as burns, blisters or skin infection. IV drug use is also listed.'],
      ['10', 'Medication and sepsis risk', 'The deck highlights DAMN drugs as a risk consideration: diuretics, ACE inhibitors, metformin and NSAIDs. Record the patient’s medicines and relevant medical history, but do not use one medication group alone to diagnose or exclude sepsis.'],
      ['11', 'Pregnancy and recent pregnancy', 'Women who have recently given birth or have recently been pregnant, including after miscarriage or abortion, are identified as a risk group. The deck states that NEWS2 is not to be used with pregnant patients. Use the appropriate pregnancy-specific pathway and senior clinical guidance.'],
      ['12', 'Babies and sepsis risk', 'Babies younger than one year are identified as a risk group. Risk is also increased when a baby was born prematurely or the mother had an infection during pregnancy. The deck notes that this may include a very mild maternal infection.'],
      ['13', 'Neutrophils and infection', 'Neutrophils are white blood cells that play a key role in fighting infection. Some medical treatments suppress white-blood-cell production, reducing the body’s ability to respond to infection and increasing the risk of rapid deterioration.'],
      ['14', 'Neutropaenic sepsis', 'Neutropaenic sepsis is a potentially life-threatening response to infection, particularly associated with anticancer and other immunosuppressive treatment. The deck defines it as a temperature above 38 degrees Celsius or any symptoms or signs of sepsis in a patient with a lowered neutrophil count.'],
      ['15', 'Why neutropaenic sepsis can progress quickly', 'When neutrophils are reduced, the body has less ability to fight infection. The same cycle of inflammation, abnormal clotting, leaky vessels and organ damage can occur, but progression may be faster because the immune response is weakened. Escalate early when infection is possible.'],
      ['16', 'Adult and paediatric screening', 'The deck provides an adult sepsis screening tool for people aged 16 years and over and a paediatric screening tool for those aged 16 years and under. Use the relevant tool for the patient’s age and follow current local guidance rather than applying the wrong pathway.'],
      ['17', 'NEWS2 and sepsis', 'Adult sepsis screening is used in conjunction with a NEWS2 score. The deck states that NEWS2 does not diagnose sepsis. It is one part of the assessment and must be interpreted alongside appearance, history, examination, observations and suspected infection.'],
      ['18', 'Primary survey', 'Use a structured primary survey. Check for confusion or reduced level of consciousness, cyanosis, increased respiratory rate or difficulty breathing, tachycardia or an irregular pulse, prolonged capillary refill, reduced responsiveness and other signs of poor perfusion. Evaluate whether sepsis could explain the presentation.'],
      ['19', 'Secondary-survey history', 'Use SAMPLE and ask about fever or infection and its duration, relevant past medical history such as cancer or immune problems, social and drug history, SOCRATES where pain is present and possible triggers. Ask about recent surgery, devices, wounds, urinary symptoms and other likely sources.'],
      ['20', 'Secondary-survey examination', 'Auscultate the chest, look for rashes, complete a review of systems and assess the patient’s overall appearance. Examine for a possible infection source while continuing to monitor for airway, breathing, circulation and neurological deterioration.'],
      ['21', 'Important observations', 'Monitor level of consciousness and confusion, pallor, hypoxia or cyanosis, respiratory rate, heart rate and blood pressure. Capillary refill and temperature may contribute to the assessment, but the deck notes that temperature has limited value on its own and blood pressure can be an unreliable indicator of sepsis.'],
      ['22', 'When to suspect sepsis', 'Suspect sepsis in a patient with fever or who appears unwell when NEWS2 is 5 or greater and/or the patient looks unwell with a history of infection. This is a prompt for urgent clinical assessment, not a stand-alone diagnosis.'],
      ['23', 'Time-critical actions', 'NEWS2 does not diagnose sepsis, but a concerning score or clinical appearance should trigger early escalation. Keep on-scene time to a minimum, provide a pre-alert and include the NEWS2 score when sepsis is suspected.'],
      ['24', 'Treatment and senior support', 'Follow current JRCALC and local guidance for oxygen and other interventions. The deck identifies senior-clinician involvement for treatments such as benzylpenicillin, IV paramedic-level interventions and sodium chloride. Do not provide interventions outside scope, and request appropriate support early.'],
      ['25', 'Key learning points', 'Sepsis may arise from any infection and can cause leaky vessels, abnormal clotting, shock and organ failure. Recognise high-risk patients, consider neutropaenic and pregnancy-specific pathways, use the correct screening tools, remember that NEWS2 does not diagnose sepsis, keep on-scene time short, pre-alert and escalate early.']
    ]
  },
  'wounds-and-bleeding': {
    source: 'Wounds and Bleeding AAP August 2026.ppsx',
    intro: 'Wounds and bleeding range from minor skin injury to life-threatening haemorrhage and concealed internal injury. Use scene safety, PPE and a structured DRABCDE approach. Identify catastrophic haemorrhage early, assess for damage beneath the wound, control bleeding with the appropriate intervention, estimate blood loss in context and reassess continuously. Follow JRCALC and local scope of practice, especially for advanced haemorrhage-control products.',
    sections: [
      ['01', 'What is a wound?', 'A wound is a break in the continuity of tissue. A closed wound damages soft tissue beneath intact skin, while an open wound breaks the skin or a mucous membrane. Open wounds expose underlying tissue to infection and may involve blood loss, bone, organs or other structures.'],
      ['02', 'Closed and open wounds', 'A closed wound may hide significant damage even when the skin is intact. An open wound creates a route for contamination and external bleeding. Consider the force, mechanism and location of every wound, and look for associated bony, vascular or organ injury rather than judging severity from the surface appearance alone.'],
      ['03', 'Complications of wounds', 'Complications include external or internal bleeding, damage to bones, organs or blood vessels, infection, sepsis and retained foreign bodies. A wound can have more than one complication. Complete the primary survey first, then examine the wound and the surrounding area in the context of the mechanism.'],
      ['04', 'Types of wounds', 'The deck describes contusions, lacerations, incisions, puncture or penetrating wounds, and gunshot or blast injuries. The type gives clues about the mechanism and possible tissue damage, but it does not replace a full assessment.'],
      ['05', 'Contusion', 'A contusion is usually caused by blunt trauma. Blood vessels beneath the skin are damaged, producing bruising or swelling. Tissue damage and fractures may also be present, so assess the mechanism, pain, movement, circulation and the possibility of deeper injury.'],
      ['06', 'Laceration', 'A laceration usually results from snagging or tearing of tissue. It may extend through to underlying structures. The deck notes that lacerations may bleed less freely than incisions but can have more prolonged healing, so assess depth, contamination, function and associated damage.'],
      ['07', 'Incision', 'An incision is usually made by a sharp-edged object and is often clean. It may bleed freely and can heal quickly when uncomplicated, but the wound may still damage tendons, nerves, vessels or deeper structures. Assess function and distal circulation as well as bleeding.'],
      ['08', 'Puncture and penetrating wounds', 'Puncture wounds are usually caused by a pointed object and can be difficult to assess from the surface. Assume that underlying structures may be damaged. Do not probe the wound and do not remove an impaled object in the prehospital setting.'],
      ['09', 'Gunshot and blast injuries', 'Gunshot and blast injuries may involve bullets, shot or shrapnel, with entry and exit wounds and associated internal injury. They can be complex to manage. Crew safety is paramount, and it is not necessary to determine the type of gun before providing safe clinical care.'],
      ['10', 'Cavitation injuries', 'High-energy projectiles can create a temporary cavity when a pressure wave pushes surrounding tissue away. Some damaged tissue may not return to its normal position, leaving a permanent cavity. The visible wound may therefore underestimate the extent of internal injury.'],
      ['11', 'Amputation', 'An amputation is the loss of a body part caused by an accident or injury. Ensure scene safety, control haemorrhage and complete ABCDEs. Treat the patient for associated trauma and shock, protect the amputated part appropriately and escalate early.'],
      ['12', 'Degloving injury', 'Degloving occurs when layers of skin and soft tissue are torn away by shearing forces. It is highly susceptible to infection and may involve extensive hidden tissue damage. Control haemorrhage, cover exposed tissue with an appropriate dressing, assess circulation and obtain specialist support.'],
      ['13', 'Foreign objects in wounds', 'Small foreign objects may be washed from a wound with water when appropriate. Larger objects that are not easily removed should be left in situ. Apply a non-adherent sterile dressing around the object, secure the object to prevent movement and never probe the wound.'],
      ['14', 'Impaled objects', 'Do not remove an impaled object because removal may worsen bleeding or damage underlying structures. Stabilise it in its existing position with suitable dressings, manage the primary survey and transport or escalate for definitive care. Reassess circulation and the patient’s response to treatment.'],
      ['15', 'External and internal bleeding', 'Bleeding is the loss of blood from a damaged blood vessel and may be external or internal. External bleeding is visible outside the body. Internal bleeding is not seen externally and may be concealed, so the mechanism, observations, examination and patient presentation are essential.'],
      ['16', 'Types of external bleeding', 'Arterial bleeding may spurt or pulsate and is usually bright red. Venous bleeding is often steady and slower, with darker red blood. Capillary bleeding is usually slow and even. These descriptions are helpful but should not delay direct haemorrhage control.'],
      ['17', 'Assessing concealed bleeding', 'Use the “blood on the floor and four more” approach. Assess the chest, abdomen, pelvis and long bones for concealed blood loss. Look at the mechanism, inspect for bruising or external marks, assess chest movement and breath sounds, assess the pelvis carefully, and compare the limbs using circulation and movement checks.'],
      ['18', 'Signs of internal bleeding', 'Internal bleeding can be difficult to diagnose and may present with swelling or a haematoma. Concerning features include altered level of consciousness, pallor, cool skin, shallow rapid breathing, tachycardia and pain. Blood may appear from body openings as haematemesis, haemoptysis, melaena, haematuria or haematochezia.'],
      ['19', 'General bleeding management', 'Use safety and PPE, identify catastrophic haemorrhage early and do not overlook the ABCDEs. Observe respiratory rate and heart rate for signs of hypovolaemia, consider oxygen according to JRCALC and follow the haemorrhage-control algorithm. Reassess continuously and escalate early.'],
      ['20', 'Direct pressure', 'Apply firm direct pressure to an actively bleeding wound using an appropriate dressing. Maintain pressure and reassess whether bleeding is controlled. A simple dressing is not sufficient when the patient is exsanguinating; use the appropriate step on the haemorrhage-control ladder and follow JRCALC.'],
      ['21', 'Trauma pressure dressings', 'Trauma pressure dressings apply direct pressure and compression to control active bleeding. Large-area dressings cover wounds such as amputations, blast injuries and large abdominal or chest wounds. Select the correct dressing for the wound size and location, then secure it without compromising circulation.'],
      ['22', 'Tourniquet principles', 'An arterial tourniquet is applied directly to the skin, as low as possible but above the bleeding site, leaving approximately 5 cm or 2 inches between the tourniquet and wound. Tighten it and turn the windlass strap until bleeding is controlled. A second tourniquet may be used if bleeding continues.'],
      ['23', 'Haemostatic gauze and scope', 'Haemostatic gauze contains an agent that aggregates red blood cells and platelets. The deck states that it is not within AAP scope of practice and may only be used by T/EMTs under direct supervision of a qualified colleague. Always follow current JRCALC and local scope guidance.'],
      ['24', 'Haemorrhage-control ladder', 'JRCALC presents haemorrhage control as a ladder: start at the appropriate level for the bleeding. Options include a simple dressing, direct pressure and elevation, a haemorrhage-control dressing, a tourniquet or authorised haemostatic gauze, and tranexamic acid within paramedic scope. Do not start with a simple dressing if the patient is exsanguinating.'],
      ['25', 'Care after haemorrhage control', 'Continue DRACBDE, beware of distracting injuries, use neutral alignment and splintage where indicated, dress open wounds and assess MSCx4. Give Entonox for pain where authorised, monitor observations for trends and use SBAR to escalate important findings.'],
      ['26', 'Amputated body parts', 'Place an amputated limb or part in a moist dressing, then in a bag and on ice if possible, without direct contact between tissue and ice. Control the patient’s haemorrhage, protect the part, label or keep it with the patient and convey it according to local clinical guidance.'],
      ['27', 'Estimating blood loss', 'Estimate blood loss in context because the same volume represents a different physiological burden in different patients. Thirty millilitres is approximately six teaspoons, 100 ml is similar to an intravenous paracetamol bottle and 250 ml is similar to a standard 250 ml can. Visual estimates are difficult, so use observations and trends as well.'],
      ['28', 'Blood loss and fractures', 'The deck gives approximate blood-loss ranges: rib 125 ml, radius or ulna 250–500 ml, humerus 500–750 ml, tibia 500–1000 ml, femur 1000–2000 ml and pelvis 1000–5000 ml. The deck advises doubling the number for open fractures. Treat these as estimates and assess the patient’s physiology.'],
      ['29', 'Pressure ulcers', 'Pressure ulcers are localised damage to skin and underlying tissue, commonly over bony prominences. Risk factors include unrelieved pressure, shearing, friction, incontinence, immobility, poor hydration or nutrition, reduced sensation, friable skin and critical or end-of-life illness. Common sites include the sacrum, heels, ischial tuberosities, greater trochanters, elbows, occiput and ears.'],
      ['30', 'Pressure-ulcer grading and handover', 'Category 1 is non-blanchable erythema with intact skin. Category 2 is partial-thickness skin loss or a blister. Category 3 is full-thickness loss with visible subcutaneous fat but no exposed bone, tendon or muscle. Category 4 exposes bone, tendon or muscle; an unstageable ulcer has an obscured base. Document the location, do not position the patient on sores, escalate safeguarding concerns for high-grade ulcers and hand over clearly.']
    ]
  },
  'maxillofacial-injuries': {
    source: 'L3 Maxillo Facial Injuries V3 Jan 2026.ppsx',
    intro: 'Maxillofacial injuries affect the face, maxilla, teeth, eyes, nose and ears. Facial trauma can look dramatic because facial tissues have a rich blood supply, but the immediate danger is often a threatened airway, aspiration, associated head or cervical-spine injury, eye damage or concealed haemorrhage. Use a structured <C>ABC approach, protect yourself with appropriate PPE, manage the airway early and arrange specialist help when required.',
    sections: [
      ['01', 'Topic overview', 'Maxillofacial injuries are common after road traffic collisions, assaults, falls and sports injuries. Alcohol is identified in the deck as a common factor, particularly in assault-related injury. Assess the mechanism, the patient presentation and the possibility of associated head, cervical-spine, airway, eye and dental injury.'],
      ['02', 'Immediate priorities', 'The complications highlighted in the lesson include airway compromise, aspiration, haemorrhage and infection. Longer-term problems include scarring, permanent facial deformity and nerve damage affecting facial sensation, movement, smell, taste or vision. Start with life threats before focusing on the appearance of the wounds.'],
      ['03', 'Airway and cervical spine', 'Airway compromise can occur because of blood, teeth, debris, swelling, facial deformity or reduced consciousness. Consider cervical-spine injury, but do not allow spine precautions to delay clearing a non-patent airway. Call for advanced assistance early when airway or breathing problems are present.'],
      ['04', 'Initial <C>ABC assessment', 'Follow the standard <C>ABC approach. Consider catastrophic haemorrhage, then assess the airway and clear visible debris from the mouth when necessary. Assess breathing and oxygenation, then control bleeding and continue reassessment. Open the mouth carefully to look and feel for lacerations, loose teeth, foreign material or deformity.'],
      ['05', 'Positioning and drainage', 'Patients with facial injuries are generally best managed sitting up and leaning forward where this is safe and there is no overriding spinal concern. This position can allow blood and debris to drain from the mouth and reduce venous return. Reassess the airway and breathing continuously, and adapt the position if the patient deteriorates.'],
      ['06', 'Unconscious facial-injury patient', 'Use facial and eye protection, clear debris from the mouth and consider postural drainage or suction. Oropharyngeal airways may not be helpful in facial injuries. A nasopharyngeal airway may be better tolerated but must be used cautiously, especially when facial or skull-base injury is possible. BVM ventilation can be difficult, so request early advanced airway support.'],
      ['07', 'Maxilla and mid-face injury', 'The maxilla is commonly fractured in road traffic collisions and can also be injured by assaults or sports trauma. A maxillary fracture may be associated with other middle-face injuries. Look for deformity, instability, swelling, bleeding, dental injury, airway contamination and changes in vision or sensation.'],
      ['08', 'Orbital blowout injury', 'A blow to the eye can fracture the orbit and cause a blowout injury. The injury may affect the position and movement of the globe and may damage surrounding nerves. Treat any significant orbital injury as potentially associated with wider facial or head trauma.'],
      ['09', 'Signs of orbital blowout', 'The deck lists enophthalmos, where the eyeball retracts into the socket, double vision, loss of upward vision, pupils at different horizontal levels and loss of sensation above the eyebrow or over the cheek. Document visual symptoms and arrange specialist eye assessment.'],
      ['10', 'Dental trauma', 'Teeth may be knocked out during facial trauma and an avulsed tooth may be inhaled by an unconscious patient. A tooth can also become displaced into soft tissue, including the lips. A crown fracture can be extremely painful. Check the mouth carefully for loose, missing or displaced teeth and consider aspiration risk.'],
      ['11', 'Facial soft-tissue injury', 'Facial tissue has an abundant blood supply, so lacerations can look dramatic and often gape because of muscle pull. Significant hypovolaemia from facial bleeding is uncommon except in children, so consider other causes if the patient shows shock. Middle-third facial swelling can develop quickly and may mask underlying fractures.'],
      ['12', 'Managing facial haemorrhage', 'Control external bleeding with direct pressure where appropriate. If a laceration passes completely through the cheek, pressure may be needed on both sides of the wound. Continue to assess the airway because blood can collect in the mouth and be aspirated. Escalate early when bleeding cannot be controlled or the airway is threatened.'],
      ['13', 'Eye-injury categories', 'The deck groups eye injuries into chemical burns, corneal abrasions, blunt trauma and foreign bodies. These may appear minor while still threatening vision. Avoid pressure on an injured eye, explain what you are doing and arrange specialist assessment when the globe, cornea or vision may be affected.'],
      ['14', 'Chemical eye burns', 'Chemical eye injuries vary in severity and can cause severe corneal injury or blindness. Protect yourself with appropriate facial and eye PPE. Irrigate the affected eye with copious clean water for 20 minutes, taking care not to contaminate the unaffected eye. Do not delay irrigation while trying to identify the chemical.'],
      ['15', 'Managing chemical eye injury', 'Flush continuously with copious clean water and avoid transferring contaminated fluid into the other eye. Do not apply direct pressure to the eye. After irrigation, cover both eyes with sterile dressings to reduce eye movement, obtain a sample of the chemical if this can be done safely and arrange specialist assessment.'],
      ['16', 'Corneal abrasion', 'A corneal abrasion is a superficial injury to the surface of the cornea. It can be caused by a foreign body such as a leaf, branch or fingernail. It is not normally a serious injury, but it is often very painful and requires ophthalmic assessment. Avoid rubbing or pressing on the eye.'],
      ['17', 'Blunt eye trauma', 'Blunt trauma may follow a fist strike or impact from a small ball. Assess visual acuity by asking the patient to read text where possible and compare each eye. Apparently unaffected vision does not exclude significant injury, so a thorough examination and appropriate referral are still required.'],
      ['18', 'Loose ocular foreign bodies', 'Small loose foreign bodies such as grit or an eyelash may be rinsed out of the eye. If an object is under the upper eyelid, the deck describes gently pulling the upper lid over the lower lid using the eyelashes. Stop if this causes pain or resistance and do not probe the eye.'],
      ['19', 'Embedded or intra-ocular objects', 'Do not remove an object embedded in the cornea or an intra-ocular foreign body. Do not apply direct pressure to the eye itself. Build up dressings around the object to prevent movement, cover both eyes with sterile dressings and convey the patient for specialist care.'],
      ['20', 'Eye examination', 'Check the orbits and eyelids for bruising, swelling, lacerations and tenderness. Examine the conjunctivae for redness, pus and foreign bodies. Assess pupil size, shape, equality and reaction. Test visual acuity in each eye separately, assess movement in all directions and document every result.'],
      ['21', 'General eye management', 'Ask whether the patient wears contact lenses and ask them to remove the lenses if possible. Cover both eyes to reduce movement of an injured eye caused by movement of the good eye. Explain what is happening, provide reassurance and remember that eye injuries may be associated with maxillofacial, head or cervical-spine injury.'],
      ['22', 'Serious eye complications', 'Complications listed in the deck include lacerations and contusions, an avulsed eyelid, laceration of the eyeball, avulsion of the eye and burns. Treat severe pain, vision change, deformity, penetrating injury or an embedded object as reasons for urgent specialist assessment.'],
      ['23', 'Nasal injury and epistaxis', 'Nasal injuries commonly produce epistaxis. Causes include direct trauma, nose-picking, forceful nose blowing, bleeding disorders, a dry nasal lining and some medicines. In children with nasal fractures, consider the possibility of abuse as part of the wider assessment and safeguarding process.'],
      ['24', 'Initial epistaxis management', 'Sit the patient down and lean them forward. Ask them to squeeze the soft part of the nose for ten minutes and breathe through the mouth. Ask them to spit blood out gently rather than swallowing it. Reassess after ten minutes and reapply pressure for a further ten minutes if bleeding continues.'],
      ['25', 'When epistaxis needs transport', 'The deck advises transport to an appropriate receiving unit when bleeding continues for more than 30 minutes or when the patient is taking anticoagulant medication. Continue to assess the airway, circulation and overall condition rather than treating the nosebleed in isolation.'],
      ['26', 'Ear emergencies', 'Ear emergencies may result from foreign objects, sudden changes in air pressure or loud noise. The ear canal is a tube of solid bone lined with thin, sensitive skin, so an object pressing against it can be very painful. Do not probe the canal or attempt an unsafe removal.'],
      ['27', 'Ear signs and symptoms', 'Possible findings include bleeding or clear fluid that may be cerebrospinal fluid, bruising, redness, dizziness, hearing loss, nausea, vomiting, tinnitus, earache, swelling or a visible object. Clear fluid or blood after significant facial or head trauma should raise concern for associated skull or cervical-spine injury.'],
      ['28', 'Ruptured eardrum', 'For a suspected ruptured eardrum, place sterile cotton gently in the outer ear canal to keep the inside clean and remove the patient to hospital. Do not put liquid into the ear. Consider pain relief within local guidance and avoid further manipulation of the canal.'],
      ['29', 'Cuts and avulsion of the outer ear', 'Apply direct pressure to a cut on the outer ear until bleeding stops. Cover the injury with a sterile dressing shaped to the ear and tape it loosely. A cold compress over the dressing can reduce pain and swelling. If part of the ear is amputated, keep the part, cover it and place it on ice for transport according to local guidance.'],
      ['30', 'Drainage from the ear', 'Cover drainage from inside the ear with a sterile dressing shaped to the ear and tape it loosely. Do not block the drainage or clean and wash the ear canal. The deck advises positioning the patient with the affected ear down so it can drain, considering cervical-spine injury and conveying to hospital.'],
      ['31', 'Key learning points', 'Protect yourself, manage the airway early and remember that blood, teeth, debris and swelling can rapidly threaten breathing. Control facial bleeding, but look for associated head and cervical-spine injury. Protect injured eyes from pressure, irrigate chemical exposure promptly, never remove embedded ocular objects, manage epistaxis by leaning forward and applying pressure, and never probe or block ear drainage.']
    ]
  },
  'post-operative-complications': {
    source: 'Post Operative complications.ppsx',
    intro: 'Post-operative complications may be specific to the operation performed or may present as more general illness. The supplied lesson groups common complications by the time since surgery, usually from the first 1-2 days through days 3-5 and 5 or more days. These time bands help guide questions and differential diagnosis, but they do not replace a full set of observations, clinical assessment or senior review.',
    sections: [
      ['01', 'Topic overview', 'A patient can deteriorate after surgery because of a complication related to the operation, an infection, reduced mobility, a clotting event or another medical problem. The patient may present with a specific local complaint or with general symptoms such as fever, weakness, breathlessness or confusion.'],
      ['02', 'Timing after surgery', 'The deck states that post-operative complications typically occur between 1 and 5 or more days after surgery. It divides common presentations into days 0-2, days 3-5 and day 5 or later. Use the timing as a prompt for assessment rather than as proof of a diagnosis.'],
      ['03', 'Why the operation matters', 'The same symptom can have different causes depending on the operation, the patient\'s co-morbidities and the recovery course. Ask what surgery was performed, when it happened, what the expected recovery was, what treatment or devices remain in place and whether the patient has developed a new or worsening complaint.'],
      ['04', 'Common complaints', 'The deck highlights fever, wound infections and embolic complications as common post-operative problems. A patient may also present with pain, swelling, breathlessness, reduced mobility, urinary symptoms, altered observations or a wound concern. Establish the main complaint and look for additional symptoms.'],
      ['05', 'Days 0-2: early complications', 'Within the first 0-2 days, the deck highlights post-operative fever, infection specific to the surgery and haematoma. These complications can overlap with expected post-operative discomfort, so compare the patient\'s current state with the expected recovery and look for deterioration or abnormal observations.'],
      ['06', 'Post-operative fever', 'Fever after surgery is a clinical finding that needs assessment rather than a diagnosis by itself. Consider the time since surgery, the operation, the wound, respiratory and urinary symptoms, medications, observations and the patient\'s general appearance. A senior clinician should complete the differential diagnosis.'],
      ['07', 'Surgery-specific infection', 'An infection may relate directly to the operation or the body system involved. Ask about increasing pain, swelling, discharge, wound changes and systemic symptoms. The location of the operation should guide the focused assessment, but a normal-looking wound does not exclude a deeper or more general infection.'],
      ['08', 'Haematoma', 'A haematoma is a collection of blood that can develop after surgery. Consider it when there is increasing local swelling, bruising, pain, pressure or a change in observations. The significance depends on the operation, the location, the size, the patient\'s symptoms and whether there is evidence of ongoing bleeding or compromise of nearby structures.'],
      ['09', 'Days 3-5: changing risks', 'The deck identifies pneumonia, sepsis, wound infection, deep vein thrombosis and phlebitis as complications that may present around days 3-5. These conditions can occur outside this window as well. Assess the patient rather than excluding a complication because the timing is atypical.'],
      ['10', 'Pneumonia', 'Pneumonia is listed as a days 3-5 complication. Look for respiratory symptoms and signs such as cough, breathlessness, abnormal respiratory rate, reduced oxygen saturation, chest discomfort, fever or a change in mental state. Consider the operation, mobility, aspiration risk and the patient\'s baseline respiratory function.'],
      ['11', 'Sepsis', 'Sepsis is a serious possible complication after surgery and is listed in the days 3-5 group. Suspect serious infection when the patient appears acutely unwell or has concerning changes in observations, mental state, perfusion, breathing or temperature. Escalate early and communicate the time since surgery, suspected source and clinical findings.'],
      ['12', 'Wound infection', 'Wound infection can occur in the early or later post-operative period. Assess for increasing pain, redness, warmth, swelling, discharge, wound separation or systemic illness. Ask about changes since discharge and any advice already provided by the surgical team. Do not let a wound complaint distract from a primary survey when the patient is unwell.'],
      ['13', 'Deep vein thrombosis', 'Deep vein thrombosis is listed as a complication in both the days 3-5 and 5 or more day groups. Consider it in a patient with new unilateral limb pain, swelling, warmth or change in circumference, particularly after surgery and reduced mobility. Do not rely on a single sign and escalate for appropriate assessment.'],
      ['14', 'Phlebitis', 'Phlebitis is inflammation of a vein and is listed in the days 3-5 group. It may cause local pain, tenderness, redness or a palpable tender vein, particularly around an intravenous access site. Assess the site, the limb and the patient as a whole, and distinguish a local problem from more serious thrombotic or infective complications.'],
      ['15', 'Day 5 or later', 'From day 5 onward, the deck lists wound infection, urinary tract infection, chest infection, deep vein thrombosis and pulmonary embolism. Later presentation does not make a complication less urgent. Ask about the patient\'s recovery, mobility, hydration, devices, medications and any change since earlier review.'],
      ['16', 'Urinary tract infection', 'Urinary tract infection is listed as a day 5 or later complication. Ask about dysuria, frequency, urgency, suprapubic discomfort, flank pain, fever, rigors and changes in mental state where relevant. Recent catheterisation or urinary surgery may be important context. The patient still requires a full set of observations and clinical review.'],
      ['17', 'Chest infection', 'Chest infection is included in the later post-operative complications. Assess cough, sputum, breathlessness, respiratory rate, oxygen saturation, chest discomfort and fever, while considering pneumonia, aspiration and pulmonary embolism in the differential. A post-operative patient may deteriorate quickly, so monitor trends and escalate concern.'],
      ['18', 'Pulmonary embolism', 'Pulmonary embolism is listed as a day 5 or later complication and may follow venous thrombosis. Consider it when a post-operative patient develops sudden or worsening breathlessness, chest pain, tachycardia, syncope, hypoxia or unexplained deterioration. These features require urgent escalation and should not be attributed to normal recovery without assessment.'],
      ['19', 'Embolic complications', 'The deck identifies embolisms and deep vein thrombosis as common complication themes. A clot in a limb and an embolus affecting the lungs may present differently, but both require careful assessment. Ask about mobility, previous clotting problems, anticoagulants, new limb symptoms and sudden respiratory or circulatory change.'],
      ['20', 'Full observations', 'The management slide specifically instructs clinicians to complete a full set of observations. Record the patient\'s current observations, compare them with previous readings where available and look at the trend. Include respiratory rate, oxygen saturation, pulse, blood pressure, temperature, level of consciousness and any locally required measures.'],
      ['21', 'Differential diagnosis', 'A senior clinician should complete a differential diagnosis using the presenting signs, symptoms and observations. The aim is to consider the likely source and severity rather than label every post-operative complaint as a wound infection or fever. Provide a clear account of the operation, timing, symptoms, observations, examination findings and treatment already given.'],
      ['22', 'Specific and general presentations', 'Some complications are specific to the surgery, such as a local wound or organ-related problem. Others are generalised, such as pneumonia, sepsis, thrombosis or pulmonary embolism. A local complaint can coexist with a general complication, so assess the patient beyond the surgical site.'],
      ['23', 'Red flags for deterioration', 'Concerning features include worsening breathlessness, reduced oxygen saturation, chest pain, collapse, confusion, poor perfusion, persistent fever, rapidly increasing swelling, uncontrolled pain, significant bleeding or a generally deteriorating appearance. Escalate immediately when the patient has airway, breathing or circulation compromise.'],
      ['24', 'Choosing the care pathway', 'The senior clinician should identify the appropriate patient care pathway. This may involve the surgical team, an urgent assessment service, specialist review or emergency care depending on the operation, the suspected complication and the patient\'s observations. Do not delay escalation while trying to establish a complete diagnosis in the prehospital setting.'],
      ['25', 'When emergency department transfer is appropriate', 'The deck advises conveying the patient to the nearest emergency department when appropriate. This is particularly important when the patient is acutely unwell, the complication is time-critical, the required specialist pathway is unavailable or the clinical picture cannot be safely managed in the current setting.'],
      ['26', 'Handover information', 'A useful handover includes the operation and date, the patient\'s baseline and recovery, the new complaint, onset and progression, relevant wound or system findings, full observations and trends, treatments given, response, medications such as anticoagulants and the clinical concern. State clearly what pathway or specialist advice is required.'],
      ['27', 'Reassessment', 'Post-operative patients can change quickly and complications can overlap. Repeat observations, reassess the primary survey and review the working differential when the patient deteriorates or fails to improve. A normal single observation set does not exclude a developing complication.'],
      ['28', 'Key learning points', 'Use the time since surgery to guide questions, not to exclude diagnoses. Look for early fever, surgery-specific infection and haematoma, then consider pneumonia, sepsis, wound infection, phlebitis and thrombosis. From day 5 onward, remain alert for wound infection, urinary or chest infection, DVT and pulmonary embolism. Complete full observations, involve a senior clinician in the differential diagnosis and choose the appropriate care pathway or emergency department transfer.']
    ]
  },
  'extrication-light-rescue': {
    source: 'Extrication and Light Rescue  AAP v2 nov25.ppsx',
    intro: 'This topic covers light rescue, patient extrication and immobilisation in the emergency and urgent care setting. Rescue is a last resort: never risk your own safety or the patient safety, and do not attempt a rescue when trained specialist support such as HART or the Fire Service is available. Use systematic scene assessment, dynamic risk assessment, clear SBAR communication, continuous clinical assessment and agreed ways of working.',
    sections: [
      ['01', 'What the topic covers', 'Light rescue, extrication and immobilisation are connected activities that may be required when a patient cannot safely reach care without assistance. The aim is to remove the patient from harm while managing clinical risk, manual-handling risk, entrapment, equipment limitations and the hazards of the scene.'],
      ['02', 'Light rescue', 'Light rescue is a rescue activity within the team capability and agreed ways of working. It is not a reason to improvise beyond training or scope of practice. Rescue may be required when a patient can be removed from harm safely, when the patient condition requires it or when remaining in place creates greater risk.'],
      ['03', 'Extrication', 'Extrication is the planned process of freeing and removing a patient from entrapment or a restricted location. The plan should account for the patient, the mechanism, access and egress, hazards, resources, equipment, time-critical features and the agencies needed to make the rescue safe.'],
      ['04', 'Immobilisation', 'Immobilisation is the use of appropriate handling and equipment to limit unwanted movement when injury or the environment creates a risk. The senior clinician decides the appropriate form of management and handling before, during and after the rescue attempt, in line with current guidance and scope of practice.'],
      ['05', 'When rescue may be needed', 'Rescue may be required when the patient must be removed from immediate harm or when the patient condition requires removal. It should not be attempted merely because the patient is present in a difficult location, and it must not be attempted if the rescue is unsafe. The need for rescue and its urgency should be reassessed continuously.'],
      ['06', 'Rescue is a last resort', 'Approach light rescue as a last resort. Never risk your own safety or the patient safety, and never have a go to avoid calling HART or the Fire Service. If specialist support is available, use it. A patient condition remains the clinical team responsibility, but rescue safety remains paramount.'],
      ['07', 'The senior clinician role', 'The senior clinician is responsible for deciding the appropriate management and handling before, during and after the rescue attempt. This includes balancing clinical urgency, patient movement, immobilisation, available resources, specialist-agency involvement and the safety of the rescuers and patient.'],
      ['08', 'SCENE: systematic scene assessment', 'Use SCENE to approach every scene systematically: Safety, Cause or mechanism, Environment, Number of patients and Extra resources. A systematic assessment promotes comprehensive review and reduces the chance of overlooking hazards or clinical red flags.'],
      ['09', 'Safety and PPE', 'Remember scene safety and wear appropriate PPE, including high-visibility clothing, a safety helmet and other equipment required by the hazard. Consider fire, collapse, traffic, water, electricity, hazardous substances, unstable surfaces and the route of access and egress. Liaise with the Incident Control Point.'],
      ['10', 'Dynamic risk assessment', 'Dynamic risk assessment asks: who might be harmed and how; what are you already doing; what further action or resources are needed; who needs to carry out the action; and how urgent the action is. Continue to reassess and re-evaluate because hazards, patient condition and rescue options can change.'],
      ['11', 'Factors affecting extrication speed', 'Consider the number of patients, number of crew, extra resources, equipment and time-critical features. A rescue plan must balance speed with safety. Unnecessary delay can worsen a time-critical patient, but rushing into an unsafe or poorly coordinated rescue can harm patients, staff and members of the public.'],
      ['12', 'SBAR for rescue support', 'Use SBAR when requesting help: Situation, Background, Assessment and Recommendation. Explain why you were called, the brief findings and hazards, the patient age or gender and mechanism, the obstruction or entrapment, AVPU, injuries and observations, then state what assistance is required and how urgent it is.'],
      ['13', 'M/SETHANE and wider communication', 'Use the relevant major-incident or complex-scene communication format, including M/SETHANE where appropriate. Communicate the situation, hazards, access, number of patients, resources required and the current clinical picture. Record the relevant unit call signs and maintain clear communication between agencies.'],
      ['14', 'Working with external agencies', 'Work alongside emergency services and specialist agencies rather than duplicating their role. Fire and Rescue Services provide fire and rescue support and are responsible for scene safety in their operational area. HART can provide specialist personnel, advanced PPE, vehicles, command and control, equipment support, triage and clinical interventions.'],
      ['15', 'USAR and complex environments', 'Urban Search and Rescue teams provide a response to confirmed patients at incidents involving collapsed or unsafe structures. Rescue teams are trained to work at height, in collapsed buildings and in confined spaces. Do not enter these environments without the appropriate specialist capability and control.'],
      ['16', 'Police and public safety support', 'The Metropolitan Police Service may maintain public order, secure scenes, provide entry to premises, provide safety and support, and maintain continuity at crime scenes. Police may also have specific powers in relevant circumstances. Keep communication good, record the unit call sign and preserve scene information appropriately.'],
      ['17', 'Resource management and teamwork', 'Work as a team and use crew resource management. Identify roles, communicate hazards and tasks, anticipate what will be needed, and ensure that the clinical plan and rescue plan remain connected. The team must maintain continuous patient assessment and management while coordinating with rescue partners.'],
      ['18', 'Light-rescue equipment', 'The deck lists orthopaedic stretchers, rescue boards, fire extinguishers, safety helmets, PPE, high-visibility clothing, debris gloves, cervical-spine collars and splints. The Kendrick Extrication Device is identified as HART equipment, while PAX stretchers are associated with CTM, APP and HART. Use equipment only as trained and authorised.'],
      ['19', 'Equipment and planning', 'Select equipment according to the patient, entrapment, environment, access, egress and available team. Check that equipment can reach the patient, support the intended movement and be removed safely. Do not allow unfamiliar or unavailable equipment to create false confidence or delay specialist rescue support.'],
      ['20', 'Entrapment risks', 'Entrapment may involve a patient trapped by a vehicle, structure, debris, confined space, unsafe building or other environmental obstruction. Identify what is physically preventing movement, whether the environment is stable, whether the patient has time-critical injuries and which agency has the capability to release them.'],
      ['21', 'Patient self-extrication', 'Self-extrication may be appropriate when the patient is able to move safely, understands the instructions and the environment allows it. Consider the mechanism, suspected injuries, pain, consciousness, mobility, hazards and the route out. The senior clinician should decide whether self-extrication is appropriate within the agreed way of working.'],
      ['22', 'Clinical assessment during rescue', 'The patient condition remains your responsibility throughout the rescue. Monitor AVPU, airway, breathing, circulation, observations, injuries and pain. Correct immediate life threats as they are identified, communicate deterioration promptly and keep clinical care continuous during movement.'],
      ['23', 'Movement and immobilisation planning', 'Plan how the patient will be moved, what position is appropriate, which body areas require support and what equipment is needed. Consider suspected spinal injury and other mechanism-related injury, but balance movement precautions with the need to remove the patient from harm and reach definitive care.'],
      ['24', 'Crash helmets: why removal matters', 'The deck identifies crash-helmet removal as a key learning area. Removal may be needed to assess and manage the airway or examine the head, but it can also create movement, worsen a spinal injury or make airway management more difficult. Follow current local guidance and trained procedures, with the senior clinician directing the decision.'],
      ['25', 'Potential helmet-removal complications', 'Potential complications include unwanted movement of the head and neck, aggravation of cervical-spine injury, worsening airway compromise, pain and loss of support. Prepare the team, communicate clearly, use appropriate equipment and continue clinical assessment before, during and after removal.'],
      ['26', 'Time-critical features', 'Time-critical features may include airway or breathing compromise, major haemorrhage, shock, reduced consciousness, entrapment in a dangerous environment, fire or structural collapse. These findings should trigger early escalation, prioritised rescue planning, continuous treatment and a clear recommendation in the SBAR.'],
      ['27', 'Legislation and agreed ways of working', 'The deck references the Health and Safety at Work etc. Act 1974, the Health and Social Care Act 2008 Regulations, JRCALC, LAS Scope of Practice, OPO40, HS001 Health and Safety, HS005 Manual Handling and HS007 PPE policy. Apply the current versions of local policy and clinical guidance in practice.'],
      ['28', 'After the rescue', 'Reassess the patient after removal and repeat the primary survey as needed. Check for injuries that were hidden by the environment or equipment, review observations and pain, confirm immobilisation or movement plans, document the rescue and hand over the mechanism, hazards, patient findings, interventions and outstanding concerns.'],
      ['29', 'When rescue cannot safely proceed', 'If the scene is unsafe or specialist rescue is needed, withdraw or maintain a safe position, communicate the risk and request the appropriate agency. Never substitute personal bravery for training, equipment, command and control. The safest action may be to wait for HART, Fire and Rescue or another specialist partner.'],
      ['30', 'Key learning points', 'Safety first. Use SCENE and dynamic risk assessment, communicate with SBAR and M/SETHANE, work with external agencies, use trained equipment and keep assessing the patient. Rescue only when necessary and safe, ask for specialist support early, plan extrication and immobilisation, and maintain clinical care until the patient reaches definitive assessment.']
    ]
  }
};
